x_comment(x_greaterUnit,"An #$OrderingPredicate and a specialization of #$interconvertibleUnits (q.v.). <code>(#$greaterUnit BIGUNIT SMALLUNIT)</code> means that the #$UnitOfMeasures <code>BIGUNIT</code> and <code>SMALLUNIT</code> are interconvertible units such that <code>BIGUNIT</code> is a larger unit that <code>SMALLUNIT</code>.  In other words, <code>BIGUNIT</code> and <code>SMALLUNIT</code> both measure the same sort of #$Quantity[ies], but <code>BIGUNIT</code> measures them in larger increments.  This implies that there is some real number <code>N</code> such that <code>(#$unitMultiplicationFactor SMALLUNIT BIGUNIT N)</code> holds.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$greaterUnit #$Meter #$Inch) holds, and so does (#$unitMultiplicationFactor #$Inch #$Meter 39.3696).\n<p>\nFor a similar predicate that compares the sizes of measurable quantities directly, see #$greaterThan.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a973c).
