x_comment('x_ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-Covering',"A specialization of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries (q.v.).  Each instance TYPE of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-Covering is a `series' of `solid' intervals, which is conventionally named, such that members of TYPE jointly cover the time line in the sense that each time point (relative to the notion of time in the context) is subsumed by some member of TYPE.  Note that members of an instance TYPE of this collection do not have to be temporally disjoint, unlike members of the specialization #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint of this collection.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a98c5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a98c5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a98c5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a98c5,541).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a98c5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a98c5,[]).
x_comment('x_ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint',"A specialization, in fact the intersection, of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-Disjoint and #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-Covering (q.v.v.).  This collection sounds very much like #$CalendarCoveringType, but here are the differences. (I) Not all instances of #$CalendarCoveringType are instances of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint. The judgment on whether an instance INS of the former is an instance of the latter depends on whether it makes sense in ordinary English to say `the next INS'. For example, `the next year', `the next month', `the next week', `the next day' (tomorrow) etc. make perfect sense in English, perhaps `the next hour' or even `the next minutes' are still okay. It is hard to say whether `the next second' is a member of this collection, but `the next Planck second' or `the next 10^-24 second' should not belong to this collection. (II) Certain interval types not included in #$CalendarCoveringType may belong to #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint. For example, if we want to talk about `stages' in an long event or a scenerio, or `stages' in a plan, we can have these types of intervals included in #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint. (III) Each instance of #$CalendarCoveringType must contain intervals that are enough to literally cover the entire time line, while an instance of #$ConventionallyClassifiedTimeIntervalSeries-CoveringAndDisjoint does not have to have that property. For example, when we talk about `stages' in a scenerio, we don't have to consider the time after the scenerio, and therefore the `stages' do not have be extended to cover the end of time.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a98c6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a98c6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a98c6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a98c6,542).
