x_comment(x_interArgResultIsaReln,"A quintary #$MetaRelation predicate for stating that a certain kind of complex, partly #$isa based relationship holds between a given argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned. (#$interArgResultIsaReln FUNC N RELATION RELATION-NAT-TERM-ARG RELATION-COLLECTION-ARG) means that, if there is a sentence asserted in the KB relating NAT-TERM and COLLECTION via RELATION and NAT-TERM is the RELATION-NAT-TERM-ARGth argument in the sentence and COLLECTION is the RELATION-COLLECTION-ARGth argument, then when NAT-TERM appears as the Nth argument to FUNC, then (regardless of what the other arguments to FUNC might be) the value (if any) of that function for those arguments is an instance of COLLECTION.  That is: if (RELATION ... NAT_TERM ... COLLECTION ...) or (RELATION ... COLLECTION ... NAT_TERM  ...) is an asserted sentence, and (FUNC ARG(1) ... ARG(N-1) NAT_TERM ...) = VALUE, then VALUE is an instance of COLLECTION. Obviously, COLLECTION must be a collection; so RELATION's RELATION-COLLECTION-ARGth argument-place is likely to be isa constrained (see #$argIsa) to #$Collection or some specialization thereof.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9971).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9971,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9971,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9971,776).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a9971,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a9971,[]).
x_comment(x_interArgResultGenlReln,"A quintary #$MetaRelation predicate for stating that a certain  kind of complex, partly #$genls based relationship holds between a given  argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned.  (#$interArgResultGenlReln FUNC N RELATION RELATION-NAT-TERM-ARG RELATION-COLLECTION-ARG) means that, if there is a sentence asserted in the KB relating NAT-TERM and COLLECTION via RELATION and NAT-TERM is the RELATION-NAT-TERM-ARGth argument in the sentence and COLLECTION is the RELATION-COLLECTION-ARGth argument, then when NAT-TERM appears as the Nth argument to FUNC, then (regardless of what the other arguments to FUNC might be) the value (if any) of that function for those arguments is a subcollection of COLLECTION. That is: if (RELATION ... NAT_TERM ... COLLECTION ...) or  (RELATION ... COLLECTION ... NAT_TERM  ...) is an asserted sentence, and (FUNC ARG(1) ... ARG(N-1) NAT_TERM ...) = VALUE, then VALUE is a subcollection of COLLECTION. Obviously, COLLECTION must be a collection; so RELATION's RELATION-COLLECTION-ARGth argument-place is likely to be isa  constrained (see #$argIsa) to #$Collection or some specialization thereof.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9972).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9972,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9972,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9972,777).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a9972,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a9972,[]).
x_comment(x_followingValueOnScale,"A specialization of #$followingOrEqualValueOnScale (q.v.) that is used to compare the positions or \"ranks\" of two quantities/attributes/properties that are on the same scale.  More precisely, <code>(#$followingValueOnScale VALUE1 VALUE2 SCALE)</code> means that <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> are both instances of the #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder <code>SCALE</code>, and that <code>VALUE2</code> is \"greater than\" (i.e. comes later than) <code>VALUE1</code> in the ordering scheme conventionally associated with <code>SCALE</code>.\n<p>\nTypically, <code>VALUE1</code> and <code>VALUE2</code> will either (i) both be instances of #$Quantity (q.v.) or (ii) both be instances of #$FirstOrderCollection (q.v.) such that they correspond to properties of individuals.  In case (i), <code>SCALE</code> will be a #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType, and <code>(#$followingValue VALUE1 VALUE2)</code> will hold.\n<p>\nFor example, both of these hold:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$followingValueOnScale #$MildlyToxic #$Toxic #$Toxicity)\n</pre>\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$followingValueOnScale #$Corporal-Rank #$Sergeant-Rank #$USArmyRank) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nSee also the specialization of #$followingValueOnScale, #$succeedingValueOnScale.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9973).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9973,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9973,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9973,778).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a9973,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a9973,[]).
x_comment(x_EventLikeOrdering,"The collection of instances of #$PartialOrdering-Strict whose ordering relations order their base sets in the manner in which events are ordered in linear time.  A #$PartialOrdering-Strict <code>ORDER</code>, with #$orderingRelation <code>R</code>, is an instance of #$EventLikeOrdering just in case for any <code>W</code>, <code>X</code>, <code>Y</code>, and <code>Z</code> in the #$baseSet <code>S</code> of <code>ORDER</code>, <code>(R(W X) & R(Y Z))</code> implies <code>(R(W Z) v R(Y X))</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, consider the set of time intervals and the relation \"entirely later than\".  For any two pairs of times in which one time is entirely later than the other, the later of one of the pairs is entirely later than the earlier of the other pair.  So the order whose #$baseSet is the set of all #$TimeIntervals, and whose ordering relation is #$startsAfterEndingOf, is an #$EventLikeOrdering.\n<p>\nSee also #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9974).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9974,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9974,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9974,793).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a9974,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a9974,[]).
