x_comment(x_typesDifferInValue,"(#$typesDifferInValue COLL1 COLL2 COLL-TYPE) holds just in case instances\nof COLL1 will differ from instances of COLL2 with respect to COLL-TYPE.  For instance,\n(#$typesDifferInValue #$Emerald-Gem #$Ruby-Gem #$Color) holds because instances of\n#$Emerald-Gem are a different color than instances of #$Ruby-Gem.  So, if Ruby-1 and Emerald-1\nare instances of #$Ruby-Gem and #$Emerald-Gem, respectively, then (#$differentInValue \nRuby-1 Emerald-1 #$Color) holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a99ab).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a99ab,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a99ab,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a99ab,894).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a99ab,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a99ab,[]).
x_comment(x_typesResembleInValue,"This is a type level version of the predicate #$resemblesInValue. (#$typesResembleInValue TYPE1 TYPE2 COLL-TYPE) means that where INST1 and INST2 are any instances drawn, respectively, from TYPE1 and TYPE2, it will be the case that (#$resemblesInValue INST1 INST2 COLL-TYPE).") :- x_cid(a65b16517a99ac).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a99ac,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a99ac,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a99ac,900).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a99ac,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a99ac,[]).
x_comment(x_resemblesInValue,"(#$resemblesInValue THING1 THING2 COLL) means that the two things THING1 and THING2 resemble each other in their values of the collection type COLL. Here 'resembles' means has a \"close\" or similar value in that property -- what this means, and what the standard of closeness or similarity is, depends on the context.  At the very least, THING1 and THING2 will both be instances of some instance of COLL.  If COLL is an instance of #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder, then it is likely the resemblance consists in each being an instance of some instance of COLL and the collections they belong to are \"close\" to each other in the ordering on COLL.  See also #$identicalInValue and #$differentInValue.  For similar predicates that utilize specializations of #$Quantity in the third argument place see #$resemblesInQuantity, #$identicalInQuantity, and #$differentInQuantity.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a99ad).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a99ad,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a99ad,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a99ad,901).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a99ad,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a99ad,[]).
x_comment(x_identicalInValue,"(#$identicalInValue THING1 THING2 TYPE) holds just in case\nTHING1 and THING2 are the same with respect to some instance of TYPE.  In many cases, this means\nthat they are both instances of the same instance of TYPE.  For instance, \n(#$identicalInValue RedBall-01  #$JonsEscort #$Color) holds because both RedBall-01 \nand #$JonsEscort are instances of #$RedColor which is an instance of #$Color.  In other words, it means RedBall-01 and #$JonsEscort are the same color.  Use this predicate to state that two things share a property but can't specify the exact value they share. \n#$identicalInValue and #$differentInValue cannot both be correctly asserted of \nthe same two things. See also #$resemblesInValue.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a99ae).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a99ae,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a99ae,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a99ae,902).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a99ae,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a99ae,[]).
x_comment(x_differentInValue,"(#$differentInValue THING1 THING2 COLL) holds just in case THING1\nand THING2 are different with respect to COLL.  For instance, \n(#$differentInValue ARedThing ABlueThing #$Color) holds because ARedThing and\nABlueThing differ with respect to color.  That is, there is no one color that they both can\nbe said to have.  Statements like these hold, in most cases, \nbecause there is no instance of COLL such that THING1 and THING2 are both instances\nof it, yet they are each an instance of some instance of COLL.  #$identicalInValue\nand #$differentInValue cannot both be correctly asserted of the same two things. \nSee also #$resemblesInValue.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a99af).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a99af,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a99af,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a99af,909).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a99af,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a99af,[]).
x_comment(x_differentInRelation,"This predicate compares two things in terms of some #$BinaryPredicate that relates them to different instances of a given collection (provided by the argument constraints on the predicate).  (#$differentInRelation THING1 THING2 BIN-PRED) holds just in case there exists REL-1 and REL-2 such that (BIN-PRED THING1 REL-1),(BIN-PRED THING2 REL-2), and REL-1 is different than REL-2.  ") :- x_cid(a65b16517a99b0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a99b0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a99b0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a99b0,918).
