x_comment(x_ApplyOperatorFn,"A syntactic function on CycL expressions.  #$ApplyOperatorFn takes as its arguments a #$CycLOperator (q.v.) and one or more other #$CycLTerms, and returns the #$CycLFormula that is the syntactic result of applying that operator to those terms.  (#$ApplyOperatorFn OP TERM1...TERMn) is the CycL formula (OP TERM1...TERMn) constructed by writing OP followed by TERM1...TERMn (in that order) and enclosing the result in a pair of parentheses.  For example, #$ApplyOperatorFn maps the sequence of expressions <`#$loves', `#$Socrates', `#$Plato'> to the sentence `(#$loves #$Socrates #$Plato)'; or to put the point in pure CycL:\n<p>\n(#$equals\n(#$ApplyOperatorFn (#$Quote #$loves) (#$Quote #$Socrates) (#$Quote #$Plato))\n(#$Quote (#$loves #$Socrates #$Plato))) .\n<p>\nSimilarly, (#$ApplyOperatorFn (#$Quote #$MotherFn) (#$Quote #$Plato)) is the non-atomic term (#$Quote (#$MotherFn #$Plato)).\n<p>\n#$ApplyOperatorFn is similar to a concatenation function on sequences of CycL terms (where the first term is an operator).  More precisely, (#$ApplyOperatorFn OP TERM1...TERMn) is the concatenation of the left-parenthesis, OP, TERM1, ..., TERMn, and the right-parenthesis (in that order).\n<p>\nAs the above examples show, #$ApplyOperatorFn in effect enables us to \"distribute\" `#$Quote' (q.v.) over the component terms within a formula (and to do the reverse).  By the same token, it enables us to \"quantify into\" quoted formulae.  Thus, the sentence\n<p>\n(#$thereExists ?TERM\n(#$and\n(#$denotes ?TERM ?THING)\n(#$isa ?THING #$Philosopher)\n(#$isa\n(#$ApplyOperatorFn (#$Quote #$loves) ?TERM (#$Quote #$Plato))\n#$CycLAssertion)))\n<p>\nmeans that there is some sentence of the form\n<p>\n(#$loves TERM #$Plato)\n<p>\nasserted in the Cyc Knowledge Base such that the term TERM used in that sentence denotes a philosopher; i.e. there is some philosopher such that it is asserted in the KB that s/he loves Plato.\n<p>\nBut note that the terms used in an `#$ApplyOperatorFn'-based formula are _not_required_ to be #$CycLQuotedTerms (q.v.): any CycL expression that denotes a CycL term (or operator, in the case of the formula's first argument) can be used in such a formula.\n<p>\nNote also that the formula (OP TERM1...TERMn) returned by #$ApplyOperatorFn, while syntactically well-formed, is not guaranted to be \"semantically well-formed\".  Thus, the #$arity of the #$Relation RELN denoted by OP might not be n; and the denotatum of a given TERMi might not satisfy the argument-type constraints (see #$ArgTypePredicate) on RELN's ith argument.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9a48).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9a48,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9a48,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9a48,1184).
