x_comment(x_facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg,"A #$TernaryPredicate that is used to state that a special relation holds between a #$FacetingCollectionType and a #$Predicate -- that the former is \"based on\" the latter in a specific way.\n<p>\nWhere <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET-COL)</code> and <code>PRED</code> is an n-place predicate, <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg FACET-COL PRED ARG)</code> means that for all <code>X</code> in <code>FACET-COL</code>, there is an ordered n-tuple <<code>Y<sub>1</sub></code>,...,<code>Y<sub>n</sub></code>> such that, by the definition of <code>X</code>, for any specialization <code>Z</code> of <code>COL</code>, the result of replacing <code>Y<sub>ARG</sub></code> with <code>Z</code> in <code>(PRED Y<sub>1</sub>...Y<sub>n</sub>)</code> holds iff <code>Z</code> = <code>X</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg #$OrganizationTypeByActivityType #$characteristicActivityTypeOfOrganizationType 1)</code> means that for all <code>ORG-TYPE</code> in #$OrganizationTypeByActivityType there is an ordered pair <code><DUMMY, ACT-TYPE></code> such that, by the definition of <code>ORG-TYPE</code>, for any specialization <code>SPEC</code> of #$Organization, <code>(#$characteristicActivityTypeOfOrganizationType SPEC ACT-TYPE)</code> holds iff <code>SPEC = ORG-TYPE</code>.\n<p>\nNote that it is also true that <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg #$OrganizationTypeByActivityType #$equals 1)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg #$OrganizationTypeByActivityType #$equals 2)</code>.  Indeed, <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg FACET-COL #$equals 1)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnTypePredicateArg FACET-COL #$equals 2)</code> are trivially true for any <code>FACET-COL</code>, since the following is a logical truth:\n<pre>\n(#$forAll ?X\n  (#$thereExists ?Y\n    (#$forAll ?Z\n      (#$equiv\n        (#$equals ?Z ?Y)\n        (#$equals ?Z ?X))))).\n</pre>\nFor precisely this reason, there is no point in asserting that a facet is based on #$equals.\n<p>\nFor the very common special case in which <code>PRED</code> is a #$BinaryPredicate, see #$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPred and #$facetBasedOnTypeBinaryPredInverse.  See also #$facetBasedOnPredicateArg and #$SharedNoteOnFacetBasisPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9b28).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9b28,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9b28,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9b28,1623).
