x_comment(x_satisfiesNeed,"This predicate is used for stating satisfaction conditions with respect to agents' needs. #$satisfiesNeed relates an agent, something the agent needs (in a very broad sense of 'need'), and a sufficent condition under which the agent's need for that thing would be satisfied.  Thus it implies that the agent does have the need, and that it has not (or not yet) been satisfied. For a fuller account of the intended sense of 'need', see the comment on #$needs-Generic.  (#$satisfiesNeed AGENT WHATEVER COND) means that AGENT's need with respect to WHATEVER would be satisfied (all else being equal) if COND were to become true. There are various sentential forms that COND will tend to take, depending on the type of thing WHATEVER is (and this also determines which specialization of needs-Generic is relevant to a given case). (a) If WHATEVER is a #$SomethingExisting, then it is something AGENT needs to obtain or possess (see needs), and COND might have the form (possesses AGENT WHATEVER). (b) If WHATEVER is a _collection_ of #$SomethingExistings, then WHATEVER is such that AGENT needs to obtain some arbitrary instance of it (see #$needs-Type), and (assuming for simplicity that AGENT currently possesses _no_ WHATEVERs) COND might have the form (relationInstanceExists possesses AGENT WHATEVER).  (c) If WHATEVER is a collection of Events, then AGENT needs to be a \"doer\" (see #$doneBy) in some arbitrary (future) instance of that event-type (see #$needsToDo), and (assuming for simplicity that AGENT has never yet done a WHATEVER act) COND might have the form (#$relationInstanceExists doneBy AGENT WHATEVER). (d) If WHATEVER is an #$AttributeValue, then AGENT needs to have that attribute (see #$needsToHaveAttribute), and COND might have the form (#$hasAttributes AGENT WHATEVER). (e) Finally, if WHATEVER is a sentence (i.e. an #$ELSentence-Assertible), then AGENT needs the proposition expressed by COND to be true (see #$needs-Prop), and COND might simply be the sentence WHATEVER itself. For other ways that COND might usefully be formulated (e.g. when the simplifying assumptions used above do not apply) see the asserted rules on #$satisfiesNeed.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9eab).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9eab,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9eab,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9eab,3399).
