x_comment(x_GenericValuePredicate,"A event-like-ordered collection of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlots each instance of which corresponds to a particular instance of #$GenericValueFunction (q.v.).  Each instance <code>PRED</code> of #$GenericValuePredicate relates a given #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType <code>QUANTTYPE</code> to the particular generic instance (see #$GenericQuantity) <code>QUANT</code> of <code>QUANTTYPE</code> that is the \"amount\" that <code>PRED</code> (by definition) represents -- viz. the value that <code>PRED</code>'s corresponding generic-value function returns when given <code>QUANTTYPE</code> as its argument.\n<p>\nFor example, #$highAmountOf is the generic-value predicate that corresponds to the function #$HighAmountFn.  <code>(#$highAmountOf #$Happiness (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness))</code> holds because (#$HighAmountFn #$Happiness) is the generic-quantity of happiness that is <i>a high amount of happiness</i>.  Other generic-value predicates include #$noAmountOf and #$mediumToVeryHighAmountOf.\n<p>\nA more substantive use of #$GenericValuePredicates is as arguments to certain other predicates, whose meanings they thereby serve to modify.  For example, the sentence <code>(#$levelOfCompatibility OLDSYSTEM NEWSYSTEM #$noAmountOf)</code> means that <code>OLDSYSTEM</code> and <code>NEWSYSTEM</code> are not at all compatible.\n<p>\nNote that this latter use of generic-value predicates allows us to avoid having either (a) to reify an entire array of (e.g.) compatibility-level predicates such as \"<code>noLevelOfCompatibility</code>\", \"<code>lowLevelOfCompatibility</code>\", and so on, or (b) to reify special-case quantity-types like \"<code>LevelOfCompatibility</code>\" (which would need to have a generic-value function applied to it and then be used as an argument to a predicate like #$levelOfCompatibility anyway).\n<p>\nSee also #$RelativeGenericValueFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9ebb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9ebb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9ebb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9ebb,3419).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517a9ebb,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517a9ebb,[]).
x_comment(x_MapFunctionWithArgsOverLists,"This function applies an #$EvaluatableFunction to a (partially) varying set of arguments.  (#$MapFunctionWithArgsOverLists FUNC CONST-INDICES CONST-ARGS VAR-INDICES VAR-ARG-LISTS) is interpreted as follows.  FUNC is an #$EvaluatableFunction which will be mapped over arguments constructed from CONST-ARGS and VAR-ARG-LISTS.  CONST-INDICES is a #$List of non-negative integers which are the argument indices of the corresponding elements of CONST-ARGS.  CONST-ARGS is a #$List of arguments for FUNC which will remain constant.  VAR-INDICES is a #$List of non-negative integers which are the indices of the corresponding elements of VAR-ARG-LISTS.  NB: VAR-INDICES must be disjoint with CONST-INDICES.  VAR-ARG-LISTS is a #$List each element of which is a #$List of arguments over which FUNC is mapped.  NB: all elements of VAR-ARG-LISTS must be of the same length.  So, for example, (#$evaluate ?VAR (#$MapFunctionWithArgsOverLists #$LogarithmFn (#$TheList 1) (#$TheList 2) (#$TheList 0) (#$TheList (#$TheList 2 4 8 16 32)))) will evaluate the base 2 logarithms of 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32, returning (#$TheList 1.0d0 2.0d0 3.0d0 4.0d0 5.0d0).  Also, (#$evaluate ?VAR (#$MapFunctionWithArgsOverLists #$LogarithmFn #$TheEmptyList #$TheEmptyList (#$TheList 0 1) (#$TheList (#$TheList 2 4 8 16 32) (#$TheList 2 4 8 16 32))) will evaluate the base 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32 logarithms of 2, 4, 8, 16, and 32, respectively, returning (#$TheList 1.0d0 1.0d0 1.0d0 1.0d0 1.0d0).  See also #$MapFunctionOverList.") :- x_cid(a65b16517a9ebc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517a9ebc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517a9ebc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517a9ebc,3428).
