x_comment(x_termHasPositionInFormula,"A ternary #$MetaLanguagePredicate that is used to state the precise position of a given #$CycLTerm within a given #$CycLFormula in which it occurs.  The term might be an immediate constituent of the formula, or it might be nested (to any depth) inside some other constituent(s).\n<p>\n(#$termHasPositionInFormula TERM INTEGER-LIST FORMULA) means that TERM occurs in  the location within FORMULA that is described by INTEGER-LIST, according  to the following convention.  The first integer in INTEGER-LIST is the  number of an argument-place in FORMULA that is occupied by either (a) TERM itself or (b) by some sub-formula SUBFORMULA that contains TERM. In case (a), TERM's exact location has been identified, and INTEGER-LIST  will contain no further integers.  In case (b), the second integer in INTEGER-LIST is the number of the argument-place in SUBFORMULA that is  occupied by either (a) TERM itself or (b) by some sub-formula  SUBSUBFORMULA that contains TERM.  And so on.\n<p>\nFor example, the sentence '(#$termHasPositionInFormula #$Dog (#$TheList 1)  (#$genls #$Dog #$Mammal))' holds, as does '(#$termHasPositionInFormula #$Dog  (#$TheList 2 1) (#$isa #$Muffet (#$AdultFn #$Dog))'.\n<p>\nNote that #$termHasPositionInFormula's first and third argument-places are both \"quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument).  See also #$argN.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa083).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa083,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa083,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa083,4347).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa083,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa083,[]).
x_comment(x_TermWithPositionInFormulaFn,"A binary function that takes as arguments a #$CycLFormula and an integer-list that specifies a position within it, and returns the #$CycLTerm (if any) that appears in that position in that formula. The term might be an immediate constituent of the formula, or it might be nested (to any depth) inside some other constituent(s).\n<p>\n(#$TermWithPositionInFormulaFn INTEGER-LIST FORMULA) is the term TERM (if there is one) that occurs in the location in FORMULA that is precisely described by INTEGER-LIST, according to the following convention. The first integer in INTEGER-LIST is the number of an argument-place in FORMULA that is occupied by either (a) TERM itself or (b) by some sub-formula SUBFORMULA that contains TERM. In case (a), TERM's exact location has been identified, and INTEGER-LIST will contain no furtherintegers.  In case (b), the second integer in INTEGER-LIST is the number of the argument-place in SUBFORMULA that is occupied by either (a) TERM itself or (b) by some sub-formula SUBSUBFORMULA that contains TERM.  And so on.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$TermWithPositionInFormulaFn (#$TheList 2 1) (#$isa #$Muffet (#$AdultFn #$Dog))) is the CycL term '#$Dog' (as opposed to the collection of dogs).\n<p>\nThis function corresponds to the predicate #$termHasPositionInFormula.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa084).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa084,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
