x_comment('x_ensures-SitProp',"A binary predicate that is used to state that a certain situation's obtaining ensures the truth or inevitabilty of a certain proposition.  (#$ensures-SitProp SIT SENT) means that the (presupposed) fact that SIT obtains or occurs guarantees that SENT is or will become true.  This implies that SENT expresses a contingent truth: although SENT is or will be true, it might not have been true (and perhaps but for SIT would never have been true).  Note that SIT must play a material and decisive role in SENT's being or becoming true; it is not sufficient (or necessary) that SIT be a situation in which some agent verifies or becomes convinced of the truth or inevitability of SENT.  For example, if the Tigers win a game that clinches them a spot in the playoffs, then that win ensures that the sentence 'The Tigers are in the playoffs' will (come playoff time) be true.  See also #$ensures-AgentProp.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa0ae).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa0ae,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa0ae,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa0ae,4441).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa0ae,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa0ae,[]).
