x_comment(x_interArgResultGenlPred,"A quaternary #$MetaRelation predicate for stating that a certain kind of complex, partly #$genlPreds based relationship holds between a given argument to a given function and the corresponding value that is returned. (#$interArgResultGenlPred FUNC N INDEPENDENT-ARG-PRED DEPENDENT-ARG-PRED) means that, when SPEC is a specialization of INDEPENDENT-ARG-PRED such that (#$genlPreds SPEC-PRED INDEPENDENT-ARG-PRED) holds,  if SPEC-PRED  appears as the Nth argument to FUNC, then (regardless of what the other arguments to FUNC might be) the value (if any) of that function for those arguments is a predicate that is a specialization of DEPENDENT-ARG-PRED.  That is: if SPEC-PRED is a predicate that is a specialization of INDEPENDENT-ARG-PRED and (FUNC ARG(1) ... ARG(N-1) SPEC-PRED ...) = VALUE, then VALUE is a predicate that is a specialization of DEPENDENT-ARG-PRED.  Note that, unlike (e.g.) the #$InterArgTypePredicates, #$interArgResultGenlPred _cannot_ be used to put semantic well-formedness constraints on functions.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa144).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa144,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa144,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa144,4744).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa144,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa144,[]).
x_comment(x_toTheSideOf,"(#$toTheSideOf OBJ1 OBJ2) says that OBJ1 is located to OBJ2's left or right side.  Thus the arg2 of #$toTheSideOf is restricted to instances of #$LeftAndRightSidedObject. The arg1 can be any instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized.  Because of this asymmetry in argument restrictions, #$toTheSideOf is not sufficient for representing all cases in which one object is to the right or left of another (see #$toTheRightOf and #$toTheLeftOf).  Note that this predicate cannot be used to state that an object is to the side of another, from the perspective of a third party, except as an argument in a #$perceivesThat assertion.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa145).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa145,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa145,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa145,4745).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa145,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa145,[]).
x_comment(x_preservesGenlPredsInArg,"(#$preservesGenlPredsInArg FUNC N) means that when #$genlPreds holds between successive arguments in the predicate-denoting function FUNC's Nth argument-place, #$genlPreds also holds between the corresponding successive values of FUNC.  In other words, (#$preservesGenlPredsInArg FUNCTION N) means that the #$PredicateDenotingFunction FUNCTION has the following property: argument place N of FUNCTION is constrained to instances of #$Predicate, and if PRED1 and PRED2 can both serve as the Nth argument of FUNCTION, and PRED2 is a specialization of PRED1 (so that (#$genlPreds PRED2 PRED1) holds), then (#$genlPreds (FUNCTION ARG_1 ARG_2 ... ARG_N-1 PRED1   ... ARG_M) (FUNCTION ARG_1 ARG_2 .. ARG_N-1 PRED2 ... ARG_M)) also holds, where ARG_1, ARG_2, ..., ARG_M (where M is the #$arity of FUNCTION) are admitted arguments for FUNCTION (see #$admittedArgument). See also the collection #$GenlPredsPreservingFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa146).
