x_comment(x_coGenlPreds,"A #$MetaPredicate and a specialization of #$genlPreds (q.v.) that is used to state that one predicate is both a generalization and a specialization of another. <code>(#$coGenlPreds PRED1 PRED2)</code> means that <code>PRED1</code> is a generalization of  <code>PRED2</code> and <code>PRED2</code> is a generalization of <code>PRED1</code>.  That is, for any argument-sequence <code>ARGS</code>, <code>(PRED1 . ARGS)</code> holds if and only if <code>(PRED2 . ARGS)</code> holds.\n<p>\nFor example:\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$coGenlPreds #$physicalPartTypes\n    (#$Kappa\n      (?SUPER-TYPE ?SUB-TYPE)\n      (#$relationAllExists #$physicalParts ?SUPER-TYPE ?SUB-TYPE))) .\n</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa15b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa15b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa15b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa15b,4779).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa15b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa15b,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapping-Identity',"An assertion of the form (#$roleMapping-Identity SIT-TYPE TYPE SET) is used to represent a role-mapping relations between actors of TYPE within instances of SIT-TYPE.  The relevant set of roles is captured by SET.  SET is a set of lists, each element of which has two elements.  The first element is a sub-collection of #$Situation that SIT-TYPE has as at least a candidate scene (see: #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes) and the second element is a #$BinaryRolePredicate.  Specifically, it asserts that if LIST contains as elements (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE1 ROLE1) and (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE2 ROLE2), given certain other conditions, this entails that whatever plays ROLE1 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE1 is identical to the player of ROLE2 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE2.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa15c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa15c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa15c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa15c,4789).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa15c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa15c,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapping-Equivalence',"An assertion of the form (#$roleMapping-Equivalence SIT-TYPE TYPE SET) is used to represent a role-mapping relations between actors of TYPE in the sub-events of instances of SIT-TYPE.  The relevant set of roles is captured by SET.  SET is a set of lists, each element of which has two elements.  The first element is a sub-collection of #$Situation that SIT-TYPE is either identical to or which SIT-TYPE has as at least a candidate scene (see: #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes) and the second element is a #$BinaryRolePredicate.  Specifically, it asserts that if SET contains as elements (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE1 ROLE1) and (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE2 ROLE2), given certain other conditions, this entails that any instance of TYPE playing ROLE1 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE1 will also play ROLE2 in the instance of SUBSIT-TYPE2. It is worth noting that SUBSIT-TYPE1 and SUBSIT-TYPE2 can be identical, or one could be identical to SIT-TYPE itself.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa15d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa15d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa15d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa15d,4790).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa15d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa15d,[]).
x_comment('x_SitTypePredicate-RoleMappingByPath',"#$SitTypePredicate-RoleMappingByPath is a collection of predicates which are used to represent relations among actors of a given type within instances of a type of complex #$Situation.  Roughly, the instances of this collection point to a rule or rules which will specify the types of inferences that can be made about these associated actors.  For example, (#$roleMapping-Identity SIT-TYPE TYPE SET), where (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE1 ROLE1) and (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE2 ROLE2) are elements of SET, means that in any instance of SIT-TYPE, instances of TYPE playing ROLE1 in the #$subSituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE1 and playing ROLE2 in the #$subSituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE2 are identitical, given certain conditions.  SUBSIT-TYPE1 and SUBSIT-TYPE2 could be identical to each other or one could be identical to SIT-TYPE.  The different predicates represent different relations among the actors. When creating new instances of the collection #$SitTypePredicate-RoleMappingByPath it is important to make sure that it is either a spec pred of an existing predicate or that you create a rule that allows for the inferences to assertions involving the predicate #$roleMappingOverlap. See #$roleMappingOverlap for examples of the type of rules which are needed.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa15e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa15e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa15e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa15e,4791).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa15e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa15e,[]).
x_comment(x_roleMappingOverlap,"An assertion of the form (#$roleMappingOverlap SIT-TYPE TYPE SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE) states that in instances of the complex type of #$Situation SIT-TYPE, there can be more than one instance of TYPE playing ROLE in the #$subSituations of the situation-instance which is itself an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE.  Additionally, and more significantly,  the different instances can go on to play seprate roles in other scenes.  So, the instances are following different role-mappings through SIT-TYPE, but those role-mappings share a common point at ROLE in the #$subSituations that is an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE.  These path crossings are inferentially significant because if one only knows that an actor is playing ROLE in an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE in an instance of of SIT-TYPE, one cannot deductively infer what other roles that actor is playing.  Other information is required.  For example, in a script representing the hijacking of an #$Airplane during flight, there will be many instances of #$Person who play  the role of #$transportees during the first stage of the flight.  However, only some of those #$transportees will go on to play the role of #$hijackerPerpetrator while others will go on to play the role of #$crimeVictim.  Although it is safe to infer that the instances of #$Person playing the role of #$hijackerPerpetrator had played the role of #$transportees, the inference from #$transportees to #$hijackerPerpetrator cannot be deductively inferred because some of the #$transportees play the role #$crimeVictim.  So, we introduce the predicate #$roleMappingOverlap to block incorrect inferences while allowing for the representation of the relevant facts.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa15f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa15f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa15f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa15f,4792).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa15f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa15f,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapping-Branch',"An assertion (#$roleMapping-Branch SIT-TYPE TYPE BRANCH-SET LINK-LIST) is used to represent a role-mapping relation among actors within instances of specs of #$Situation.  Specifically, it is used to represent situations where multiple actors fill a set of common roles, but where those actors are only a subset of the actors who fill some other roles in other scenes.  The argument BRANCH-SET is used to represent the first set of roles.  BRANCH-SET is a set of lists with the following structure: (#$TheSet (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE-1 ROLE-1) ... (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE-N ROLE-N) ...).  Assertions using this predicate behave similarly to #$roleMapping-Equivalence in terms of their arguments SIT-TYPE TYPE and BRANCH-SET.  Thus, in an instance of SIT-TYPE, if an actor of TYPE plays ROLE-1 in a #$subSituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE-1, then that actor will play ROLE-N in a #$subSituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE-N and vice-versa.  The difference comes in the relation to the argument LINK-LIST.   LINK-LIST also represents a role within a scene, and LINK-LIST could be an element of LIST for some assertion of the form (#$roleMapping-Equivalence SIT-TYPE TYPE LIST), where SIT-TYPE and TYPE are the same as above.  Thus, the LINK-LIST could be used to connect the BRANCH-SET to a single role and scene pair, or to an entire  set of roles and scenes that are another actor path for TYPE within SIT-TYPE.  As an example, consider the process of staying overnight at a hotel.  If the guests are all one family, all of the members of the family will arrive, stay over night, and depart.  However, only a subset of the family will perform the actions of checking in and checking out.  In order to capture this structure, you can place the checking in event type on one branch and checking out event type on another branch (presuming that there is no guarantee that the same actor will perform both actions) and have those branches connect up to a path that involves the other activities associated with staying at a hotel.  If there are multiple roles in scenes associated with a branch, then they should all be listed as a sublist which is an element of the argument BRANCH-SET.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa160).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa160,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa160,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa160,4793).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa160,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa160,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapping-Collaboration',"An assertion of the form (#$roleMapping-Collaboration SIT-TYPE TYPE LIST) is used to represent the relationship between actors of TYPE within instances of SIT-TYPE.  The relevant set of actors is captured by SET, a set of lists,  each element of which is a list of two elements.  The first element is a sub-collection of #$Situation that SIT-TYPE has as at least a candidate scene (see: #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes) and the second element is a #$BinaryRolePredicate.  Specifically, it asserts that if SET contains as elements (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE1 ROLE1) and (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE2 ROLE2) then, in any instance of SIT-TYPE, for any two actors that are instances of TYPE, where one is playing ROLE1 in the sub-eventof type  SUBSIT-TYPE1 and the other is  playing ROLE2 in the sub-event of type SUBSIT-TYPE2, those two actors are #$collaborators for the duration of the instance of SIT-TYPE.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa161).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa161,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa161,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa161,4794).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa161,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa161,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapping-Branch-Identity',"An assertion (#$roleMapping-Branch-Identity SIT-TYPE TYPE BRANCH-SET LINK-LIST) is used to represent the inheritance relations among actors within instances of some complex type of #$Situation.  Specifically, it is used to represent situations where a single actor fills a set of common roles, but and then goes on to be one of possibly several actors who fill some other roles in other scenes.  The argument BRANCH-SET is used to represent the first set of roles.  BRANCH-SET is a set of lists with the following structure: (#$TheList (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE-1 ROLE-1) ... (#$TheList SUBSIT-TYPE-N ROLE-N) ...).  Assertions using this predicate behave similarly to #$roleMapping-Identity in terms of their arguments SIT-TYPE TYPE and BRANCH-SET.  Thus, in an instance of SIT-TYPE, if an actor of TYPE plays ROLE-1 in a #$subSituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE-1, then that actor is numerically identical to the actor playing ROLE-N in a #$subSituations of type SUBSIT-TYPE-N.  The difference comes in the relation to the argument LINK-LIST.   LINK-LIST also represents a role within a scene, and it could be that  LINK-LIST will be an element of LIST for some assertion of the form (#$roleMapping-Equivalence SIT-TYPE TYPE LIST), where SIT-TYPE and TYPE are the same as above.  Thus, the LINK-LIST could be used to connect the BRANCH-SET to a single scene-role pair, or to several scene-role pairs that are another actor path for TYPE within SIT-TYPE.  Because the roles on BRANCH-SET only entail the roles on LINK-LIST, there is no identity constraint on the role in LINK-LIST or any othe role-mapping structure of which LINK-LIST is a part.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa162).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa162,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa162,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa162,4795).
