x_comment(x_DogTypeByBreed,"A collection of collections. Each #$DogTypeByBreed is a\ncollection of #$Dogs with a common history of development. Generally, \ninstances of #$DogTypeByBreed are also instances of one of its specializations: #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure, #$DogTypeByBreed-Wild, and #$DogTypeByBreed-Mix. Because of the historical development of #$Dogs with specific traits useful to #$HomoSapiens (task-suitable temperament, behavior, and physical form), classification of #$Dogs according to #$DogTypeByBreed is common even when that ancestry is not\nrestricted to an identifiable  pure breed or wild type.  Mixed-breed dogs,\nfor example, are often classified by the types of dogs they appear to\nhave as close ancestors.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa1b1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa1b1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa1b1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa1b1,4884).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa1b1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa1b1,[]).
x_comment('x_DogTypeByBreed-Wild',"A collection of collections, a specialization of\n#$DogTypeByBreed. Each #$DogTypeByBreed is a collection of\n#$Dogs with a similar genetic heritage and physical type, usually resulting naturally from the interbreeding of wild dogs in a particular location. Unlike instance of \n#$DogTypeByBreed-Pure, instances of #$DogTypeByBreed-Wild are\nnot the direct result of intentional human intervention.  Each\n#$DogTypeByBreed-Wild is a specialized type of dog that occurs in\nthe wild, that is, in at least one freely-reproducing population\nliving apart from direct control by humans. A typical examples is\nthe #$Dingo.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa1b2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa1b2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa1b2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa1b2,4890).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa1b2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa1b2,[]).
x_comment(x_DogTypeByFunctionalGroup,"A collection of collections. Each #$DogTypeByFunctionalGroup\nis a collection of #$Dogs according to the activities for which their\ntype has been specially bred.  Typical instances of\n#$DogTypeByFunctionalGroup include #$Hound and #$Terrier-FunctionalGroup, each of which collects together dogs bred to perform a specialized type of activity\nrelated to #$Hunting. Note that the proper functional grouping of #$Dogs is subject to disagreement, and that related assertions most often belong in a lower mt, one specific to some organization concerned with dog standards.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa1b3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa1b3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa1b3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa1b3,4899).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa1b3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa1b3,[]).
x_comment(x_DogTypeByPhysicalVariety,"A collection of collections. Each #$DogTypeByPhysicalVariety is a collection of #$Dogs according to some shared physical characteristic. Common points of variation include coat type, coat\ncolor, and size.  Many instances of #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure are further\ndivided by #$DogTypeByPhysicalVariety, as for example in (#$MiniatureSizedVarietyFn #$Dachshund).  Note that the classification of a dog type as a Breed or Variety can be subject to some disagreement. Related assertions should therefore be made in a lower microtheory, such as one specific to an organization concerned with dog standards.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa1b4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa1b4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa1b4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa1b4,4904).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa1b4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa1b4,[]).
x_comment('x_DogTypeByBreed-Mix',"A collection of collections, a specialization of\n#$DogTypeByBreed. Each #$DogTypeByBreed-Mix is a collection of #$Dogs\nsharing a particular blend of ancestry.  That ancestry may be\nidentified as involving some specific #$DogTypeByBreed-Pure,\n#$DogTypeByBreed-Wild, #$DogTypeByPhysicalVariety, #$DogTypeByFunctionalGroup, or even one or more instances of #$DogTypeByBreed-Mix. Note also that breed mixes may be specified only by approximation, referring only to the functional type whose traits a #$Dog appears to have inherited, as when we say that a particular #$Dog must have some #$Hound in him. For that reason, a #$DogTypeByBreed-Mix may also be indentified as involving some #$DogTypeByFunctionalGroup.  Examples of\nsuch mix identifications one might commonly see include Lab x Collie \nmix, Dingo mix, and Hound mix, respectively.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa1b5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa1b5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa1b5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa1b5,4907).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa1b5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa1b5,[]).
x_comment(x_DogTypeByBreedAndVariety,"A collection of collections. Each #$DogTypeByBreedAndVariety\nis a collection of #$Dogs according to some #$DogTypeByBreed and some shared,\ndistinguishing physical characteristic (see #$DogTypeByPhysicalVariety). Common points of variation include coat type, coat color, and size. Many instances of\n#$DogTypeByBreed are standardly recognized as having distinct\nvarieties, such as the (#$MiniatureSizedVarietyFn #$Dachshund). Note, however, that the classification of a dog type as a #$DogTypeByBreed, #$DogTypeByPhysicalVariety, or #$DogTypeByBreedAndVariety can be a matter of disagreement.  Related assertions should therefore be made in a lower microtheory, such as one specific to an organization with dog standards as a primary concern.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa1b6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa1b6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
