x_comment(x_SituationalStuffType,"A specialization of #$StuffType (q.v.) whose instances are all and only those collections that are situationally stuff-like. A collection COL is situationally stuff-like just in case every sub-situation (see #$subSituations) of an instance of <code>COL</code> is itself an instance of <code>COL</code>, unless it is a sub-situation of a \"situational granule\" of <code>COL</code> (see #$granuleOfStuff).\n<p>\nMore precisely, for a collection <code>COL</code> to be an instance of #$SituationalStuffType it is both a necessary and sufficient condition that for any <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> (with <code>OBJ2</code> at or above <code>COL</code>'s situational granularity level), if <code>(#$isa OBJ1 COL)</code> and <code>(#$subSituations OBJ1 OBJ2)</code>, then <code>(#$isa OBJ2 COL)</code>.\n<p>\nConsider #$CombustionProcess.  Take an arbitrary instance <code>COMBUST</code> of this collection (say the rusting of a derelict ocean liner); and then take an arbitrary sub-situation <code>SUB-COMBUST</code> of <code>COMBUST</code> (say the rusting of the longest hand-rail on the ocean liner).  <code>SUB-COMBUST</code> is itself an instance of #$CombustionProcess.  So #$CombustionProcess is an instance of #$SituationalStuffType.\n<p>\nSee #$SituationalObjectType for the disjoint notion of being situationally object-like.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa283).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa283,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa283,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa283,5392).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa283,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa283,[]).
x_comment(x_assignedValueOfQuantityForEvaluationType,"<code>(#$assignedValueOfQuantityForEvaluationType NUMBER INTERVAL EVALUATION-TYPE)</code> says that the #$Quantity  <code>QUANT</code> has been assigned the real number <code>NUMBER</code> for purposes of performing evaluations of type <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code>.  That is, instances of <code>EVALUATION-TYPE</code> will invoke a numeric scale <code>NUM-SCALE</code> isomorphic with a non-numeric scale <code>SCALE</code> such that <code>NUMBER</code> and <code>QUANT</code> are points along <code>NUM-SCALE</code> and <code>SCALE</code>, respectively, where <code>NUMBER</code> is mapped to <code>QUANT</code>.  For example, given a request such as, 'on a scale from 1 to 10, rate your satisfaction with your new Honda,' the scale that ranges from 1 to 10 is mapped onto a scale that ranges from a certain level of #$Dissatisfaction to a certain level of #$Satisfaction; #$assignedValueOfQuantityForEvaluationType is the predicate one would use in this case to identify the mapping of numbers to levels of satisfaction.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa284).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa284,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa284,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa284,5399).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa284,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa284,[]).
x_comment(x_incompatibleBeliefSystems,"An instance of #$BinaryPredicate.  (#$incompatibleBeliefSystems BS1 BS2) means that one cannot have both BS1 and BS2 as #$BeliefSystems without contradicting oneself.  That is, anyone having both BS1 and BS2 is committed to (though may not explicitly believe) at least one contradictory proposition.  There is a default rule stating that agents don't have incompatible belief systems.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa285).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa285,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa285,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa285,5400).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa285,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa285,[]).
x_comment(x_properSubEvents,"A specialization of #$properSubSituations and #$subEvents (qq.v.). This predicate relates events to further events which are their proper sub-events.  <code>(#$properSubEvents EVENT SUBEVENT)</code> means that the #$Event <code>SUBEVENT</code> is a part of the #$Event <code>EVENT</code>, a complete description of <code>EVENT</code> would have to involve a description of <code>SUBEVENT</code>, and <code>SUBEVENT</code> is not identical to <code>EVENT</code> itself.  For example, an event in which a car is being parallel-parked might be an event in which a person turning a steering wheel is a proper sub-event.  See also #$properSubEventTypes.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa286).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa286,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa286,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa286,5401).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa286,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa286,[]).
x_comment(x_properSubEventTypes,"A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a specialization of #$properSubSituationTypes and #$subEventTypes (qq.v.) and a type-level correlate of #$properSubEvents (q.v.). This predicate is used to state that events of one given type always have events of another given type as proper subevents. <code>(#$properSubEventTypes EVENTTYPE SUBEVENTTYPE)</code> means that <code>EVENTTYPE</code> (a specialization of #$Event) has the specialization of #$Event <code>SUBEVENTTYPE</code> as a proper sub-event type.  For example, (#$properSubEventTypes #$CatchingWithHands #$ArmMovement) means that each catching event has an arm movement as a proper subevent.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa287).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa287,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
