x_comment(x_EpistemologicalLevelRelation,"A specialization of #$Relation whose instances are predicates and functions whose CycL names appear as the main operator in sentences only at the \"epistemological level\" (EL) and never at the \"heuristc level\" (HL) of the Cyc system.  Each instance <code>ELR</code> of #$EpistemologicalLevelRelation is such that, when the CycL constant that denotes <code>ELR</code> occurs as the main operator in (i.e. in the \"arg0\" position of) a sentence presented for assertion to the Cyc system, the #$CycCanonicalizer \"transforms\" the sentence into another, logically equivalent sentence in which the constant for <code>ELR</code> does not occur; and it is the latter sentence that is actually asserted to the Knowledge Base.  In some cases, <code>ELR</code> will be correlated (see #$elCorrelates) with some non-EL-only relation <code>HLR</code>, and the asserted sentence will contain the CycL constant for <code>HLR</code> (but not necessarily as it's main operator).\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$EpistemologicalLevelRelation include #$ELRelation and #$ExceptionPredicate (qq.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa3bc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa3bc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa3bc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa3bc,5945).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa3bc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa3bc,[]).
x_comment(x_elCorrelates,"A binary #$MetaKnowledgePredicate that relates an #$EpistemologicalLevelRelation (q.v.) predicate to a non-EL-only predicate that is (one of) its \"canonicalization-transform\"(s).  <code>(#$elCorrelates PRED EL-PRED)</code> means that whenever a sentence of the form <code>(EL-PRED . ARGS)</code> is used to make an assertion, the #$CycCanonicalizer \"transforms\" it into a logically equivalent sentence <code>(... (PRED . ARGS-PRIME) ...)</code> in which the CycL term for <code>PRED</code> occurs but that for <code>EL-PRED</code> does not; and the latter sentence is what actually gets stored in the Knowledge Base.\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$elCorrelates #$genls #$specs)</code> holds because a sentence of the form <code>(#$specs GENL SPEC)</code> gets canonicalized into one of the form <code>(#$genls SPEC GENL)</code>.  And <code>(#$elCorrelates #$abnormal #$exceptWhen)</code> holds because a sentence of the form <code>(#$exceptWhen EXCEPTION RULE)</code> (where <code>X1</code>,..., <code>Xn</code> are all the variables that occur free in both <code>EXCEPTION</code> and <code>RULE)</code> gets canonicalized into a sentence of the form\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    EXCEPTION\n    (#$abnormal (#$TheList X1 .... Xn) RULE))</code> .\n</pre>\n<p>\nThese examples illustrate two noteworthy points.  First, the arguments to <code>EL-PRED</code> in the pre-canonicalizer sentence, while they might not be exactly the same, or be in the same order, as those of <code>PRED</code> in the post-canonicalizer sentence, will all typically appear <i>somewhere</i> in the latter sentence.  Second, while the pre- and post-canonicalizer sentences are logically eqivalent, <code>PRED</code> and <code>EL-PRED</code> themselves might be equivalent (modulo a permutation in argument-order) or might not be.\n<p>\nNote that #$elCorrelates is <i>not functional</i> in either argument-place.  E.g. it relates #$abnormal to both of the exception predicates #$exceptWhen and #$exceptFor, and it relates both #$pragmaticallyNormal and #$meetsPragmaticRequirement to the exception predicate #$pragmaticRequirement.\n<p>\nAn important specialization of #$elCorrelates is #$elInverse (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa3bd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa3bd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa3bd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa3bd,5948).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa3bd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa3bd,[]).
x_comment(x_inheritRolePlayersOfType,"This is a type-constrained version of the primitive role\ninheritance predicate,\n#$inheritRole. (#$inheritRolePlayersOfType SIT-TYPE\nSUBSIT-TYPE-FROM SUBSIT-TYPE-TO ROLE-FROM ROLE-TO ACTOR-TYPE) means\nthat where SIT is an instance of SIT-TYPE, any actor of type\nACTOR-TYPE that plays the role ROLE-FROM in an instance of\nSUBSIT-TYPE-FROM that is a #$subSituations of SIT also plays the role\nROLE-TO in any instance of SUBSIT-TYPE-TO that is a #$subSituations of\nSIT.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa3be).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa3be,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
