x_comment(x_TwoDimensionalQuantity,"A specialization of #$MultiDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$TwoDimensionalQuantity is a quantity that is derivable as the product or ratio (see #$TimesFn and #$QuotientFn) of two #$OneDimensionalQuantity(ies) (q.v.).  For example, (#$MilesPerHour 65) is a two-dimensional quantity obtainable by dividing (#$Mile 65) by (#$HoursDuration 1), and (#$SquareMeter 4) is a two-dimensional quantity obtainable by multiplying (#$Meter 2) by itself.  In contrast, (#$Mile 65), (#$HoursDuration 1), and (#$Meter 2) are all instances of #$OneDimensionalQuantity; and (#$CubicMeter 8) is a #$ThreeDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).\n<p>\nEach two-dimensional quantity is in principle equal to the result of applying some (reified or otherwise) #$TwoDimensionalUnitOfMeasure (q.v.) to some #$NumericInterval (or pair of #$NumericIntervals).\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$TwoDimensionalQuantity include #$Area and #$Speed.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa3ef).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa3ef,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa3ef,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
