x_comment(x_ThreeDimensionalQuantity,"A specialization of #$MultiDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ThreeDimensionalQuantity is a quantity that is derivable as the product or ratio of three #$OneDimensionalQuantity(ies) (q.v.).\n<p>\nFor example, (#$MetersPerSecondPerSecond 10) is a three-dimensional quantity obtainable by dividing (#$Meter 10) by (#$SecondsDuration 1), and then dividing <i>that</i> result by (#$SecondsDuration 1).  And (#$CubicFoot 8) is a three-dimensional quantity obtainable by multiplying (#$Foot-UnitOfMeasure 2) by itself twice.\n<p>\nEach three-dimensional quantity is in principle equal to the result of applying some (reified or otherwise) #$ThreeDimensionalUnitOfMeasure (q.v.) to some #$NumericInterval (or pair of #$NumericIntervals).  When a #$TwoDimensionalQuantity (q.v.) is multiplied or divided by (or divides) a #$OneDimensionalQuantity, the result is often (but not always) a three-dimensional quantity.  (An example of an exception to this is (#$ProductFn (#$MilesPerHour 65) (#$HoursDuration 1)), which is equal to the <i>one</i>-dimensional quantity (#$Mile 65).\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$ThreeDimensionalQuantity include #$Volume and #$Acceleration.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa3f7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa3f7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
