x_comment(x_ClarifyingCollectionType,"A specialization of #$Collection. Instances are collections that are usefully invoked for purposes of resolving ambiguity and confusion that arise from the use of non-perspicuous languages.\n Suppose a human or other intelligent agent uses a language, such as a natural language, that is rife with ambiguity, and uses a word WORD of that language whose intended reference is unclear: in the context WORD might refer to THING1 or to THING2, but not both.  One strategy for resolving this ambiguity is to find properties of THING1 and THING2, say P1 and P2, that distinguish them from one another, and ask the speaker whether by \"WORD,\" s/he intended to refer to something with P1, or to something with P2.  For example, the speaker might use the word \"bank,\" and in response one might ask whether a financial institution or a piece of shoreline was meant.  Clearly, what values of P1 and P2 are chosen cannot be arbitrary; asking whether the speaker meant to refer to a multi-individual agent versus a non-agentive place is not as effective as asking the speaker to choose between the \"financial institution\" and \"shoreline\" interpretations described above.  Instances of #$ClarifyingCollectionType just are those collections -- such as the collection of financial institutions -- that might plausibly be invoked to resolve cases of ambiguity of the kind described here.  In particular, suppose TYPE1 is an instance of #$ClarifyingCollectionType and TYPE2 is not, and THING is an instance of both TYPE1 and TYPE2.  The fact that (#$isa TYPE1 #$ClarifyingCollectionType) is a reason to prefer TYPE1 over TYPE2 when calculating possible clarifying properties for THING.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa402).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa402,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa402,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa402,6183).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa402,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa402,[]).
x_comment(x_situationTypeInWhich,"The corresponding predicate for the function #$SituationTypeInWhichFn, #$situationTypeInWhich is used to relate a sub-collection of #$Situation (and an instance of #$SituationTypeByPropositionalContent) to a #$CycLSentence-Assertible.  Specifically, an assertion of the form (#$situationTypeInWhich SIT-TYPE PROP) means that the sub-collection of #$Situation SIT-TYPE is the collection whose instances are all of those situations of which PROP, a #$CycLSentence-Assertible, is constitutively true.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa403).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa403,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
