x_comment(x_CountQuantity,"A specialization of #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.).  Each #$CountQuantity is a quantity that can measure the number of things of a given sort that make up (or are otherwise parts of) some whole or totality.  For example, (#$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit 5) -- the quantity of five moles -- might be the count of the molecules in a given portion of a chemical substance.\n<p>\nIn CycL, count-quantities are typically represented using #$UnitOfCount (q.v.) functions (e.g. #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit), which are themselves often represented using #$UnitOfCountFn (q.v.).  For example, ((#$UnitOfCountFn #$Person) 4) is the count-quantity of four persons: the count (e.g.) of all four-person groups (such as the classic lineup of #$TheBeatles-MusicGroup).\n<p>\nAn important use of count-quantities is as components of those instances of #$RatioQuantity (q.v.) that count things with respect to some other quantity.  For example, (#$VehiclesPerHour 100) is equal to the result of dividing (see #$QuotientFn) the count-quantity ((#$UnitOfCountFn #$TransportationDevice-Vehicle) 100) by the time-quantity (#$HoursDuration 1).  Or to say (in 2004 CE) that the US national debt works out to $20,000 dollars per person is to talk about the ratio-quantity\n<p>\n<pre>\n  (#$QuotientFn\n    ((#$USDollarFn 2004) 20000)\n    ((#$UnitOfCountFn #$UnitedStatesPerson) 1) .\n</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa41d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa41d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa41d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa41d,6233).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa41d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa41d,[]).
