xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa48e,[]).
x_comment(x_sufficientForAvoidance,"An assertion of the form (#$sufficientForAvoidance MOTIVE1 MOTIVE2) states that the #$Motivation MOTIVE1 has been adopted by some #$IntelligentAgent because its specification is perceived to be sufficient to prevent the specification of #$AvoidanceMotivation MOTIVE2 from coming about.  As an example, given an #$AvoidanceMotivation to prevent a  murderer from killing again (M2), an agent's adopting the motivation to execute that killer (M1) would make the assertion (#$sufficientForAvoidance M1 M2) hold.  This predicate is neutral on whether the specification of MOTIVE1 is necessary to bring about the specification of MOTIVE2, see #$necessaryForAvoidance for a predicate with that meaning.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa48f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa48f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa48f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa48f,6495).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa48f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa48f,[]).
x_comment(x_derivativeSpecificationOf,"An assertion of the form (#$derivativeSpecificationOf SPEC1 SPEC2) states that the #$NormativeSpecification SPEC1 has been adopted by some #$IntelligentAgent because of some perceived causal connection to the specification of #$NormativeSpecification SPEC2.  This is a very general relation for representing the creation of a #$NormativeSpecification based on the existence of a previous #$NormativeSpecification.  In particular, it is neutral on whether the specification of SPEC1 is understood to increase or decrease the likelihood of the specification of SPEC2 coming about.  The more specialized predicate #$adoptedToHelpAchieve and #$adoptedToHelpAvoid and their specializations introduce such causal distinctions. Further, this predicate and its specializations should be differentiated from #$subSpecification (q.v.) which relates #$NormativeSpecifications for the purposes of dividing the content of a whole into separate pieces.  So, whereas #$subSpecification would focus on relating #$NormativeSpecifications that collectively amount to the specification of the #$NormativeSpecification of which they are parts, #$derivativeSpecificationOf focuses instead on one the specification of one #$NormativeSpecification 'setting the stage' for being able to fulfill a #$NormativeSpecification.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa490).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa490,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa490,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa490,6496).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa490,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa490,[]).
x_comment(x_necessaryForAchieving,"An assertion of the form (#$necessaryForAchieving MOTIVE1 MOTIVE2) states that the #$Motivation MOTIVE1 has been adopted by some #$IntelligentAgent because its specification is perceived to meet a necessary condition for bringing about the specification of #$AchievementMotivation MOTIVE2.  So, an agent could have the #$AchievementMotivation M2 of winning the lottery, because this requires possessing (in some way) a ticket in that lottery, that agent could form a second motivation to possess a lottery ticket (M1).  In such a situation, the assertion (#$necessaryForAchieving M1 M2) would hold.  This predicate is neutral on whether the specification of MOTIVE1 is sufficient to bring about the specification of MOTIVE2, see #$sufficientToAchieve for a predicate with that meaning.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa491).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa491,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa491,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa491,6497).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa491,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa491,[]).
x_comment(x_normativeSpecificationParts,"An assertion of the form (#$normativeSpecificationParts WHOLE PART) is used to relate an instance of #$NormativeSpecification WHOLE to an instance of #$NormativeSpecification PART where the relationship is a whole to part relationship.  This is a distinct relationship from #$derivativeSpecificationOf, in that PART refers directly to the content of WHOLE, whereas a derivative specification is indirectly related to the second specification.  For example, consider the project of killing two people, where that project is carried out for the purposes of winning an inheritance.  The specification to kill both people is derived from the specification of winning the inheritance.  However, if I have two separate plans for killing the people, each plan is part of the larger specification.  #$normativeSpecificationParts is used when completing one of the part-specifications partially realizes the state of affairs which is the content of the whole-specification. #$derivativeSpecificationOf is used when the content of the derived #$NormativeSpecification sets the stage for realizing the content of the more fundamental #$NormativeSpecification  More formally, #$normativeSpecificationParts is used to relate an logical entailment relation of the content of the part-motive by the content of the whole-specification. The contents of the source-specification and derived-specifications are related by knowledge that realizing the content of the derived-specification has causal implications for the realization of the content of the source-specification.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa492).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa492,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa492,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa492,6498).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa492,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa492,[]).
x_comment(x_performanceOfWorkOccursAt,"This predicate relates a #$ConceptualWork with some #$Place\nwhere a performance of that work occurs. (#$performanceOfWorkOccursAt\nWORK PLACE) is true iff there exists some PERFORMANCE such that\n(#$performanceOfWork PERFORMANCE WORK) and (#$eventOccursAt PERFORMANCE\nPLACE).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa493).
