x_comment(x_comparableScalars,"An instance of both #$ComparisonPredicate and #$EquivalenceRelation (qq.v.), and a generalization of #$numericallyComparable, #$colinearQuantities, and #$quantityIntersects (qq.v.).  #$comparableScalars is the most general comparison predicate for #$ScalarIntervals.  <code> (#$comparableScalars SCALAR1 SCALAR2)</code> means that <code>SCALAR1</code> and <code>SCALAR2</code> can be compared in terms of their relative magnitudes.  Thus they either (i) can be compared numerically, via (e.g.) #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, (ii) they have positions on the same scale (see #$CollectionWithAnEventLikeOrder) that can be compared via (e.g.) #$followingValue, or (iii) they intersect or overlap each other in such a way that their respective minimums and maximums (see #$minQuantValue and #$maxQuantValue) can be compared numerically.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4a0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4a0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
