x_comment(x_derivedAllocations,"An assertion of the form (#$derivedAllocations WHOLE PART) is used to express a kind of whole-part relation between two instances of #$ResourceAllocationSpecification.  Specifically, the instance of #$ResourceAllocationSpecification  WHOLE has the other instance of Allotment PART as a part. So, PART represents a portion of the total amount of resources represented by WHOLE. However, this is not a consitutive part-whole relation. Such an assertion is used to express the fact that the #$ResourceAllocationSpecification  PART is subject to the overall constraint imposed by the #$ResourceAllocationSpecification WHOLE.  An #$ResourceAllocationSpecification represents the reservation of some resources for some purpose.  If that purpose itself involves other sub-projects, those sub-projects will require an additional reservation of resources, most likely with the constraint that they cannot extend beyond the resources reserved for the larger purpose. So, the budget for meals during a vacation is constrained by the total budget for the vacation. This predicate is used to represent that dependency of one #$ResourceAllocationSpecification by another.  An alternative relationship between two instances of #$ResourceAllocationSpecification is #$allocationPortions which represents how one #$ResourceAllocationSpecification constitute a part of an #$ResourceAllocationSpecification.  Let's say that I have two savings accounts the contents of which will go to help me buy a new house. The money in those two accounts bear the relation #$allocationPortions to the whole #$ResourceAllocationSpecification which is dedicated to that task. However, I may set aside particular chunks of the whole #$ResourceAllocationSpecification for certain purposes (i.e., paying bank fees), that relationship between those chunks and the whole #$ResourceAllocationSpecification (since those chunks are themselves #$ResourceAllocationSpecification) would be #$derivedAllocations.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4db).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4db,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4db,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa4db,6635).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa4db,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa4db,[]).
x_comment(x_interconvertibleUnits,"An #$EquivalenceRelation that holds between #$UnitOfMeasures (q.v.) that are interconvertible.  <code>(#$interconvertibleUnits UNIT1 UNIT2)</code> means that any #$Quantity (q.v.) measured by <code>UNIT1</code> can also be measured in <code>UNIT2</code> (and vice versa), and there is some constant multiplication factor <code>CONV</code> for converting any <code>UNIT1</code> measurement into an equivalent <code>UNIT2</code> measurement (see #$unitMultiplicationFactor).  That is, for any number (or other #$NumericInterval, or pair of such intervals) <code>NUM</code>, the following holds:\n<p>\n    <code>(#$equals (#$TimesFn (UNIT1 NUM) CONV) (UNIT2 NUM))</code> .\n<p>\nFor example, #$Meter and #$Inch are interconvertible units since they both measure #$Distances: any measurement given in meters can be converted into a measurement given in inches, and vice versa.  Conversely, neither of those units are interconvertible with #$Kilogram, which is a measure of #$Mass rather than distance.\n<p>\nUnits-of-measure are interconvertible if and only if they are both instances of the same #$InterconvertibleUnitType (q.v.).  Also see #$numericallyComparable.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4dc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4dc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4dc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
