x_comment(x_sitServesHigherSpecificationForAgents,"An assertion of the form (#$sitServesHigherSpecificationForAgents SIT SPEC AGENT) is used to represent the fact that a particular #$Situation SIT indirectly increases the likelihood that the #$Agent-Generic AGENT's #$NormativeSpecification SPEC will be satisfied.  A #$NormativeSpecification describes a type of situation.  This predicate is most useful for associating a situation that bears the relation #$sitServesSpecificationForAgents to another instance of #$NormativeSpecification for AGENT, SPEC2, such that the assertion (#$derivativeSpecificationOf SPEC2 SPEC) holds.  So, let us say that Billy plans to rob a bank in order to buy a car.   The actual event of him robbing a bank would bear the relation #$sitServesHigherSpecificationForAgents to the #$NormativeSpecification of buying a car for Billy because the robbery bears the relation #$sitSatisfiesSpecificationForAgents to the #$NormativeSpecification of robbing a bank.  This predicate implies that AGENT is aware of SIT.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4f5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4f5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4f5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa4f5,6674).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa4f5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa4f5,[]).
x_comment(x_sitServesSpecificationForAgents,"An assertion of the form (#$sitServesSpecificationForAgents SIT SPEC AGENT) is used to relate an instance of #$Situation SIT to an instance of #$NormativeSpecification and an instance of #$Agent-Generic.  Instances of #$NormativeSpecification describe types of situations that some agents want to come about, and agents can have a variety of attitudes towards a #$NormativeSpecification. The meaning of the initial assertion is that SIT helps, in a very generic sense, AGENT with regard to bringing about SPEC. As an example, consider a person who wants to be a millionaire (where we represent that desire as an instance of #$NormativeSpecification), if that person won a hundred thousand dollars in a lottery then that winning event would bear the relation #$sitServesSpecificationForAgents to that #$NormativeSpecification and that person.  AGENT need not have any awareness of SIT.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4f6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4f6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4f6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa4f6,6675).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa4f6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa4f6,[]).
x_comment(x_sitSatisfiesSpecificationForAgents,"An assertion of the form #$sitSatisfiesSpecificationForAgents SIT SPEC AGENT) is used to represent the fact that a particular #$Situation SIT  satisfies the #$NormativeSpecification SPEC for the #$Agent-Generic AGENT.  A #$NormativeSpecification amounts to a reason for an agent to behave in a particular way.  This notion of 'satisfaction' is  generic in the sense that the requirements of #$NormativeSpecification have been met by the #$Situation SIT such that AGENT need no longer consider how to bring about events which would satisfy SPEC  For example, let us say that SPEC has the specification that AGENT goes on vacation to Mexico in May 2004, if SIT is Mexican vacation in May 2004 performed by AGENT, then SIT would bear the relation #$sitSatisfiesSpecificationForAgents to SPEC and AGENT.  This predicate implies that AGENT is aware of SIT. ") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4f7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4f7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4f7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa4f7,6676).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa4f7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa4f7,[]).
x_comment(x_sitPartiallySatisfiesSpecificationForAgents,"An assertion of the form #$sitPartiallySatisfiesSpecificationForAgents SIT SPEC AGENT) is used to represent the fact that a particular #$Situation SIT partially satisfies the #$NormativeSpecification SPEC for the #$Agent-Generic AGENT.  A #$NormativeSpecification describes a type of #$Situation that an agent has some interest in either bringing about, maintaining, or preventing,.  For example, let us say that SPEC has the specification that AGENT's business competitors COMP1 and COMP2 die, then if SIT is the death of COMP1, then SIT would bear the relation #$sitPartiallySatisfiesSpecificationForAgents to SPEC and AGENT.  More generally, this predicate serves as a way of relating an situation to a #$NormativeSpecification when the situation bears the relation #$sitSatisfiesSpecificationForAgents to some other #$NormativeSpecification to which the first #$NormativeSpecification bears the relation #$subSpecification  This predicate implies that AGENT is aware of SIT.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4f8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4f8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4f8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa4f8,6677).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa4f8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa4f8,[]).
x_comment(x_sitConflictsWithSpecificationForAgents,"An assertion of the form (#$sitConflictsWithSpecificationForAgents SIT SPEC AGENT) is used to relate an instance of #$Situation SIT to an instance of #$NormativeSpecification and an instance of #$Agent-Generic.  Instances of #$NormativeSpecification describe some type of situation that some agent either wants to bring about or wants to avoid.  The effect of such an assertion is to state that SIT undermines or otherwise hinders, in a very generic sense, AGENT with regard to satisfying or achieving SPEC.  As an example, consider a person who wants to sell her house (where we represent that as an instance of #$NormativeSpecification). If the house burns down, then that event would bear the relation #$sitConflictsWithSpecificationForAgents to that #$NormativeSpecification and that person.  AGENT need not have any awareness of SIT.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa4f9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa4f9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa4f9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa4f9,6678).
