x_comment(x_intendsByInUtterancePegContext,"(#$intendsByInUtterancePeg SPEAKER PEG INTERPRETATION UTTERANCE-PEG) says that SPEAKER, an instance of #$IntelligentAgent, in the context of the (possibly larger) instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg-CompleteUtterance UTTERANCE-PEG, intended to refer to INTERPRETATION when introducing the instance of #$LinguisticExpressionPeg PEG (a sub-expression of UTTERANCE-PEG -- see #$subExpressions).  So, for example, if PEG is a linguistic expression peg for \"my dog\" and UTTERANCE-PEG is a peg for \"I fed my dog some tuna,\" then (#$intendsByInUtterancePeg SPEAKER PEG Dog001 UTTERANCE-PEG) says that by introducing \"my dog\" in the context of the utterance that introduced \"I fed my dog some tuna,\" \"my dog\" was intended to refer to Dog001.  The purpose of representing UTTERANCE-PEG is to provide a larger context that might help one deduce the intended referent of PEG; contrast the Dog001 interpretation with the empty-set interpretation were UTTERANCE-PEG the peg for, \"She asked me how my dog is, not knowing that I've never owned one.\"") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa500).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa500,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa500,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
