x_comment(x_ScalarExponentFn,"A #$MathematicalFunctionOnScalars that is effectively the arithmetical operation of taking a number to a specified power (see #$ExponentFn), extended to all (measurable) #$ScalarIntervals.  <code>(#$ScalarExponentFn SCALAR EXP)</code> is <code>SCALAR^EXP</code>, i.e. <code>SCALAR</code> to the power <code>EXP</code>.  If <code>SCALAR</code> is a #$RealNumber, <code>SCALAR^EXP</code> is equal to <code>(#$ExponentFn SCALAR EXP)</code>.  If <code>SCALAR</code> is a #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.), then <code>SCALAR^EXP</code> is also a measurable-quantity, and of a different \"dimensionality\" than <code>SCALAR</code> (see e.g. #$MultiDimensionalQuantity).  More specifically, if <code>SCALAR = (UNIT NUM)</code>, for some #$UnitOfMeasure <code>UNIT</code> and #$NumericInterval <code>NUM</code>, and <code>EXP</code> is a #$PositiveInteger, then <code>SCALAR^EXP = ((UNIT^EXP) (#$ExponentFn NUM EXP))</code>, where <code>(UNIT^EXP)</code> is the result of \"multiplying\" (via #$UnitProductFn) <code>UNIT</code> by itself <code>EXP</code>-1 times.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$ScalarExponentFn (#$Meter 5) 2) is equal to\n<p>\n<pre>\n  ((#$UnitProductFn #$Meter #$Meter) (#$ExponentFn 5 2)) ,\n</pre>\n<p>\nwhich is equal to (#$SquareMeter 25).  And (#$ScalarExponentFn (#$Inch 5) 3) is equal to (#$CubicInch 125).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa50d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa50d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa50d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa50d,6703).
