x_comment(x_unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis,"A binary predicate that is used to show how a complex interconvertible-unit-type can be analyzed in terms of more basic unit-types.  #$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis relates a non-primitive #$InterconvertibleUnitType (q.v.) to a set of #$Lists that represents an equivalent \"product\" of #$FundamentalUnitTypes.\n<p>\n<code>(#$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis UNITTYPE PRODUCT-SET)</code> means that any #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.) returned by an instance of <code>UNITTYPE</code> can be converted to (i.e. represented as) a product of quantities measured in units of the (fundamental) types in <code>PRODUCT-SET</code>.  More precisely, each element of <code>PRODUCT-SET</code> is of the form <code>(#$TheList FUND-UNITTYPE EXPONENT)</code>, and so <code>PRODUCT-SET</code> itself is of the form\n<pre>\n  (#$TheSet\n    (#$TheList FUND-UNITTYPE-1 EXPONENT-1)\n    (#$TheList FUND-UNITTYPE-2 EXPONENT-2)\n    . . .\n    (#$TheList FUND-UNITTYPE-n EXPONENT-n)).\n</pre>\n<p>\nHence, <code>(#$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis UNITTYPE PRODUCT-SET)</code> means that <code>UNITTYPE</code> is equivalent to (put schematically)\n<pre>\n  (FUND-UNITTYPE-1 ^ EXPONENT-1) *\n  (FUND-UNITTYPE-2 ^ EXPONENT-2) *\n  . . . *\n  (FUND-UNITTYPE-n ^ EXPONENT-n).\n</pre>\n<p>\nIn other words: for any #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) function <code>UNIT</code> that is an instance of <code>UNITTYPE</code>, <code>(UNIT 1)</code> (i.e. the quantity one-<code>UNIT)</code> is equal to a quantity of the form\n<pre>\n  (#$TimesFn\n    (#$ScalarExponentFn (FUND-UNIT-1 1) EXPONENT-1)\n    (#$ScalarExponentFn (FUND-UNIT-2 1) EXPONENT-2)\n    . . .\n    (#$ScalarExponentFn (FUND-UNIT-n 1) EXPONENT-n))</code> ,\n</pre>\n<p>\nwhere each <code>FUND-UNIT-i</code> is some instance of <code>FUND-UNITTYPE-i</code> (for 1 <= <code>i</code> <= <code>n)</code>.\n<p>\nFor example,\n<pre>\n  (#$unitTypeDimensionalAnalysis\n    #$UnitOfSpeed\n    (#$TheSet\n      (#$TheList #$UnitOfDistance 1)\n      (#$TheList #$UnitOfTime -1)))\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that each #$UnitOfSpeed is obtainable by \"dividing\" a #$UnitOfDistance by a #$UnitOfTime (e.g. #$MilesPerHour = (#$PerFn #$Mile #$HoursDuration)).  Put more simply: speed is distance over time.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa552).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa552,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa552,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa552,6801).
