x_comment(x_StageSubSitRoleSpecFn,"A #$TernaryFunction, #$StageSubSitRoleSpecFn is used to denote an instance of #$RoleSpecification-SubSitType, an abstract individual which is used to pick out a particular role in the subsituations of a type of #$Situation.    An expression of the form (#$StageSubSitRoleSpecFn TEMP-STAGE SUBSIT-TYPE ROLE) designates the #$RoleSpecification-SubSitType that is the representation of the #$BinaryRolePredicate ROLE when played in an instance of the #$Situation type SUBSIT-TYPE when it is a subsituation of some instance of the #$Situation type, SIT-TYPE. This function is distinct from #$SubSitRoleSpecFn because it triangulates on the role by referring to an instance of #$TemporalStageSpecification. ") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5af).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5af,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5af,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa5af,6940).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa5af,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa5af,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapSpecification-Disjoint',"The predicate #$roleMapSpecification-Disjoint is used to represent that, in the instances of a given type of #$Situation and in those instances subsituations, that the actors in particular roles must be numerically different individuals.  An assertion of the form (#$roleMapSpecification-Disjoint SIT-YPE RM-SPEC) connects the #$RoleMappingSpecification to the type of #$Situation to which it applies, SIT-TYPE.  RM-SPEC will be associated with several instances of #$RoleSpecification, each of these will pick out a role either in the instances of SIT-TYPE themselves or in their subsituations.   Specifically, given the assertions (#$subSpecification-Subject RM-SPEC ROLE-SPEC1) and (#$subSpecification-Object RM-SPEC ROLE-SPEC2), then any individual who plays the role picked out by ROLE-SPEC1 in an instance of SIT-TYPE cannot also play the role picked out by ROLE-SPEC2 in the same instance of SIT-TYPE.  It is possible for RM-SPEC to be connected to many instances of #$RoleSpecification.  Although the label 'disjoint' on this predicate indicates a symmetric relationship, this predicate is associated with the predicates #$subSpecification-Subject and #$subSpecification-Object in order to efficiently represent multiple disjoint roles through a single instance of #$RoleMappingSpecification.  Thus, if it is known that one set of roles is disjoint with another set of roles and each set shares actors among its roles, then one set can be picked out with the 'subject' predicate and the other can be picked out with the 'object' predicate without entailing any unwanted disjointness claims.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5b0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5b0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5b0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa5b0,6941).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa5b0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa5b0,[]).
x_comment('x_subSpecification-Symmetric',"The predicate #$subSpecification-Symmetric is used to relate one instance of #$SituationSpecification to another instance of #$SituationSpecification.  Its specific purpose is for establishing a symmetric relationships among the parts of an instance of some sub-collection of #$Situation.  It is the common specialization of the predicates #$subSpecification-Object and #$subSpecification-Subject.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5b1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5b1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5b1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa5b1,6942).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa5b1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa5b1,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapSpecification-Identity',"The predicate #$roleMapSpecification-Identity is used to represent that, in the instances of a given type of #$Situation and in those instances subsituations, exactly one individuals plays some set of roles.  An assertion of the form (#$roleMapSpecification-Identity SIT-YPE RM-SPEC) connects the #$RoleMappingSpecification to the type of #$Situation to which it applies, SIT-TYPE.  RM-SPEC will be associated with several instances of #$RoleSpecification, each of these will pick out a role either in the instances of SIT-TYPE themselves or in their subsituations.   Specifically, given the assertions (#$subSpecification-Symmetric RM-SPEC ROLE-SPEC1) and (#$subSpecification-Symmetric RM-SPEC ROLE-SPEC2), then any individual who plays the role picked out by ROLE-SPEC1 in an instance of SIT-TYPE will also play the role picked out by ROLE-SPEC2 in the same instance of SIT-TYPE and, vice versa.  It is possible for RM-SPEC to be connected to many instances of #$RoleSpecification, any two which are connected by the relation #$subSpecification will have identical actors.  If a situation type has multiple role mappings of this sort, but they cover different roles, one should introduce a #$RoleMappingSpecification for each pattern.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5b2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5b2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5b2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa5b2,6943).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa5b2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa5b2,[]).
x_comment('x_roleMapSpecification-Subset',"The predicate #$roleMapSpecification-Subset is used to represent an implication relation among the roles in the instances of a given type of #$Situation and in those instances subsituations.  An assertion of the form (#$roleMapSpecification-Subset SIT-YPE RM-SPEC) connects the #$RoleMappingSpecification to the type of #$Situation to which it applies, SIT-TYPE.  RM-SPEC will be associated with several instances of #$RoleSpecification, each of these will pick out a role either in the instances of SIT-TYPE themselves or in their subsituations.  The direction of the implication will be based on how RM-SPEC is connected to the instances of #$RoleSpecification.  Specifically, given the assertions (#$subSpecification-Subject RM-SPEC ROLE-SPEC1) and (#$subSpecification-Object RM-SPEC ROLE-SPEC2), then any individual who plays the role picked out by ROLE-SPEC1 in an instance of SIT-TYPE will also play the role picked out by ROLE-SPEC2 in the same instance of SIT-TYPE.  It is possible for RM-SPEC to be connected to many instances of #$RoleSpecification by the predicates #$subSpecification-Object, #$subSpecification-Subject, or their common specialization #$subSpecification-Symmetric.  Regardless of the number, if one #$RoleSpecification is related to RM-SPEC by #$subSpecification-Subject and another #$RoleSpecification is related to RM-SPEC by #$subSpecification-Object, anything playing the first depicted role will also play the second depicted role. In roles have divergent entailments, on should introduce a #$RoleMappingSpecification to represent each entailment pattern.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5b3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5b3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5b3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa5b3,6944).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa5b3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa5b3,[]).
x_comment(x_roleTypeRestriction,"An assertion of the form (#$roleTypeRestriction ROLE-SPEC TYPE) is used to represent a restriction on the type of actor which will fill the role represented by the #$RoleSpecification ROLE-SPEC.   If ROLE-SPEC is a #$RoleSpecification-SubSitType, given the assertions (#$sitTypeHasSpecification SIT-TYPE ROLE-SPEC) , (#$specificationSubSitType ROLE-SPEC SUB-TYPE), (#$specificationRole ROLE-SPEC ROLE), and (#$specificationStage ROLE-SPEC STAGE), then in any instance of SIT-TYPE in any subsituation of type SUB-TYPE that bears the relation  #$situationFallsUnderStage to the #$TemporalStageSpecification STAGE, then only instances of TYPE can play ROLE in that subsituation.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5b4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5b4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5b4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
