x_comment(x_populationOfRegion,"A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot for stating the size of a specified population living in a given #$GeographicalRegion or in the territory of a given #$GeopoliticalEntity.  <code>(#$populationOfRegion GEO COUNT)</code> means that <code>COUNT</code> is a #$CountQuantity (q.v.) that represents the number of beings of the type <code>TYPE</code> (typically, <i>humans</i>) counted by <code>COUNT</code> that are living in (or in the territory of) <code>GEO</code>.  In practice <code>COUNT</code> will normally be given in the form <code>((#$UnitOfCountFn TYPE) NUMBER)</code>.  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$populationOfRegion #$IvoryCoast ((#$UnitOfCountFn #$Person) 15000000)</code>\n</pre>\n<p>\nmeans that 15 million people live in the Ivory Coast.\n<p>\nNote that, when <code>TYPE</code> is #$Person (or #$HomoSapiens), <code>NUMBER</code> is not necessarily the same as the number of <i>citizens</i> of <code>GEO</code>, since citizens may be living abroad, and aliens may be residing in <code>GEO</code>.  Note also that a sentence using #$populationOfRegion should generally be asserted in a #$TemporalMicrotheory (q.v.), as the number of people living in a region often changes over time.\n<p>\nSee #$numberOfInhabitants for a similar predicate that relates a region directly to a number.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa5ba).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa5ba,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa5ba,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa5ba,6951).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa5ba,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa5ba,[]).
