x_comment(x_integralWRTUnit,"A binary predicate that relates a given #$UnitOfMeasure (q.v.) to the #$ScalarPointValues (q.v.) that are \"integral\" with respect to it.  <code>(#$integralWRTUnit SCALAR UNIT)</code> means that there is some #$Integer <code>INT</code> such that <code>(UNIT INT)</code> is equal to <code>SCALAR</code>.  Typically, <code>SCALAR</code> will be a #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.).  When <code>UNIT</code> is #$Unity, however, <code>SCALAR</code> will be an #$Integer, namely <code>INT</code> itself.\n<p>\nThe result of applying a #$UnitOfMeasure to an integer is of course always integral with respect to that unit, and the result of applying a unit-of-measure to an non-integer is always <i>not</i> integral with respect to that unit.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$Meter 5) is integral with respect to both #$Meter and (#$Milli #$Meter) (since 5 meters is equal to ((#$Milli #$Meter) 5000)).  Conversely, (#$Meter 5) is <i>not</i> integral with respect to either #$Mile or #$Kilometer (since 5 meters is equal to (#$Kilometer 0.005)).\n<p>\nSee also #$coIntegralScalars.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa665).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa665,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa665,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa665,7192).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa665,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa665,[]).
