xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa68c,[]).
x_comment(x_skillTypicalForPersonTypeByActivity,"(#$skillTypicalForPersonTypeByActivity PERSON-TYPE SKILLED-ACTIVITY-TYPE) holds just in case SKILLED-ACTIVITY-TYPE is the instance of #$SkilledActivityType that corresponds to the activity that PERSON-TYPE is faceted by.  For instance, #$BaseballPlayer is an instance of #$PersonTypeByActivity and each instance of that collection is an instance because he or she participates to some degree in baseball playing activities.  The generic skill of \"being able to play baseball\" is the skill that corresponds to the activity of playing baseball.  So, (#$skillTypicalForPersonTypeByActivity #$BaseballPlayer BaseballPlayingSkilledActivity) holds.  This is the corresponding predicate (see #$functionCorrespondingPredicate-Canonical) to #$SkillTypicalForTypeFn and often the second argument to this predicate will be functionally denoted.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa68d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa68d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
