x_comment(x_nonAbducibleSentence,"An instance of #$AbductionConstraintConstant (q.v.). <code>(#$nonAbducibleSentence SENT)</code> means that <code>SENT</code> is provable by the Cyc inference engine without using abduction. \n<p>\n#$nonAbducibleSentence is primarily used as a means of indicating that certain literals in a rule should never be abduced.  Generally, it is used in the pragmatic requirements of rules as a wrapper around literals that already occur in the antecedents of these rules.  \n<p>\nFor example, consider the following rule:\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$and\n      (#$isa ?METEOR #$Meteor)\n      (#$primaryObjectMoving ?METEOR ?METEOROID))\n    (#$inputsDestroyed ?METEOR ?METEOROID))\n</pre>\nIn this rule, if one wanted the  '(#$primaryObjectMoving ?METEOR ?METEOROID)' literal to always be proved only through non-abductive means, one could add this pragmatic requirement as a meta-assertion:\n<pre>\n  (#$pragmaticRequirement\n    (#$nonAbducibleSentence (#$primaryObjectMoving ?METEOR ?METEOROID))\n  (#$implies\n    (#$and\n      (#$isa ?METEOR #$Meteor)\n      (#$primaryObjectMoving ?METEOR ?METEOROID))\n    (#$inputsDestroyed ?METEOR ?METEOROID)))\n</pre>\nIn this case, if the inference engine were trying to use this rule to prove (#$inputsDestroyed Event001 Object001) the inference engine would be free to abduce (#$isa Event001 #$Meteor) (assuming #$Meteor is not an instance of #$NonAbducibleCollection in the context of the proof), but the inference engine could not abduce (#$primaryObjectMoving Event001 Object001) -- that literal would have to be proven by other means, if at all.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa7e8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa7e8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa7e8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa7e8,7878).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa7e8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa7e8,[]).
