x_comment(x_genlKBContentTests,"A specialization of #$genlSpecifications (q.v.) that relates instances of #$KBContentTest (q.v.) that ask the same question, but where one of the tests might specify more fully how the query is to be carried out (see #$softwareParameterValueInSpecification).  \n<p>\n<code>(#$genlKBContentTests SPECTEST GENLTEST)</code> means that the #$InferenceParameter values specified for SPECTEST subsume those specified for GENLTEST, and that SPECTEST and GENLTEST have the same \"question-content\" (see #$kbContentTestQuestion-Content).  The latter condition can be broken down into two cases:  either (i) SPECTEST and GENLTEST both have the same CycL formula as their respective #$InferenceSentenceParameter values or (ii) SPECTEST has the CycL formula <code>SENT</code> as its #$InferenceSentenceParameter value and GENLTEST is a #$KBContentTest-QueryContentOnly (q.v.) whose question-content is the intensional meaning (see #$IntensionOfQuerySentenceFn) expressed by <code>SENT</code>.\n<p>\nIn cases (i) above, GENLTEST might be a #$KBContentTest-QuerySentenceOnly (q.v.) that serves as a common generalization of various #$KBContentTest-FullySpecifieds.  In cases (ii), GENLTEST will be a #$KBContentTest-QueryContentOnly (q.v.) that serves as a common generalization of various fully- and partially-specified KB content tests whose specified inference sentence is logically equivalent to or synonymous with SENT.\n<p>\nThe intended use of #$genlKBContentTests is for tying together #$KBContentTest-FullySpecifieds that amount to variations on the same question, either because their respective query sentences are logically equivalent, or because they use the same query sentence but differ in some other inference parameter settings, such as the maximum time or the allowance of HL predicate transformations.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa8a0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa8a0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa8a0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa8a0,8150).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa8a0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa8a0,[]).
x_comment('x_kbContentTestQuestion-Content',"A binary predicate that relates a given #$KBContentTest (q.v.) to the propositional or otherwise intensional content (see #$Information-Content) of the question that the test is asking.  <code>(#$kbContentTestQuestion-Content TEST CONTENT)</code> means that TEST asks about CONTENT.  If the question being asked in TEST is a \"Yes/No\" question, then CONTENT will be a #$Proposition.   If the question being asked in TEST is a \"what things are such that\" question -- i.e. a question that asks for bindings for variables -- then CONTENT will be an intensional property or propositional function.\n<p>\nIf TEST has a specific CycL sentence SENT as its #$InferenceSentenceParameter (q.v.) value, then CONTENT will be (#$IntensionOfQuerySentenceFn SENT): the intensional meaning of SENT with respect to its potential use in a Cyc query.  If TEST is a #$KBContentTest-QueryContentOnly, then any specialization SPECTEST of TEST (see #$genlKBContentTests) must have CONTENT as its question-content as well; and if SPECTEST has SPECSENT as the value of its #$InferenceSentenceParameter, then (#$IntensionOfQuerySentenceFn SPECSENT) will be equal to CONTENT.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa8a1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa8a1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa8a1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa8a1,8157).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa8a1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa8a1,[]).
x_comment('x_kbContentTestImplies-QuerySentenceOnly',"A binary predicate that holds between two instances of #$KBContentTest-QuerySentenceOnly (q.v.) such that the query sentence of the first logically entails the query sentence of the second.  <code>(#$kbContentTestImplies-QuerySentenceOnly TEST1 TEST2)</code> means that if both of these hold:\n<pre>\n  (#$softwareParameterValueInSpecification\n    #$InferenceSentenceParameter SENT1 (#$TestQueryFn TEST1))\n\n  (#$softwareParameterValueInSpecification\n    #$InferenceSentenceParameter SENT2 (#$TestQueryFn TEST2))\n</pre>\nthen so does this:\n<pre>\n  (#$sentenceImplies SENT1 SENT2) .") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa8a2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aa8a2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aa8a2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aa8a2,8160).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aa8a2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aa8a2,[]).
x_comment('x_indMightPlayRoleInSitType-Evidential',"An assertion of the form (#$indMightPlayRoleInSitType-Linkage OBJ ROLE SIT-TYPE) is used to assert that there exists evidence that the #$SomethingExisting OBJ may be filling ROLE, an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate, in an instance of SIT-TYPE, some sub-collection of #$Situation") :- x_cid(a65b16517aa8a3).
