x_comment(x_genlPredsWRTArgs,"A ternary #$MetaPredicate that is used to state that two predicates stand in a #$genlPreds -like relation to each other with respect to a specified correlation between their argument-places.  The first predicate's #$arity might be the same as, greater than, or less than that of the second predicate; and the argument-place correlation is encoded by a list of non-negative integers whose length is equal to the arity of the second predicate.  The encoding scheme is roughly this: the position of a given integer within the list represents that same argument-position within the second predicate, while the size of the integer represents the corresponding argument-position within the first predicate.\n<p>\nFirst, suppose that <code>SPECPRED</code>'s arity is greater than or equal to that of <code>GENLPRED</code>.  In that case, each integer in <code>ARGNUMLIST</code> should be greater than 0, and <code>(#$genlPredsWRTArgs SPECPRED GENLPRED ARGNUMLIST)</code> means that, whenever <code>SPECPRED</code> holds of a given argument-sequence <code>SPEC-SEQ</code>, then <code>GENLPREDS</code> holds of the sequence <code>GENL-SEQ</code>, defined as follows: where the <code>I</code>th item in <code>ARGNUMLIST</code> is the integer <code>N</code>, the <code>I</code>th item in <code>GENL-SEQ</code> is the same as the <code>N</code>th item in <code>SPEC-SEQ</code>.  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$genlPredsWRTArgs #$touchesWith #$touches (#$TheList 3 2))\n</pre>\nentails\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$touchesWith #$MingXu Steak8 Fork25)\n    (#$touches Fork25 Steak8) .\n</pre>\nSecond, suppose that <code>SPECPRED</code>'s arity is less than that of <code>GENLPRED</code>, and the difference in their arities is <code>M</code>.  In that case, the integer 0 should appear in <code>ARGNUMLIST</code> in exactly <code>M</code> different places, and <code>(#$genlPredsWRTArgs SPECPRED GENLPRED ARGNUMLIST)</code> means that, whenever <code>SPECPRED</code> holds of a given argument-sequence <code>SPEC-SEQ</code>, there are <code>M</code> things <code>OBJ-1</code>, ..., <code>OBJ-M</code> such that <code>GENLPREDS</code> holds of the sequence <code>GENL-SEQ</code>, defined as follows: where the <code>I</code>th item in <code>ARGNUMLIST</code> is the integer <code>N</code> (for <code>N</code> > 0), the <code>I</code>th item in <code>GENL-SEQ</code> is the same as the <code>N</code>th item in <code>SPEC-SEQ</code>; and where the <code>I</code>th item in <code>ARGNUMLIST</code> is the <code>J</code>th appearance of 0 in <code>ARGNUMLIST</code> (for 1 <= <code>J</code> <= <code>M</code>), the <code>I</code>th item in <code>GENL-SEQ</code> is <code>OBJ-J</code>.  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$genlPredsWRTArgs #$transports #$transportsVia (#$TheList 1 2 0))\n</pre>\nentails\n<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$transports #$Klein Clutch5)\n    (#$thereExists ?X (#$transportsVia #$Klein Clutch5 ?X)) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nSee also #$genlPredsWRTTypes.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aac4a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aac4a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aac4a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aac4a,9794).
