x_comment(x_predicateUnion,"A ternary #$MetaPredicate that relates two predicates to the predicate that is by definition their <i>union</i> or <i>disjunction</i>, which holds whenever either of the first two predicates hold.  <code>(#$predicateUnion UNIONPRED PRED1 PRED2)</code> means that, for any sequence  <code>ARGS</code> and any context <code>MT</code>, <code>(UNIONPRED . ARGS)</code> holds in <code>MT</code> if and only if either <code>(PRED1 . ARGS)</code> or <code>(PRED2 . ARGS)</code> holds in <code>MT</code>.  <code>UNIONPRED</code> is thus  a generalization (via #$genlPreds) of both <code>PRED1</code> and <code>PRED1</code>; indeed  <code>UNIONPRED</code> is a <i>nearest possible common generalization</i> of the other two predicates.\n<p>\nFor example, this holds:\n<pre>\n  (#$predicateUnion #$biologicalOffspring #$biologicalSons #$biologicalDaughters)\n</pre>\nIf either <code>PRED1</code> or <code>PRED2</code> is of fixed arity <code>N</code>, then the other must either be of arity <code>N</code> or be variable-arity, and <code>UNIONPRED</code> is itself of arity <code>N</code>.  If both <code>PRED1</code> and <code>PRED2</code> are variable-arity, then so is <code>UNIONPRED</code>.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding function #$PredicateUnionFn; and see #$predicateIntersection and #$predicateIsBinaryCompositionOf.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aace7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aace7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aace7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aace7,10024).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aace7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aace7,[]).
x_comment(x_CommutativeRelation,"An instance of #$RelationTypeByLogicalFeature (q.v.) and a specialization of #$AtLeastPartiallyCommutativeRelation (q.v.).  Each instance of #$CommutativeRelation <code>COMRELN</code> is a predicate or function that is commutative in all of its argument-places.  That is, if <code>COMRELN</code> is a predicate (function) that holds among (has a value <code>VAL</code> for) a given sequence of arguments, then <code>COMRELN</code> also holds among (has the same value <code>VAL</code> for) any permutation of that  sequence.  \n<p>\nExamples of commutative relations include #$PlusFn, #$or, #$bordersOn, #$temporallyIntersects, and #$teammates.  Specialization include #$SymmetricBinaryPredicate.  \n<p>\nNote that most relations are <i>not</i> commutative.  Cf. the disjoint collection  #$PartiallyCommutativeRelation.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aace8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aace8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aace8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
