xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad11,[]).
x_comment(x_VisualSensoryQuantity,"A specialization of #$SensoryQuantity. Each instance of #$VisualSensoryQuantity is a sensory quality that can characterize a visual experience.  For example, it might be the subjective impression of a particular shade of blue (see #$ColorSensoryPerception), a certain level of perceived brightness, or a particular subjectively-experienced shape.  In short, a #$VisualSensoryQuantity is any property that can characterize \"how a thing looks\" to the experiencing subject.\n<p>\nAlthough typically used to describe experiences involved in #$VisualPerception, note that instances of #$VisualSensoryQuantity are also manifested in dreams and (visual) hallucinations.  And some synesthetics experience visual sensory quantities when presented with stimili not usually related to visual experience (e.g. sounds).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad12).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad12,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad12,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad12,10113).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad12,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad12,[]).
x_comment(x_ColorSensoryQuantity,"A specialization of #$VisualSensoryQuantity (q.v.). Instances of #$ColorSensoryQuantity are sensory qualities that characterize visual experience in term of the colors perceived.  For example, the subjective impression of a particular shade of blue is a color sensory quantity.\n<p>\nIf an object that is in fact blue looks (say) purple to me, then my concurrent visual experience displays the #$ColorSensoryQuantity normally associated only with experiences purple objects.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad13).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad13,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
