x_comment(x_subAssignments,"A binary #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate that applies to #$Assignment-Specifications (q.v.).  (#$subAssignments SUPER SUB) means that SUPER has SUB as one of its subordinate tasks.  This implies that (i) the fulfillment of SUPER (see #$actionFulfillsAssignment) depends in part upon the fulfillment of SUB, (ii) each of the #$allottedAgents of SUB must either be an assignee of SUPER or one of the #$groupMembers of (a #$MultiIndividualAgent that is) an assignee of SUPER, and (iii) the #$assignmentDeadline of SUB (if any) cannot be later than that of SUPER.\n<p>\nThe vocabulary for specifying how to satisfy an #$Assignment-Specification is quite flexible on the issue of representing complex assignments.  Specifically, one could break a single assignment down into multiple assignments and relate them using the #$subAssignments vocabulary.  Alternatively, one could have a single #$Assignment-Specification which bears the #$normativeSpecificationDescribesSituationType to a novel type of #$Situation, where that novel type has a complex sub-event structure.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad1b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad1b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
