x_comment(x_incapacitates,"<code>(#$incapacitates SIT AGENT ACT-TYPE)</code> means that <code>SIT</code> causes <code>AGENT</code> to be certainly incapable of doing actions that are instances of <code>ACT-TYPE</code>, <i>i.e.</i>, <code>ACT</code> brings it about that the #$derivedProbability of '<code>AGENT</code> would be successful at performing an instance of <code>ACT-TYPE</code> if she tried' is zero. Note that incapacitating factors prevent, but preventing factors need not be incapacitating. An example:\nbeing locked in a closet incapacitates one to play tennis; being a\nconvicted felon incapacitates one to run for elective offices in most\nU.S. states. Negative example: an appointment prevents, but doesn't\nincapacitate, me from meeting you at 3pm.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad4e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad4e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad4e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad4e,10250).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad4e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad4e,[]).
x_comment(x_actionFulfillsAssignment,"A specialization of #$obligationFulfilled that applies to #$Assignment-Obligations.  (#$actionFulfillsAssignment ACT TASK) means that the #$Action ACT fulfills or completes TASK.  This implies that (i) ACT is an instance of the type of action specified by TASK, (ii)  the agent(s) to whom the task was assigned (see #$allottedAgents) are all actors in ACT, and (iii) ACT is performed during the time the assignment is in effect (see #$assignmentPeriod).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad4f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad4f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad4f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad4f,10255).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad4f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad4f,[]).
x_comment(x_normativelyBoundAgents,"A #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate that is a generalization of both #$agreeingAgents and #$obligatedAgents (qq.v.).  The predicate #$normativelyBoundAgents relates a normative specification to any agent that it \"normatively constrains\" in some way or other.  Thus, it relates an agreement to the agents who are party to it, a proposal to the agent who proposed it, an obligation to the agents who are subject to it, and so on.\n<p>\nMore precisely: where <code>NORM</code> is a #$NormativeSpecification (q.v.) and <code>AGENT</code> is an #$IntelligentAgent, <code>(#$normativelyBoundAgents NORM AGENT)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is normatively constrained with respect to <code>NORM</code>.  <i>Normative constraints</i> are taken here to include commitments, responsibilities, duties, and prohibitions of all sorts -- moral, legal, religious, social, familial, professional, and so on.\n<p>\nIn some cases, the normative constraint derives primarily from some action of <code>AGENT</code>'s that has brought <code>NORM</code> about.  For example, <code>AGENT</code> might enter into an #$Agreement or put forth a #$Proposal, and thereby incur certain commitments or responsibilities.  In other cases, the normative constraint derives primarily from the fact that <code>AGENT</code> is in the scope of some normative principle embodied in <code>NORM</code>.  For example, by virtue of being an employed US citizen, <code>AGENT</code> might be subject to the general #$Obligation #$USIncomeTax.  And still other cases involve constraints deriving from a combination of both of the above sources.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad50).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad50,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad50,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad50,10256).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad50,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad50,[]).
x_comment(x_obligationsViolated,"(#$obligationsViolated ACTION OB) means that the #$Action ACTION is in violation of the #$Obligation OB. Note that the class of #$Obligations is very wide, and can include many types of 'obligation,' e.g., etiquette, moral codes, military rules of engagement, etc.  See also #$obligationFulfilled.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad51).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad51,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad51,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad51,10261).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad51,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad51,[]).
x_comment(x_preventsFromPlayingSpecifiedRole,"<code>(#$preventsFromPlayingSpecifiedRole SIT OBJ ROLE SPECIFICATION)</code> means that the occurance of the #$Situation <code>SIT</code> prevents <code>OBJ</code> from playing the role <code>ROLE</code> in any situation described by the #$NormativeSpecification <code>SPECIFICATION</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad52).
