x_comment(x_sociallySuperiorTo,"A specialization of both #$ableToControl and #$influencesAgent.  <code>(#$sociallySuperiorTo SUPERIOR SUBORDINATE)</code> means that <code>SUPERIOR</code> has some type of socially superior position over <code>SUBORDINATE</code> that gives <code>SUPERIOR</code> power over <code>SUBORDINATE</code>.  In many cases, <code>SUPERIOR</code> is able to command and punish <code>SUBORDINATE</code>, and can be held responsible for <code>SUBORDINATE</code>'s actions.  An important specialization of this predicate is #$hasAgents.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad75).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad75,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad75,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad75,10332).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad75,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad75,[]).
x_comment(x_ableToControl,"A predicate that relates an agent to some cotemporal thing s/he is able to control.  More precisely, <code>(#$ableToControl AGENT THING)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> is in a position of being able to either:\n<p>\n(i) influence (<i>i.e.</i>, prohibit, enable, or constrain) some significant set of behaviors (or actions or functionings) of <code>THING</code> or <br>\n(ii) influence some significant set of behaviors of other agents with respect to <code>THING</code>.  \n<p>\nFor example, Wilbur Post might be able to control some range of his horse Ed's actions in virtue of having the (physical and legal) power to force Ed to walk down this bridle path, stay in that corral, and so on; and Wilbur might also be able to control Ed in the sense of deciding who has access to him (<i>e.g.</i>, deciding who may see Ed and who may ride him).  \n<p>\nThe controlled <code>THING</code> might be a non-living possession of <code>AGENT</code>'s or, as in the above example, another <i>agent</i>.  Since one's ability to control something is rarely comprehensive, <code>(#$ableToControl AGENT THING)</code> can hold despite the fact there  are many behaviors/actions/functionings of <code>THING</code>'s that are beyond <code>AGENT</code>'s control.  Thus, although Wilbur might be able to control which paths Ed is ridden on, where he sleeps, and what he eats, Wilbur might not be able to control such things as Ed's breathing rate, tail swishing, or what he does in the privacy of his stall.  \n<p>\nNote that <code>AGENT</code>'s being #$ableToControl <code>THING</code> does not entail that <code>AGENT</code> actually exercises this ability.  Note also that <code>AGENT</code>'s merely being able to influence <i>some</i> behavior of <code>THING</code> (or some behavior of another agent's with respect to <code>THING</code>) is not sufficient for her being #$ableToControl <code>THING</code>; <code>AGENT</code> must be able to influence a significant set or range of <code>THING</code>'s behaviors (or of other agents' behaviors with respect to <code>THING</code>) in order to be able to control <code>THING</code> (see #$influencesAgent).  (Unavoidably, the notions of being \"able\" to do something and of a \"significant range\" of a thing's behaviors/actions/functionings are both rather vague.)  For describing events in which an agent actually wields control over something, consider using #$ControllingSomething and #$objectControlled.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad76).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad76,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad76,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad76,10333).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad76,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad76,[]).
x_comment(x_wouldBeEvidenceForPlayingRole,"(#$wouldBeEvidenceForPlayingRole AGENT SITUATION ROLE SENTENCE) means that if SENTENCE were true, it would be evidence that (ROLE SITUATION AGENT).  This can be regarded as meaning that if SENTENCE were true, the conditional probability of (ROLE SITUATION AGENT) given SENTENCE would be higher than the prior (or extant) probability of (ROLE SITUATION AGENT).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad77).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad77,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad77,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad77,10343).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad77,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad77,[]).
x_comment('x_fosters-SitTypeSitType',"A #$TransitiveBinaryPredicate that relates types of situations to other types of situations, where instances of the latter types are fostered by instances of the former types. <code>(#$fosters-SitTypeSitType SIT-TYPE1 SIT-TYPE2)</code> means that the kind of #$Situation <code>SIT-TYPE1</code> fosters the kind of #$Situation <code>SIT-TYPE2</code>.  Often <code>SIT-TYPE2</code> is a specialization of #$Event and <code>SIT-TYPE1</code> is a specialization of #$Event or #$Action.  Fostering is a looser relation than causation: if <code>SIT-TYPE1</code> causes <code>SIT-TYPE2</code> (#$causes-SitTypeSitType), it certainly fosters it; but a situation-type can foster another without being a cause of it.  While it isn't necessarily the case when <code>(#$fosters-SitTypeSitType SIT-TYPE1 SIT-TYPE2)</code> that the occurrence of an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE1</code> is typically associated with the occurrence of an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE2</code>, it will be the case that the occurrence of an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE1</code> is one of the most promising ways of bringing about an occurrence of an instance of <code>SIT-TYPE2</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad78).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad78,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad78,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad78,10344).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad78,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad78,[]).
x_comment('x_causes-SitProp',"(#$causes-SitProp SIT PROP) means that the situation SIT causes the #$CycLSentence-Assertible PROP to become true.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad79).
