x_comment(x_instanceActualizesSubSituationType,"The predicate #$instanceActualizesSubSituationType is used to represent the actualization of a specialization of #$Situation's conditional sub-situation types (see, #$candidateProperSubSituationTypes) within an instance of that #$Situation.  Specifically, an assertion of the form (#$instanceActualizesSubSituationType SIT-INSTANCE SUB-SIT-TYPE) means that the SIT-INSTANCE has as a #$subSituations an instance of SUB-SIT-TYPE.  The purpose of this predicate is to allow for the representation of candidate / conditional sub-situation types, but also to represent the actualization of those candidate / conditional scenes within its instances.  This predicate is introduced as a way of reducing the distinct number of situation types which are needed.  Let us focus on the complex event type #$StayingAtCommercialLodging.  A very common, though not essential, sub-event in instances of #$StayingAtCommercialLodging would be the request for and receiving of a wake-up call.  Now, rather than have two event types, one which includes the request and receiving and the other which does not, we can use the predicate #$instanceActualizesSubSituationType to specify rules that dictate the conditions under which instances of a single type will have instances of the requesting and receiving as sub-events.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad7d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad7d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad7d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad7d,10349).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad7d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad7d,[]).
x_comment(x_goalProvidesMotiveFor,"<code>(#$goalProvidesMotiveFor GOAL AGT\nACT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that <code>AGT</code>'s having the goal <code>GOAL</code> would provide her with a motive for acting in the specified way -- <i>i.e.</i>, for playing <code>ROLE</code> in an instance of <code>ACT-TYPE</code>.  Note that <code>(#$goalProvidesMotiveFor GOAL\nAGT ...)</code> does not entail that <code>AGT</code> actually has <code>GOAL</code> as a goal -- only that, <i>if</i> she had this goal,\nshe would be credited (ordinarily) with having a motive for doing\nsome instance of <code>ACT-TYPE</code>. #$goalProvidesMotiveFor is a specialization of #$providesMotiveFor which focuses on common motivational links\nbetween #$goals and action-types. For further discussion concerning\nthe notion of 'motive' at work here, see the comment on\n#$providesMotiveFor.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad7e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad7e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aad7e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aad7e,10350).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aad7e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aad7e,[]).
x_comment(x_CognitivePredicate,"A specialization of #$AgentPredicate.  Each instance of #$CognitivePredicate is a (binary or higher-arity) relation that holds among (at least) an agent and some thing to which the agent has some cognitive connection (perceptual, epistemic, or attitudinal).  Two important specializations of #$CognitivePredicate are #$CognitiveAwarenessPredicate (q.v.), whose instances (e.g. #$tastes, #$knowsAbout) relate an agent to that which the agent perceives or is otherwise aware of, and #$CognitiveAttitudePredicate (q.v.), whose instances (e.g. #$beliefs, #$goals) relate an agent (at least) to an #$CycLSentence-Assertible expressing (all or part of) the content of the agent's belief, goal, etc.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad7f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad7f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
