x_comment(x_mtMonad,"For microtheories <code>MT</code> and <code>BASE</code>, <code>(#$mtMonad MT MONAD)</code> means that <code>MONAD</code> constitutes the 'base' of <code>MT</code> in the sense that <code>MT</code> is otherwise specified by its values of specific dimensions (see #$MicrotheoryDimensionPredicate).  Another way to look at this is that <code>MONAD</code> constitutes whatever left of <code>MT</code> (or the 'rest' of <code>MT</code>) after having the values of explicit dimensions we can handle.  For example, if one creates a microtheory <code>MT</code> about social life in U.S. during 1990-2000, s/he can simply assert (#$mtMonad MT #$UnitedStatesSocialLifeMt) and (#$mtTimeIndex <code>MT</code> (#$TimeIntervalInclusiveFn (#$YearFn 1990) (#$YearFn 2000))) etc.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aad8e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aad8e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
