x_comment(x_OneDimensionalQuantity,"A specialization of #$MeasurableQuantity (q.v.).  Each instance of #$OneDimensionalQuantity is a measurable quantity that is <i>not</i> defined in terms of (i.e. as the product or ratio of) other quantities.  For example, (#$Mile 65) and (#$HoursDuration 1) are both instances of #$OneDimensionalQuantity; but (#$MilesPerHour 65), derived by dividing the former quantity by the latter, is not (cf. #$MultiDimensionalQuantity).\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$OneDimensionalQuantity include #$Distance, #$Mass, and #$Time-Quantity.\n<p>\nEach one-dimensional quantity is equal to the result of applying some #$OneDimensionalUnitOfMeasure (q.v.) to some number or other #$NumericInterval (or pair of such intervals).   If a #$OneDimensionalQuantity is multiplied or divided by a numeric-interval, the result is still a one-dimensional quantity; if two one-dimensional quantities that are #$numericallyComparable (q.v.) are added or subtracted, the result is still a one-dimensional quantity.  Conversely, whenever one #$OneDimensionalQuantity is multiplied or divided by another #$OneDimensionalQuantity, the result is a #$TwoDimensionalQuantity.  Two quantities that are not numerically-comparable cannot be added to or substracted from each other.  And when a #$OneDimensionalQuantity is multiplied or divided by (or divides) a #$TwoDimensionalQuantity, the result is in many cases a #$ThreeDimensionalQuantity (q.v.).\n<p>\nSee also #$PrimitiveOrderedQuantityType and #$FundamentalUnitType.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aae24).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aae24,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aae24,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aae24,10585).
