x_comment('x_sourceOfAssertion-NonTrivial',"<code>(#$sourceOfAssertion-NonTrivial SOURCE ASSERTION)</code> means that the instance of #$InformationStore <code>SOURCE</code> is a non-trivial source for the instance of #$CycLAssertion <code>ASSERTION</code>. \n<p>\nA <i>non-trivial source</i> is a source such as a dictionary, a webpage or a periodicals that contains the information expressed by <code>ASSERTION</code>, and which was used to as a justification for representing <code>ASSERTION</code>. A <i>trivial source</i>, in contrast, is the mechanism used to assert <code>ASSERTION</code> (such as the work of a particular cyclist -- see #$assertionAssertBy).\n<p>\nThis predicate allows the system to distinguish source information that should be\ngenerated to a user from source information that should not. For example, the assertion '(#$literacyRateForGroupInRegion #$Estonia #$Person (#$Percent 100))' was asserted into the KB by Kathy Panton, and she got this information from the CIA 1997 World Factbook. #$sourceOfAssertion-NonTrivial is defined to indicate that the CIA 1997 World Factbook, not Kathy, is the non-trivial source.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aae35).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aae35,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aae35,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
