x_comment(x_ThatFn,"A unary function that takes a #$CycLPropositionalSentence (q.v.) to the #$Proposition it #$expresses with respect to the intended interpretation of the CycL language.  <code>(#$ThatFn SENTENCE)</code> is the proposition that <code>SENTENCE</code> is true.  For example, the non-atomic term\n<pre>\n  (#$ThatFn (#$likesAsFriend #$Muffet #$Murray))\n</pre>\ndenotes the proposition that Muffet like Murray.\n<p>\nNote that the argument-place of #$ThatFn is \"implicitly quoted\" (see #$quotedArgument).  Thus the above non-atomic term was constructed by applying the functor <code>#$ThatFn</code> to the <code>#$likesAsFriend</code> sentence directly; there was no need to wrap the sentence in <code>#$Quote</code>.  Note further that, because of this quoted argment-place, #$ThatFn is <i>not</i> related to the predicate #$expresses (which has no quoted argument-place) via #$functionCorrespondingPredicate.  But for any #$CycLPropositionalSentence <code>SENTENCE</code>, the following holds:\n<pre>\n  (#$expresses (#$Quote SENTENCE) (#$ThatFn SENTENCE)) .") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaf74).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaf74,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
