x_comment('x_facets-Generic',"A #$TaxonomicSlotForCollections and a specialization (via #$genlInverse) of #$typeGenls (q.v.). This is the most general of the \"faceting\" predicates. <code>(#$facets-Generic COL COLTYPE)</code> means that <code>COLTYPE</code> corresponds to one way of dividing <code>COL</code> into subcollections. The instances of <code>COLTYPE</code> are all \"the same sort\" of subcollection of <code>COL</code>. For example, #$MovieTypeByGenre facets #$Movie-CW according to movie-genre; the former's instances thus include such collections as #$WesternMovie and #$HorrorMovie. And #$FunctionTypeByRangeType facets #$Function-Denotational according to the type of thing a given function returns as values; the former's instances thus include such collections as #$IndividualDenotingFunction and #$CollectionDenotingFunction.\n<p>\nNote that generic faceting does not entail that the instances of <code>COLTYPE</code> are mutually disjoint. Thus, a given film might be an instance of <i>both</i> #$ChildrensMovie and #$ActionMovie.\n<p>\nNote also that generic faceting does not entail that every instance of <code>COL</code> falls under some instance of <code>COLTYPE</code>. Thus, consider the universal identity function #$IdentityFn. Its range of values is so broad that it does not fall under any instance of #$FunctionTypeByRangeType.\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$facets-Generic include #$facets-Covering, #$facets-Strict, and #$facets-Partition, as well as #$facets-NearCovering, #$facets-NearStrict, and #$facets-NearPartition.  Whenever possible, one of these more specific predicates should be used.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaf76).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaf76,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aaf76,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
