x_comment(x_equalSymbols,"A binary #$MetaPredicate and a specialization of #$equals, both of whose arguments are #$quotedArguments (q.v.), that can be used to make identity assertions about symbols.  Stated loosely,  <code>(#$equalSymbols X Y)</code> means that <code>X</code> and <code>Y</code> are one and the same symbol.  Given that the arguments are \"quoted\", however, it is more accurate to say: A closed atomic formula consisting of the expression <code>#$equalSymbols</code> followed by CycL expressions <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> (all enclosed within a pair of parentheses) is true if and only if <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> are (two occurrences of) the same expression.\n<p>\nNote that this is stronger than merely saying that <code>A</code> and <code>B</code> denote the same thing.  As an example (using English expressions for convenience), the name 'Bertrand Russell' and the description 'the author of \"On Denoting\"' are two different expressions, even though they both denote the same person.  Two symbols can even share the same intensional meaning and still be different symbols; e.g. 'bachelor' and 'unmarried male'.  The relation #$equalSymbols <i>does</i> hold, however, between 'bachelor' and 'bachelor' (at least if we pretend that 'bachelor' is a CycL expression), as it does between the CycL constant <code>#$BertrandRussell</code> and the CycL constant <code>#$BertrandRussell</code>.\n<p>\nSee also #$differentSymbols.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaf84).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaf84,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aaf84,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aaf84,11088).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aaf84,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aaf84,[]).
x_comment(x_HistoricalPredicate,"A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation (qq.v.).  A  predicate <code>PRED</code> is a #$HistoricalPredicate if and only if it has the following property: if any given sentence of the form <code>(PRED ARG1 ... ARGN)</code> is true at time index <code>T</code> (see #$mtTimeIndex) it necessarily remains so for the rest of time.  Nothing can begin its existence with an #$HistoricalPredicate holding of it.\n<p>\nFor example, #$hasAlumni is a #$HistoricalPredicate.  Once a sentence of the form <code>(#$hasAlumni SCHOOL AGENT)</code> becomes true, it remains true for the rest of time.  Further, for nothing is it the case that #$hasAlumni holds of it at the begining of its existence.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaf85).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaf85,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aaf85,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aaf85,11093).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aaf85,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aaf85,[]).
x_comment(x_TemporalExistencePredicate,"A #$PredicateType and a specialization of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation.  #$TemporalExistencePredicate is the collection of predicates that entail the existence of at least some of their arguments throughout the entire time in which the relation holds of those arguments.\n<p>\nMore precisely, <code>PRED</code> is a #$TemporalExistencePredicate if and only if it is \"contemporary\" in at least one of its argument-places (see #$contemporaryInArg).  That is, there is at least one integer <code>I</code> (1 <= <code>I</code> <= <code>N</code>) such that, if <code>(PRED ARG1 ... ARGN)</code> holds at a time-index <code>TIME</code> (see #$mtTimeIndex), then the temporal extent of <code>ARGI</code> subsumes <code>TIME</code> (see #$temporallySubsumes).\n<p>\nExamples of #$TemporalExistencePredicates are #$lengthOfObject and #$thinksAbout.  A specialization is #$CotemporalPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaf86).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaf86,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aaf86,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
