x_comment(x_hasDenotatum,"A unary #$MetaLanguagePredicate (q.v.) that holds of instances of #$CycLTerm that denote or refer to something in the intended model of the CycL language.  <code>(#$hasDenotatum TERM)</code> means that there is something <code>THING</code> such that <code>TERM</code> denotes (see #$denotes) <code>THING</code>.  Note that #$hasDenotatum's argument-place is <i>not</i> implicitly quoted (see #$quotedArgument); instead, <code>TERM</code> will often be an explicitly quoted term -- i.e. something wrapped in the syncategorematic quotation symbol '#$Quote' (see #$Quote).  For example, the sentence '(#$hasDenotatum (#$Quote #$Plato))' means that the CycL term '#$Plato' denotes something, which is true because that term denotes the man Plato.  But note that the argument to #$hasDenotatum need not be quoted.  Suppose (e.g.) that the term '#$Plato' were the CycL constant most beloved of #$Cyclists, and that a new constant 'CyclistsFavoriteConstant' were reified and defined so as to reflect this fact.  Then 'CyclistsFavoriteConstant' would refer to the CycL constant '#$Plato', which in turn denotes the man Plato; and so the sentence '(#$hasDenotatum CyclistsFavoriteConstant)' would be true.  Note finally that the sentence '(#$hasDenotatum #$Plato)' is <i>not</i> true, as it means that the man Plato is a CycL term that denotes something, which is a patent falsehood since no man is a CycL term and no man denotes anything.  See also #$EscapeQuote, #$quotedIsa, and the shared #$NoteAboutQuotingInCycL.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aafe7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aafe7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aafe7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aafe7,11286).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aafe7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aafe7,[]).
x_comment(x_specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration,"A unary #$MetaLanguagePredicate (q.v.) that holds of a certain kind of #$CycLTerm that, via its internal structure, specifies its denotatum -- which will either be a set or a list (ordered n-tuple) -- by enumerating the items that constitute it.\n<p>\n<code>(#$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration NAT)</code> means that there is some <code>SETORLIST</code> such that <code>NAT</code> denotes (see #$denotes) <code>SETORLIST</code>, and does so in virtue of having constituent terms that serve to enumerate the items that belong to <code>SETORLIST</code>.  These constituent terms supply the arguments to a function (denoted by <code>NAT</code>'s arg0 operator) that returns the value <code>SETORLIST</code> (a particular #$Set-Mathematical or #$List) for those arguments.  The function in question might be #$TheSet, #$TheList, #$TheCovering, or #$ThePartition.\n<p>\nFor example, the sentence\n<p>\n(#$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration (#$Quote (#$ThePartition #$BaleenWhale #$ToothedWhale)))\n<p>\nis true because the CycL term \"(#$ThePartition #$BaleenWhale #$ToothedWhale)\" specifies, by enumeration, the set whose members are the two collections #$BaleenWhale and #$ToothedWhale.\n<p>\nNote that the predicate's argument-place is <i>not</i> a #$quotedArgument (q.v.), so the arg1 position of a #$specifiesSetOrListByEnumeration sentence will typically be filled by an <i>explicitly</i> quoted expression.  Thus the use of #$Quote (q.v.) in the above example is crucial; without it the arg1 would be a partition-set instead of a CycL term, in violation of the predicate's argument-constraint.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aafe8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aafe8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aafe8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
