x_comment(x_actionConnotesFeeling,"(#$actionConnotesFeeling ACTION FEELING) means that\nACTION, by virtue of its type or circumstances, suggests an expression\nof FEELING.  ACTION may or may not be genuinely motivated by FEELING\n(see #$actionExpressesFeeling), but it in some way\nsuggests this.   For example, some types of actions commonly express  certain \nfeelings (see\n#$actionTypeExpressesFeelingType), and so their instances \nmay suggest such a motivation regardless of their real cause.\n\n E.g., one might cover one's face with\none's hands out of a genuine feeling of #$Shame, or merely to feign\nsuch a feeling.  Similarly, there are various (often\nculturally-specific) ways of signaling certain feelings within certain\nsocial settings, e.g., culturally-defined ways of expressing #$Respect\nor #$Friendliness.  Such actions are often engaged in to conform to a\ncultural norm, and aren't always motivated by possession of the\nassociated feeling.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaff7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaff7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aaff7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aaff7,11314).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aaff7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aaff7,[]).
x_comment(x_actionConnotesFeelingToward,"(#$actionConnotesFeelingToward ACTION FEELING THING) means\nthat ACTION suggests or gives the appearance of expressing FEELING (#$Friendliness,\n#$Disapproval, etc.) towards THING. ACTION may or may not be genuinely\nmotivated by FEELING (see #$actionExpressesFeeling), but it in some\nway suggests this.  For further discussion of this type of link\nbetween action and feeling, see #$actionConnotesFeeling.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aaff8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aaff8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
