x_comment(x_genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs,"#$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs is used to express a #$genlPreds -like relationship between a predicate SPEC-PRED that has at least one quoted argument place (see #$quotedArgument) and a predicate GENL-PRED of the same arity that<br><br> 1) has argIsa/argGenl constraints that are at least as general as those of SPEC-PRED,<br><br> 2) is <i>not</i> quoted in at least one of the argument places in which SPEC-PRED is, and<br><br>  3) is <i>only</i> quoted in argument places in which SPEC-PRED is also quoted.  <p>As such, GENL-PRED need not have any quoted argument places.  <p>In (#$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs SPEC-PRED GENL-PRED SET), SET is the set of integers that correspond to quoted argument places in SPEC-PRED that are <i>not</i> quoted in GENL-PRED, thereby indicating a recipe for deriving a sentence GENL-SENT whose arg0 is GENL-PRED from a sentence SPEC-SENT whose arg0 is SPEC-PRED:  First, each argument in SPEC-SENT occuring in the Nth argument place, where N is neither 0 nor a member of LIST, will occur in the Nth argument place in GENL-SENT.  All other non-0 arguments in SPEC-SENT, which by definition occur in the Mth argument place, where M is an element of SET, will appear as explicitly quoted arguments in GENL-SENT, in those same argument places.  For example,\n<pre>\n   (#$genlPredsWRTQuotedArgs #$cycLActors #$situationConstituents (#$TheSet 2))\n</pre>\nentails that whenever\n<pre>\n   (#$cycLActors ExaminingTheCycLTermHashDollarDog #$Dog)\n</pre>\nholds,\n<pre>\n   (#$situationConstituents ExaminingTheCycLTermHashDollarDog (#$Quote #$Dog)).\n</pre>\nfollows.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab2ff).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab2ff,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab2ff,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab2ff,12334).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab2ff,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab2ff,[]).
