x_comment('x_geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed',"A binary predicate and specialization of #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion (q.v.) that relates two space regions (see #$HomogeneousExtendedSpaceRegion) when one is geometrically-inside the other and there is <i>no</i> open channel from the first region to the outside of the second region.  Instead, the outside of the second region is closed-off to the first region by a point connection.  \n<p>\nMore precisely, <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed INSIDE REGION)</code> holds if and only if (i) <code>(#$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion INSIDE REGION)</code> holds), (ii) the mereological sum <code>IN+OUT</code> of the inside and the outside of <code>REGION</code> -- i.e. the region denoted by\n<pre>  (#$SpaceRegionSumFn \n    (#$InsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>) \n    (#$OutsideOfSpaceRegionFn <code>REGION</code>))\n</pre>\n-- is a #$ConnectedSpaceRegion, and (iii) <code>IN+OUT</code> is <i>not</i> a #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion.\n<p>\nHere's an example.  Let <code>C1</code>, <code>C2</code>, and <code>C3</code> be three circles such that (a) <code>C1</code> is properly inside and non-tangential with respect to <code>C2</code> and (b) <code>C2</code> is properly inside <code>C3</code> and tangent to it at some point <code>P</code>.  Then the region bounded by <code>C1</code> is #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed with respect to the region between <code>C2</code> and <code>C3</code>.\n<p>\nCf. #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Open.\n<p>\nNote that #$geometricallyInsideSpaceRegion-Closed is the relation called <i>GEO-INSIDE-CLOSED</i> in #$ASpatialLogicBasedOnRegionsAndConnection-Article.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab3ee).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab3ee,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab3ee,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab3ee,12685).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab3ee,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab3ee,[]).
