x_comment(x_MultiplyConnectedThing,"A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of \"multiply connected\" spatial things, tangible and intangible.  Each instance <code>MULTCONN</code> of #$MultiplyConnectedThing is a spatially continuous (i.e. \"connected\") thing such that there is a simple closed curve (see #$ClosedLinearObject) subsumed by (see #$spatiallySubsumes) <code>MULTCONN</code> that, if shunk to a point continuously, would at some stage cease to be subsumed by <code>MULTCONN</code>.  Intuitively, this means that <code>MULTCONN</code> is a one-piece spatial thing with one or more <i>holes</i> (or pits or pockets) in it.\n<p>\nFor example, a bowling ball is multiply-connected, but a bowling pin is not.  A sewing needle is multiply-connected, but a sewing pin is not.  (Both of the pins  are instead #$SimplyConnectedThings.)   \n<p>\nNote that the question as to whether a given connected <i>tangible</i> object (as opposed to an intangible #$SpaceRegion) is simply- or multiply-connected must be  considered a context-dependent matter of \"local granularity\".  Thus, in a microscopic context, a slight scratch on a bowling pin might constititute a \"pit\", rendering the pin a multiply-connected thing in that context.  Indeed, in certain subatomic contexts <i>any</i> material object would be viewed as full of holes (and perhaps even as a disconnected thing).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab5a6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab5a6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab5a6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab5a6,13485).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab5a6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab5a6,[]).
