x_comment(x_LinearObjectTypeByStraightness,"A #$SecondOrderCollection that facets  and covers (see #$facets-Covering) the collection #$LinearObject, according to how close a linear object is to being completely straight. Relative straightness, in the sense relevent here, is measured roughly in terms of the dimensionality of a space in which a linear object can be embedded.  For example, a perfectly straight line segment (see #$LineSegment-Straight) can be embedded in a one-dimensional space; some curved line segments (e.g. #$Arcs) require at least a two-dimensional space for embedding; and other curved line segments (e.g. linear spirals) require three dimensions for embedding.  The reified instances of #$LinearObjectTypeByStraightness include the successively-subsuming collections #$LinearObject-Straight, #$LinearObject-Planar, and #$LinearObject itself.\n<p>\nNote that the qualifier \"<i>roughly</i>\" is used above because some #$LinearObjects are actually <i>poly-dimensional</i> (e.g. a piece of wire is 3-D), and we also want to distinguish between \"straight\" and \"planar\" and \"non-planar\" poly-dimensional linear objects (e.g. pins vs. staples vs. springs).  So some poly-dimensional linear objects will be borderline and/or context-dependent cases with respect to which straightness category they belong to.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab5fd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab5fd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab5fd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab5fd,13578).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab5fd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab5fd,[]).
x_comment(x_LinearObjectTypeByDimensions,"A second-order collection that facets  and partitions (see #$facets-Partition) the collection #$LinearObject (q.v.), according to how many spatial dimensions a linear-object has.  Geometric lines and line-segments are of course one-dimensional.  In a two-dimensional contexts, however, certain \"ribbon\"-shaped regions (e.g. rivers depicted on a map) might be treated as lines.  And in three-dimensional contexts (e.g. the physical world), certain long thin 3-D tangible objects (e.g. wires or pencil markings) are often treated as lines.  The instances of #$LinearObjectTypeByDimensions are thus: #$LinearObject-OneDimensional, #$LinearObject-TwoDimensional, and #$LinearObject-ThreeDimensional.\n<p>\nNote that some poly-dimensional objects will be borderline and/or context-dependent cases with respect to whether they are #$LinearObjects, as opposed to being #$Surfaces or solids.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab5fe).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab5fe,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab5fe,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
