x_comment('x_SurfaceRegion-Bounded',"A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of tangible and intangible surfaces that have some closed boundary, such as a perimeter or hole.  More precisely, a surface is an instance of #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded if and only if it has a boundary, and its (total) boundary is -- or has a part that is -- a finite, unbounded, and  spatially-continuous (see #$ClosedLinearObject).  \n<p>\nFor example, a #$Circle is a bounded surface; and so is the skin of an apple with a bite taken out of it.  Conversely, a #$Plane and intact apple skin are both  instances of #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded.  Also cf. #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab682).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab682,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab682,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab682,13763).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab682,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab682,[]).
x_comment('x_SurfaceRegion-Unbounded',"A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion (q.v.).  This is the collection of surfaces that have <i>no boundary</i> whatsoever: no perimeter, hole, or edge.  An unbounded surface might be finite or infinite, tangible or intangible.\n<p>\nFor example, a #$Plane is an unbounded, infinite, intangible surface.  And an intact apple skin is an unbounded, finite, tangible surface.  Conversely, a #$HalfPlane is a #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded (cf.), and an apple skin with a bite taken out is a #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded (cf.).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab683).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab683,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab683,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab683,13766).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab683,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab683,[]).
x_comment('x_SurfaceRegion-Tangible',"A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion and #$PartiallyTangible.  This is the collection of all physical (and thus technically three-dimensional) surfaces, i.e. tangible surfaces of tangible objects.  Each instance of #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible has a (non-zero) thickness that is much less than its average length or width.  A tangible surface region might have holes, tears, or be unconnected and in multiple pieces; it might be flat, curved, or crumpled.  Examples include the skin of an apple and the top side of a tabletop. \n<p>\nCf. #$Surface, whose instances are two-dimensional, intangible regions of space.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab684).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab684,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab684,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab684,13769).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab684,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab684,[]).
x_comment(x_titlesConventionallyQuoted,"(#$titlesConventionallyQuoted WORK) means that titles of WORK (cf. #$titleOfWork) are generally quoted in written references.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab685).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab685,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
