x_comment(x_relationInvokesTypeDerivatively,"The most general predicate for relating a relation RELATION to a collection COLL that the relation invokes, where \"invokes\" has a very specific definition.  <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively RELATION COLL)</code> iff either one of two conditions holds.  <p><i>First</i>: RELATION has an explicit #$argIsa or #$argGenl constraint to a specialization SPEC of COLL; this, by convention, is an <i>explicit</i> (albeit derived) invocation of COLL by RELATION.  For example, since <code>(#$arg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, since #$actionMotives has an explicit #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and <code>(#$genls #$Motive #$Motive)</code>.  Similarly, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> given the #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and the fact that <code>(#$genls #$Motive #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> holds.</p>  <p><i>Second</i>: RELATION is an instance of #$Predicate that, by its very definition, is such that from a closed sentence of the form, (RELATION . ARGS) one can conclude for some predicate PREDICATE (PREDICATE . PRED-ARGS) where there is some argument ARG in PRED-ARGS that is either an instance or a specialization of COLL.  For example, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$agentHasMotiveForAction #$Motive)</code> and <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$agentHasMotiveForAction #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</code> are both true, not because #$agentHasMotiveForAction has an explicit argument constraint of #$Motive, but because by the very definition of #$agentHasMotiveForAction, <code>(#$agentHasMotiveForAction AGENT ACTION)</code> <i>means</i> that there exists an instance MOTIVE of #$Motive such that AGENT has MOTIVE in performing ACTION.  This, by convention, is an <i>implicit</i> invocation of COLL.  Typically, in the case of implicit invocation, RELATION will be an instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6b2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6b2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6b2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6b2,13864).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6b2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6b2,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively,"The most general predicate for relating a relation RELATION to a collection COLL that the relation explicity and directly invokes, where \"explicitly and directly invokes\" has a very specific definition.  <code>(#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively RELATION COLL)</code> iff  RELATION has an explicit #$argIsa or #$argGenl constraint to a specialization SPEC of COLL.  For example, since \n<pre>\n   (#$arg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</pre> and\n<pre>\n   (#$genls #$Motive #$Motive)</pre> both hold, \n<pre>\n   (#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$Motive)</pre> holds.  Similarly,\n<pre>\n   (#$relationExplicitlyInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</pre> follows from the #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive, and the fact that \n<pre>\n   (#$genls #$Motive #$IntentionalMentalSituation)</pre> holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6b3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6b3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6b3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6b3,13865).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6b3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6b3,[]).
x_comment(x_nearestArgIsa,"A specialization of #$argIsa (q.v.).  <code>(#$nearestArgIsa RELATION NUMBER COL)</code> means that not only is it the case that\n<pre>\n   (#$argIsa RELATION NUMBER COL)</pre> but also\n<pre>\n   (#$unknownSentence\n     (#$thereExists ?GENL\n        (#$and\n           (#$argIsa RELATION NUMBER ?GENL)\n           (#$different ?GENL COL)\n           (#$genls ?GENL COL)))).</pre>  In other words, COL is a \"nearest\" -- i.e., non-subsuming -- #$argIsa constraint for RELATION in the NUMBER-th place.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6b4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6b4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6b4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6b4,13876).
