x_comment(x_predExpansionListForType,"An instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.  #$predExpansionListForType relates a predicate PREDICATE to a collection TYPE and a list LIST of binary predicates such that: 1) Each member MEMBER-1 through MEMBER-N of LIST has an #$arg1Isa that is at least as general as TYPE; 2) The placement of MEMBER-M in list as its Mth member indicates that the #$arg2Isa of MEMBER is consistent with the argMthIsa of PREDICATE; a conjunction of the form,\n<pre>\n  (#$and\n    (#$isa ?TYPE-INS TYPE)\n    (MEMBER-1 ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-1)\n       .\n       .\n       .\n    (MEMBER-N ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-N))</pre>\nentails a sentence of the form,\n<pre>\n  (PREDICATE ARG-2-MEMBER-1 ... ARG-2-MEMBER-N);</pre>\nand 3) from \n<pre>\n  (PREDICATE ARG-2-MEMBER-1 ... ARG-2-MEMBER-N)</pre>\na sentence of the form,\n<pre>\n  (#$thereExists ?TYPE-INS\n    (#$and\n      (#$isa ?TYPE-INS TYPE)\n      (MEMBER-1 ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-1)\n         .\n         .\n         .\n      (MEMBER-N ?TYPE-INS ARG-2-MEMBER-N)))</pre>\nfollows.  Thus <code>(#$predExpansionListForType TYPE LIST PREDICATE)</code> means that PREDICATE is fully axiomatizable -- rules giving necessary and sufficient conditions for its application can be authored -- in the way indicated above.  An important specialization of #$predExpansionListForType is #$nonDavidsonianPredReln, which allows one to define a non-davidsonian predicate from a series of binary situation predicates and a given situation-type.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6e4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6e4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6e4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6e4,13955).
