x_comment(x_relationInvokesType,"The most general predicate for relating a relation RELATION to a collection COLL that the relation directly invokes, where \"directly invokes\" has a very specific definition.  <code>(#$relationInvokesType RELATION COLL)</code> iff either one of two conditions holds.  <p><i>First</i>: RELATION has a #$nearestArgIsa or #$nearestArgGenl constraint to COLL; this, by convention, is an <i>explicit</i> invocation of COLL by RELATION.  For example, since <code>(#$nearestArg2Isa #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code>, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$actionMotives #$Motive)</code> holds, since #$actionMotives has an explicit #$argIsa constraint of #$Motive.</p>  <p><i>Second</i>: RELATION is an instance of #$Predicate that, by its very definition, is such that from a closed sentence of the form, (RELATION . ARGS) one can conclude for some predicate PREDICATE (PREDICATE . PRED-ARGS) where PREDICATE has either an #$argIsa or #$argGenl of COLL.  For example, <code>(#$relationInvokesTypeDerivatively #$agentHasMotiveForAction #$Motive)</code> is true, not because #$agentHasMotiveForAction has an explicit argument constraint of #$Motive, but because by the very definition of #$agentHasMotiveForAction, <code>(#$agentHasMotiveForAction AGENT ACTION)</code> <i>means</i> that there exists MOTIVE, such that (#$agentsActionHasMotive AGENT ACTION MOTIVE), where <code>(#$isa MOTIVE #$Motive)</code> due to the fact that <code>(#$arg3Isa #$agentsActionHasMotive #$Motive)</code>.  This, by convention, is an <i>implicit</i> invocation of COLL.  Typically, in the case of implicit invocation, RELATION will be an instance of #$RuleMacroPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6e7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6e7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6e7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6e7,13983).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6e7,[]).
