x_comment(x_ClosedSurfaceRegion,"A specialization of #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded and  #$SurfaceRegion-Finite (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$ClosedSurfaceRegion is a finite surface region with no boundary (i.e. no perimeter, edge, or hole).  \n<p>\nFor example, the total surface of a billiard ball or a cube is a closed surface region.\n<p>\nSpecializations of #$ClosedSurfaceRegion include #$Ellipsoid.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6ef).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6ef,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6ef,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6ef,13995).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6ef,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6ef,[]).
x_comment(x_SurfaceRegion,"A specialization of #$PolyDimensionalThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of all spatial surfaces, tangible or intangible (see the specializations #$SurfaceRegion-Tangible and #$Surface).  Each instance of #$SurfaceRegion is a spatial thing that has extent in at least two dimensions, and either has no thickness (i.e. is a purely two-dimensional object) or has an insignificant thickness compared to its length and width.  (If it is a closed surface, e.g. an apple skin, then any significant subregion of it must have insignificant thickness compared to that subregion's length and width.)  In sum, a surface region might be two- or three-dimensional; tangible or intangible; simply-connected or not; it might be flat, curved, folded, or crumpled.  \n<p>\nOther instances of #$SurfaceRegion are the skin of a basketball, the face-up side of a table top, and a particular face of a cube-shaped space region.  Other specializations are this collection include #$FlatSurfaceRegion, #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded, and #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6f0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6f0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6f0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6f0,14000).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6f0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6f0,[]).
x_comment(x_ConvexHullFn,"A unary function that takes a given two- or three-dimensional spatial thing to its intangible \"convex hull\".  Let <b>HULL</b> = (#$ConvexHullFn <b>OBJECT</b>).  If <b>OBJECT</b> is a #$TwoDimensionalThing, then <b>HULL</b> is the entire one-dimensional boundary (see #$ClosedLinearObject) of the minimal convex 2-D space region that #$spatiallySubsumes <b>OBJECT</b> (see #$ConvexSpaceRegion and #$Surface).  If <b>OBJECT</b> is a #$ThreeDimensionalThing, then <b>HULL</b> is the entire closed two-dimensional surface (see #$ClosedSurfaceRegion) of the minimal convex 3-D space region that subsumes <b>OBJECT</b>.  \n<p>\nNote that the dimensionality of <b>HULL</b> is always one less than that of <b>OBJECT</b>.  Also note that, while <b>OBJECT</b> might or might not be a #$SpatiallyContinuousThing, <b>HULL</b> is spatially-continuous in either case.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$convexHullOf.  To represent the whole convex space region bounded by a given object's convex hull, use #$MinimalSubsumingConvexSpaceRegionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6f1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6f1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6f1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6f1,14003).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6f1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6f1,[]).
x_comment(x_SpatiallySemiBoundedThing,"A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected (i.e. one-piece) spatial things that have a boundary, but are not fully-bounded geometrically.  In the case of a line, this means having exactly one end-point (see #$LinearObject-SemiBounded).  In the case of a surface, this means having a perimeter (or edge), but not a closed perimeter (see #$SurfaceRegion-SemiBounded).  In the case of a solid, this means having a surface, but not a closed surface (e.g. an infinitely long beam). \n<p>\nCf. #$SpatiallyBoundedThing and #$SpatiallyUnboundedThing.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6f2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6f2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6f2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
