xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6f3,[]).
x_comment(x_SpatiallyBoundedThing,"A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected (i.e. one-piece) spatial things that are fully bounded geometrically.  In the case of a line, this means having two end-points (see #$LinearObject-Bounded).  In the case of a surface, this means having a perimeter or hole (see #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded).  In the case of a solid, this means having a closed external surface or an internal cavity.\n<p>\nSince every known tangible body is spatially-bounded, the intersection of #$PartiallyTangible and #$SpatiallyContinuousThing is asserted to be a specialization of #$SpatiallyBoundedThing in the #$NaivePhysicsMt. It is not asserted in any \"top\" microtheory (such as the #$UniversalVocabularyMt), however, so as not to rule out <i>a priori</i> the possibility of a plenum universe (which is what the ancient Greek philosopher #$Parmenides, for one, believed of the actual universe).\n<p>\nCf. #$SpatiallyUnboundedThing and #$SpatiallySemiBoundedThing.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6f4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab6f4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab6f4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab6f4,14012).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab6f4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab6f4,[]).
x_comment(x_SpatiallyUnboundedThing,"A specialization of #$SpatiallyContinuousThing (q.v.).  This is the collection of self-connected (i.e. one-piece) spatial things that have no geometrical boundary.  In the case of a line, this means having no end-points (see #$LinearObject-Unbounded).  In the case of a surface, this means having no perimeter, edges, or holes (see #$SurfaceRegion-Unbounded).  In the case of a solid, this means having no (internal or external) surface whatsoever -- which effectively means being hole-free and infinitely extended in all directions.\n<p>\nCf. #$SpatiallyBoundedThing and #$SpatiallySemiBoundedThing.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab6f5).
