x_comment(x_PerimeterFn,"A unary function that takes a geometrically bounded surface (see #$SurfaceRegion-Bounded) to the closed curve (see #$ClosedLinearObject), or the mereological sum of such curves, that is the former's perimeter.  If <b>OBJ</b> is a self-connected (see #$SpatiallyContinuousThing) open surface with no holes in it, then (#$PerimeterFn <b>OBJ</b>) is a single #$ClosedLinearObject.  More generally, if <b>OBJ</b> is an open surface consisting of <b>M</b> (<b>M >= 1</b>) self-connected and pairwise-separated surfaces, and having a total of <b>N</b> (<b>N >= 0</b>) holes in it, then (#$PerimeterFn <b>OBJ</b>) is the mereological sum of <b>M+N</b> pairwise-separated closed curves.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$PerimeterFn #$Kansas-State) is a single closed curve, while (#$PerimeterFn #$Italy) is the sum of several such curves (e.g. the perimeter of the mainland, the perimeter of #$Sicily, and the border between Rome and #$VaticanCity).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab71a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab71a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab71a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab71a,14069).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab71a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab71a,[]).
