x_comment(x_stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions,"A binary #$SpaceRegionPredicate and specialization of #$connectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).  <code>(#$stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that (i) <code>REGION1</code> is connected with <code>REGION2</code> and (ii) there exists a part (<code>PART1</code>) of <code>REGION1</code> and a part (<code>PART2</code>) of <code>REGION2</code> such that the convex region of the spatial sum of <code>PART1</code> and <code>PART2</code> is a part of the spatial sum of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code>; i.e.\n<pre>\n  (#$partOfSpaceRegion \n    (#$ConvexSpaceRegionFn (#$SpaceRegionSumFn PART1 PART2))\n    (#$SpaceRegionSumFn REGION1 REGION2))\n</pre>\nIntuitively, this means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are connected by a \"convex channel\" (of the same dimensionality as they are) that is entirely part of their spatial sum.  \n<p>\nNote that if <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are <code>N</code>-dimensional, then they are strongly-connected if and only if the border that they share is <code>N-1</code>-dimensional.  Thus, two 3-D cube-shaped regions with a common 2-D surface are strongly-connected; but if all they have in common is a (1-D) edge or (0-D) corner they are weakly-connected (cf. #$weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions).  Two 2-D polygon-shaped regions with a common edge are strongly-connected; but if they only have a common vertex they are weakly-connected.  \n<p>\nSee also #$StronglyConnectedSpaceRegion.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab72f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab72f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab72f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab72f,14096).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab72f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab72f,[]).
x_comment(x_weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions,"An irreflexive and symmetric #$SpaceRegionPredicate (q.v.) that is a specialization of #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.).  <code>(#$weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are space regions (see #$HomogeneousPositiveDimensionalSpaceRegions) of the same dimensionality that are externally-connected but <i>not</i> strongly-connected (cf. #$stronglyConnectedSpaceRegions). Intuitively, this means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are <i>not</i> connected by any \"convex channel\" (of the same dimensionality as they are) that is entirely part of their spatial sum.  \n<p>\nNote that if <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are #$externallyConnectedSpaceRegions (q.v.) that are <code>N</code>-dimensional, then they are weakly-connected if and only if the border that they share is no more than <code>N-2</code>-dimensional.  Thus, two 3-D cube-shaped regions with a common 2-D surface are strongly-connected; but if all they have in common is a (1-D) edge or (0-D) corner they are weakly-connected (cf. #$weaklyConnectedSpaceRegions).  Two 2-D polygon-shaped regions with a common edge are strongly-connected; but if they only have a common vertex they are weakly-connected.  \n<p>\nFor example, the space regions occupied by adjacent black squares on a (geometrically ideal) checkerboard are weakly-connected.  Conversely, the space regions occupied by a black square and an adjacent red square are not weakly, but strongly, connected.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab730).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab730,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab730,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab730,14107).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab730,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab730,[]).
x_comment('x_SpaceChunk-Empirical',"A specialization of #$SpaceRegion-Empirical and #$SpaceChunk (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$SpaceChunk-Empirical is a three-dimensional portion of the intangible space of the empirical universe (see #$TheSpatialUniverse-Empirical).  This is the kind of space that physical objects occupy.\n<p>\nSee also #$Surface-Empirical, #$LineSegment-Empirical, and #$Point-Empirical.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab731).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab731,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
