xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab737,[]).
x_comment(x_Point,"A specialization of #$HomogeneousSpaceRegion and #$ZeroDimensionalThing (qq.v.).  This is the collection of points that have a fixed location with respect to some coordinate system or frame of reference.  \nSpace points can be used to indicate the locations (or partial locations) of things in space (see #$locatedAtPoint-Spatial).\n<p>\nAn important specialization of this collection is #$Point-Empirical (q.v.), whose instances have fixed locations with respect to some coordinate system or frame of reference in the empirically-observable universe, and thus are embedded in time.  An example of a space-point <i>not</i> in the empirical universe is a point in some abstract Euclidean space of the sort studied in geometry.\n<p>\nSee also #$Line, #$Surface, and #$SpaceChunk.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab738).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab738,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab738,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab738,14125).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab738,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab738,[]).
x_comment(x_ZeroDimensionalThing,"A #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions and a specialization of #$SpatialThing and #$IntangibleIndividual.  Each instance of #$ZeroDimensionalThing is either a single #$Point (q.v.) or an arrangement of disconnected points in space.  Zero-dimensional objects have no length or breadth or height, nor do they have parts.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab739).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab739,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab739,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab739,14131).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab739,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab739,[]).
x_comment(x_GeometricallyDescribableThing,"A subcollection of #$SpatialThing. Each instance of #$GeometricallyDescribableThing is a spatially-connected spatial thing (of one, two, three, or four dimensions) that either (i) has or approximates a simple geometric shape (e.g. it is a #$Line or a #$Hemisphere) or (ii) consists of a small number of parts in a relatively simple (and stable) simple geometric configuration, where each such part has or approximates a simple geometric shape (e.g. a table consisting of a 3-D-disc-shaped top and four cylindrical legs).  A geometrically-describable thing might be tangible or intangible. \n<p>\nNote that what counts as \"approximating\" a given simple geometric shape -- and thus what spatial things count as #$GeometricallyDescribableThings -- varies with context. In a context that was so fine-grained shape-wise that even the shapes of the individual molecules on the surface of an object were considered relevant to the object's shape, perhaps nearly every (connected, solid) tangible object would be geometrically-describable.  In more everyday contexts, on the other hand, an unopened can of soup would be geometrically-describable (as a cylinder), while a telephone or an animal's body would probably not.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab73a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab73a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab73a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab73a,14132).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab73a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab73a,[]).
x_comment(x_ShapedObject,"An #$ObjectType and a specialization of #$PositiveDimensionalThing (q.v.).  Each instance of #$ShapedObject is a spatial object that has some well-defined shape (though what counts as a \"well-defined shape\" can vary from context to context); see #$objectShapeType.  Specializations of #$ShapedObject include #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing and #$AnimalShapedThing.  Contrast with #$AmorphousThing.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab73b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab73b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab73b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab73b,14135).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab73b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab73b,[]).
x_comment(x_SheetShapedObject,"A specilization of #$ShapedObject and #$SolidTangibleThing.  Each instance of #$SheetShapedObject is an object that is flat and spread out, planar, and neither fully crumpled nor compactly folded.  Such an object has two opposing surfaces that are both #$FlatSurfaceRegions (q.v.); its other surfaces have much smaller area than the two main flat surfaces.   Thus, one of the three orthogonal dimensions (length, width or height) of a sheet-shaped object is much smaller than the other two.  \n<p>\nExamples of #$SheetShapedObject include a layer of ice over a pond and a piece of plywood.  A bedspread is a sheet-shaped object only if it is not folded into a small volume or crumpled.  (See #$CurrentShapeVsIntrinsicShape.)  \n<p>\nFor describing a sheet of something (e.g. waxed paper, aluminum foil, cloth) regardless of its current shape, use #$SheetOfSomeStuff.  For \"one-sided\" surfaces, use a specialization of #$SurfaceRegion.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab73c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab73c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab73c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab73c,14136).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab73c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab73c,[]).
x_comment(x_TwoDimensionalGeometricThing,"A specialization of #$Surface, #$GeometricallyDescribableThing, and #$ShapedObject (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$TwoDimensionalGeometricThing is an intangible, two-dimensional, self-connected object whose shape is describable in (relatively simple) geometric terms.  Instances include planes, as well as all (planar or \"curved\") polygons and elliptical regions.  For bounded two-dimensional geometric objects that <i>can</i> be embedded in a #$Plane, see the specialization #$PlaneFigure.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab73d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab73d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab73d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab73d,14141).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab73d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab73d,[]).
x_comment(x_SpaceRegionPredicate,"A specialization of both #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and #$IntangibleObjectRelatingPredicate (qq.v.).  This is the collection of relative-location predicates that are used to relate only pure space regions.  Thus, any #$argIsa constraint on a #$SpaceRegionPredicate should be to #$SpaceRegion or a specialization thereof.\n<p>\nA notable specialization of this collection is #$RCCBinaryPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab73e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab73e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
