x_comment(x_reflexiveInArgs,"A #$MetaPredicate that is used to state that a given predicate is 'reflexive' in a certain sense with respect to two of its argument-places.   <code>(#$reflexiveInArgs PRED M N)</code> means that whenever <code>PRED</code> holds with a given thing in its <code>M</code>th (or <code>N</code>th) argument-place, then it also holds with that same thing in <i>both</i> its <code>M</code>th <i>and</i> <code>N</code>th argument-places (keeping all the other arguments fixed).\n<p>\nMore precisely, <code>(#$reflexiveInArgs PRED M N)</code> means that if <code>PRED</code> holds of a given sequence of arguments <code>ARGS</code>, then it also holds of both (i) the sequence obtained by replacing the <code>N</code>th item in <code>ARGS</code> with a (repeat occurrence) of the <code>M</code>th item and (ii) the sequence obtained by replacing the <code>M</code>th item in <code>ARGS</code> with a (repeat occurrence) of the <code>N</code>th item.  Put schematically (and supposing without loss of generality that <code>M<N)</code>, if this holds:\n<p>\n(1)     <code>(PRED ... THING<sub>M</sub> ... THING<sub>N</sub> ...)</code>\n<p>\nthen so do both of these:\n<p>\n(2a)        <code>(PRED ... THING<sub>M</sub> ... THING<sub>M</sub> ...)</code>\n<p>\n(2b)        <code>(PRED ... THING<sub>N</sub> ... THING<sub>N</sub> ...)</code>\n<p>\nwhere the argument-sequence in (2a) is just like that in (1) except for having <code>THING<sub>M</sub></code> as its <code>M</code>th <i>and</i> <code>N</code>th items (and analogously for (2b) with respect to <code>THING<sub>N</sub>)</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, a ternary predicate called <i><code>compatriotsOf</code></i> that took as its arguments two persons (<i>not</i> constrained to be distinct) and a geopolitical entity would be reflexive in its first and second arguments.\n<p>\nEvery #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate (q.v.) is of course reflexive in its (only) two argument-places.  But note that the converse is not true: a binary predicate <code>BINPRED</code> such that <code>(#$reflexiveInArgs BINPRED 1 2)</code> holds might not be fully reflexive.  For the reflexive-in-args property only entails that if a thing <code>THING</code> stands in the <code>BINPRED</code> relation to something (in either argument-place) then <code>(BINPRED THING THING)</code> holds, while a #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate relates <i>everything</i> (that satisfies the predicate's argument-type constraints) to itself.  Thus, a binary predicate <i><code>attendsTheSameChurchAs</code></i> would be #$reflexiveInArgs in its two argument-places, but not a #$ReflexiveBinaryPredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab7bc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab7bc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab7bc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab7bc,14536).
