x_comment(x_slopeOfRegion,"A #$NumericIntervalSlot that relates a geographical region to its amount of slope, typically given as a percentage. <code>(#$slopeOfRegion REGION (#$Percent NUM))</code> means that <code>REGION</code> has a slope of <code>NUM</code> percent (or <code>NUM</code> /100).  For example, if <code>REGION</code> has a slope of (#$Percent 10) then, when moving up (down) the slope, an object gains (loses) 10 units of vertical distance (elevation) for every 100 units of horizontal distance travelled.  A slope of 100% corresponds to a 1:1 ratio of vertical-over-horizontal distance, or a 45-degree angle.  A vertical (90-degree) cliff has an infinite slope.\n<p>\nThe formula for converting an angle of <code>N</code> degrees (measured from the horizontal) to a slope is \n<pre>\n  SLOPE = (#$TangentFn (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure N)) \n</pre>\nTo express <code>SLOPE</code> as a percentage, multiply it by 100 and apply the #$Percent function to the result:\n<pre>\n  (#$Percent (#$TimesFn SLOPE 100)) .\n</pre>\nNote that if <code>REGION</code> slopes in <i>two</i> directions with respect to the horizontal plane, #$slopeOfRegion relates it to whichever slope is the greatest.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab7d8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab7d8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab7d8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab7d8,14614).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab7d8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab7d8,[]).
