x_comment(x_ConcatenateStringsFn,"A variable-arity #$IndividualDenotingFunction that takes one or more abstract character-strings and (in effect) concatenates them. For any instances <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> of #$CharacterString, (#$ConcatenateStringsFn <code>STRING1</code> ... <code>STRING<sub>n</sub>)</code> is the character-string that is obtained by concatenating  <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> end-to-end in the order from 1 to n.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$ConcatenateStringsFn \"B\" \"o\" \"b\") is the string \"Bob\", and (#$ConcatenateStringsFn \"Bob\" \" \" \"Dy\" \"lan\") is the string \"Bob Dylan\".\n<p>\nNote that some (or all) of <code>STRING1</code>, ..., <code>STRING<sub>n</sub></code> might be identical.  Also note that, when applied to a single argument, this function simply returns that argument unchanged; e.g. (#$ConcatenateStringsFn \"Bob\") = \"Bob\".\n<p>\n#$ConcatenateStringsFn is the restriction of #$JoinListsFn (which performs the same operation on #$Lists generally) to the realm of character-strings.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab83f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab83f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab83f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab83f,14857).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab83f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab83f,[]).
x_comment(x_CycCreationTemplate,"The collection of groups of CycLTerm that, together, form a template for the creation of constants and the generation of assertions needed for some application.  For example, consider a (fictional) SUBL function call (CREATE-USER-AND-MT \"user\") that creates two terms, User001 and Microtheory001, and three assertions, (isa User001 UserOfThisApplication), (isa Microtheory001 Microtheory) and (userApplicationMicrotheory User001 Microtheory001).  The creation template for CREATE-USER-AND-MT (see #$creationTemplateForSubLFunction) consists of an exemplar instance of #$UserOfThisApplication and #$Microtheory, as well as three assertions isomorphic with the assertions on User001 and Microtheory001 described above.  When CREATE-USER-AND-MT is called, the relevant creation template can be drawn upon to check for failure, or to optimize performance by restricting forward inference at creation time to only those rules that are known to succeed (by determining which rules successfully fired forward on FORTs associated with the template via #$creationTemplateFORTs (q.v.)), or to those rules explicitly associated with the template using #$creationTemplateAllowableRules.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab840).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab840,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab840,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
