x_comment(x_ThePartition,"A variable-arity partial #$SetDenotingFunction that is a specialization (via #$genlFunctions) of #$TheCovering (q.v.), and is used to specify a set of two or more collections or mathematical sets that partition some set or collection (see #$partitionedInto).  For example, the fact that the collection #$Whale is partitioned into the collections #$BaleenWhale and #$ToothedWhale can be expressed as '(#$partitionedInto #$Whale (#$ThePartition #$BaleenWhale #$ToothedWhale))'.\n<p>\nIf <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b> (<b>n</b>>=2) are pairwise-disjoint #$SetOrCollections, (#$ThePartition <b>SOC<sub>1</sub> ... SOCn</b>) is the set (see #$Set-Mathematical) whose elements are exactly <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b>.  If <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b> are not all mutually disjoint, then the function returns no value and '(#$ThePartition <b>SOC<sub>1</sub> ... SOCn</b>)' is #$undefined (q.v.).\n<p>\nNote that #$ThePartition is simply a restriction of the function #$TheSet (q.v.) to argument-sequences of length two or greater whose members are pairwise-disjoint (see #$disjointWith) sets or collections.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab867).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab867,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab867,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab867,14937).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab867,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab867,[]).
x_comment(x_TheCovering,"A variable-arity #$SetDenotingFunction that is a specialization (via #$genlFunctions) of #$TheSet (q.v.), and is used to specify a set of two or more collections or mathematical sets that cover some set or collection (see #$covering).  For example, the fact that the collection #$CreationOrDestructionEvent is covered by the collections #$CreationEvent and #$DestructionEvent can be expressed as '(#$covering #$CreationOrDestructionEvent (#$TheCovering #$CreationEvent #$DestructionEvent))'.\n<p>\nIf <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b> (<b>n</b>>=2) are #$SetOrCollections, (#$TheCovering <b>SOC<sub>1</sub> ... SOCn</b>) is the set (see #$Set-Mathematical) whose elements are exactly <b>SOC<sub>1</sub></b>, ..., <b>SOCn</b>.\n<p>\nNote that #$TheCovering is simply a restriction of the function #$TheSet (q.v.) to arguments that are #$SetOrCollections and to a minimum arity (see #$arityMin) of 2.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab868).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab868,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
