x_comment(x_objectShapeType,"A #$ShapePredicate (q.v.) and \"restricted specialization\" (see #$genlPredsWRTTypes)  of #$isa that relates a spatial object to a collection of things having (at least approximately) the same shape (see #$SpatialThingTypeByShape).  <code>(#$objectShapeType OBJECT SHAPETYPE)</code> means that <code>SHAPETYPE</code> characterizes <code>OBJECT</code>'s shape (at some level of generality).  If <code>SHAPETYPE</code> is a collection of things all having the same dimensionality as <code>OBJECT</code> (see #$SpatialThingTypeByNumberOfDimensions), <code>OBJECT</code> might actually be an instance of <code>SHAPETYPE</code>; but if \n<code>SHAPETYPE</code> is a collection of things all having the a different dimensionality than <code>OBJECT</code>, <code>OBJECT</code> can only approximate the shape in question. \n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$objectShapeType MyCrystalBall #$SphericalSolid)</code> holds because my crystal ball <i>is</i> a solid sphere.  On the other hand <code>(#$objectShapeType MyMousePad #$Rectangle)</code>, while true, only means that my mouse pad is <i>roughly</i> rectangular in shape, as a three-dimensional object like a mousepad can't literally have a two-dimensional shape.\n<p>\nWhen making a ground-atomic assertion to the Knowledge Base regarding a particular object's shape it is normally advisable to use the most specific applicable #$SpatialThingTypeByShape available.  But note that the predicate #$objectShapeType itself implies no such thing; a more-inclusive shape-type will always characterize an object whenever a (correlatively) less-inclusive shape-type does.  Thus, <code>(#$objectShapeType MyMousePad #$Polygon)</code> holds if the second example above does.\n<p>\nSee also the nearly corresponding type-level function #$CommonShapeFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab86d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab86d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab86d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab86d,14951).
