x_comment(x_RectangleTypeFn,"A #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that takes a pair of positive distances (see #$Distance-Absolute) to the collection of all #$Rectangles having those size dimensions.  (#$RectangleTypeFn <b>DIST1 DIST2</b>) is the collection of all and only those rectangles whose size dimensions are <b>DIST1</b> by <b>DIST2</b>.\n<p>\nFor example, the two-dimensional top surface of an 8'' x 10'' photograph is an instance of (#$RectangleTypeFn (#$Inch 8) (#$Inch 10)).\n<p>\nNote that this function is a #$CommutativeRelation, and is thus indifferent to the order of its arguments.  Neither of its argument-places, of course, has an intrinsic correlation to the highly context-dependent notions of the \"length\" or \"width\" of a given rectangle.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8a5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8a5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8a5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8a5,15080).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab8a5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab8a5,[]).
x_comment(x_canContainShapesOfSize,"A #$PhysicalFeatureBinaryPredicate that is used for indicating the maximal size of a thing (see #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize) that can fit inside a given tangible object.  <code>(#$canContainShapesOfSize OBJ SHAPETYPE)</code> means that there is sufficient room (see #$FreeSpaceRegion) inside <code>OBJ</code> that an arbitrary instance of <code>SHAPETYPE</code> can fit there (see e.g. #$fitsInsideObject).  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$canContainShapesOfSize \n     <b>MyCarTrunk</b> \n     (#$RectangularSolidTypeFn (#$Meter 1) (#$Meter 0.5) (#$Meter 0.75))\n</pre>\nsays that my car's trunk can hold a three-dimensional rectangular object whose dimensions are 1-meter by 1/2-meter by 3/4-meter.  \n<p>\nNote that assertions like the above are the most informative when they come as close as possible to giving the <i>largest</i> size of thing that fits inside an object.  This can be facilitated by choosing the #$GeometricShapeTypeBySizeFunction that corresponds most closely to the shape of  the interior space in question.  For example, if the object of interest had been a soda can instead of a trunk, #$RightCylinderTypeFn would have been more appropriate to use than #$RectangularSolidTypeFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8a6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8a6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8a6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8a6,15085).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab8a6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab8a6,[]).
x_comment('x_spatiallySubsumes-TypeType',"A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate that is a type-level correlate of #$spatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  It is used to state that things of one given type always spatially subsume something of another given type.  <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType BIGTYPE SMALLTYPE)</code> means that, for every instance <code>BIG</code> of<code> BIGTYPE</code>, there is an instance <code>SMALL</code> of <code>SMALLTYPE</code> such that <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes BIG SMALL)</code> holds.\n<p>\nFor example, every 2'' x 4'' rectangle subsumes a 2'' x 3'' rectangle:\n<pre>\n  (#$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType\n    (#$RectangleTypeFn (#$Inch 2) (#$Inch 4))\n    (#$RectangleTypeFn (#$Inch 2) (#$Inch 3)))\n</pre>\nAn important specialization of #$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType is #$physicalPartTypes.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8a7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8a7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8a7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8a7,15094).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab8a7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab8a7,[]).
x_comment('x_spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType',"A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a #$SpatialThing to a type of spatial thing such that the former is spatially subsumed by something of that type.  <code>(#$spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType OBJ TYPE)</code> means that there is at least one instance <code>INST</code> of <code>TYPE</code> such that <code>(#$spatiallySubsumes INST OBJ)</code> is true.  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType #$CityOfAustinTX #$Country)\n</pre>\nmeans that Austin is subsumed by at least one country.\n<p>\nThis predicate is often used with a #$GeometricShapeTypeBySize (q.v.) as its second argument, in order to give an approximation of the external size of an object.  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$spatiallySubsumedBy-InsType \n    <b>TheHopeDiamond</b> \n    (#$RightCylinderTypeFn (#$Centi (#$Meter 10) (#$Centi (#$Meter 10)))\n</pre>\nentails that the Hope Diamond can fit inside a #$RightCylinder that is 10 centimeters high and 10 centimeters in diameter.  Its truth follows from the fact that (at any given moment) the Hope is indeed subsumed by a #$SpaceRegion of that shape and size.\n<p>\nSee also #$spatiallySubsumes-TypeType.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8a8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8a8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8a8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8a8,15103).
