x_comment(x_TernaryOrderingPredicate,"A collection of ternary predicates, each instance of which is a ternary, rule-macro predicate for describing an ordering of two elements in a context in which that ordering holds.  A notable example is #$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType, which relates a situation type to two proper sub-situation-types that stand in a quasi-temporal order in the context of that situation type (specifically, in any instance of that situation type, there will be instances of the specified sub-situation-types that stand in the #$startsAfterStartingOf relation.  Every instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate is transitive in two of its arguments (the arguments where the ordered elements occur), which is specified using #$transitiveInArgs.  The context-argument is specified using #$orderingContextArg.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8fd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8fd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8fd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8fd,15262).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab8fd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab8fd,[]).
x_comment(x_expressionPegDefiniteness,"An instance of #$StrictlyFunctionalSlot.  (#$expressionPegDefiniteness PEG DEFINITENESS) identifies DEFINITEESS as the instance of #$ExpressionTypeByFamiliarity of which the instance PEG of #$NominalExpressionPeg is an instance.  For example, (#$expressionPegDefiniteness ADog-Peg #$IndefiniteExpression) means that the phrase that introduced ADog-Peg (presumably \"a dog\") was indefinite (had an indefinite determiner).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8fe).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8fe,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8fe,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8fe,15263).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab8fe,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab8fe,[]).
x_comment(x_orderingContextArg,"<code>(#$orderingContextArg PREDICATE ARG-NUM)</code> identifies <code>ARG-NUM</code> as the argument-place for the context against which the other arguments related by <code>PREDICATE</code> are assigned an order.  <p>For example, \n<pre>     (#$orderingContextArg #$subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork 3)</pre> means that in <pre>     (#$subWorkPriorToSubWorkInWork WORK-1 WORK-2 WORK-3)</pre> <code>WORK-1</code> and <code>WORK-2</code> are ordered in the context of <code>WORK-3</code> (in this case, <code>WORK-1</code> \"comes before\" <code>WORK-2</code> in <code>WORK-3</code>.  Similarly, <pre>     (#$orderingContextArg #$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType 1)</pre> means that in <pre>     (#$startsAfterStartOfInSituationType SIT-TYPE-1 SIT-TYPE-2 SIT-TYPE-3)</pre> <code>SIT-TYPE-2</code> and <code>SIT-TYPE-3</code> are assigned an order in the context of <code>SIT-TYPE-1</code> (in this case, <code>SIT-TYPE-2</code> is a \"stage\" that starts after the start of the stage <code>SIT-TYPE-3</code> in the larger script or process <code>SIT-TYPE-1</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab8ff).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab8ff,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab8ff,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab8ff,15264).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab8ff,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab8ff,[]).
x_comment(x_assertedSentence,"A #$KBDependentRelation (q.v.) and specialization of #$knownSentence (q.v.) that is used to state that a given #$CycLSentence-Assertible has been asserted in the KB (in some accessible #$Microtheory). More exactly, (#$assertedSentence SENT) is true in microtheory MT precisely when SENT canonicalizes into a set of clauses of the same form as the canonicalized form of some assertion in a microtheory accessible to MT. (The predicate is thus true both of assertions entered into the KB by hand and assertions deduced by Cyc from forward rules.) For the more specific claim that SENT is asserted <b>in MT</b>, see the predicate #$ist-Asserted.\n<p>\nThis predicate enables Cyc to select a subset of information when answering queries, filtering out more generic (inferrable-but-not-asserted) information. Thus, for example, the query:\n<p>\n(#$genls #$Dog #$Thing)\n<p>\nwill return #$True (in appropriate microtheories), whereas:\n<p>\n(#$assertedSentence (#$genls #$Dog #$Thing))\n<p>\nwill not.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab900).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab900,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab900,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab900,15266).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab900,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab900,[]).
x_comment(x_tokenizationTokenizer,"(#$tokenizationTokenizer TOKENIZATION TOKENIZER) means that the TOKENIZATION was created by the software agent TOKENIZER.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab901).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab901,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab901,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab901,15277).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab901,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab901,[]).
x_comment(x_creationTemplateAllowsAllRulesFromMt,"<code>(#$creationTemplateAllowableRules TEMPLATE MT)</code> means that for any value of <code>RULE</code> such that <code>(#$quotedIsa RULE #$CycLRuleAssertion)</code> and <code>(#$assertionMt RULE MT)</code>, <code>RULE</code> may be considered when calling the code-level function associated with <code>TEMPLATE</code>, an instance of #$CycCreationTemplate.  Thus #$creationTemplateAllowsAllRulesFromMt allows one to specify allowable rules (see #$creationTemplateAllowableRules) for a template at the level of an entire microtheory.  For more information about how creation templates are used, see the #$comment on\n\n#$CycCreationTemplate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab902).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab902,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab902,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab902,15278).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab902,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab902,[]).
x_comment(x_programUsesCycCreationTemplate,"<code>(#$programUsesCycCreationTemplate PROGRAM TEMPLATE)</code> means that <code>PROGRAM</code>, an instance of #$CycRelatedProgram, makes use of <code>TEMPLATE</code>, an instance of #$CycCreationTemplate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab903).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab903,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab903,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab903,15281).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab903,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab903,[]).
x_comment(x_typePrimaryFunction,"A #$FirstOrderCollectionPredicate and a specialization of #$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable (q.v.) that is used to represent the <i>primary</i> use associated with some type of object (usually instances of #$PhysicalDevice but also instances of #$Organization, or anything           else that can be said to have a function) as a consequence of the <i>intent</i> with which that type of object was designed. <code>(#$typePrimaryFunction OBJ-TYPE SIT-TYPE ROLE)</code> means that instances of <code>OBJ-TYPE</code> are primarily intended to participate in situations of type <code>SIT-TYPE</code> in the instance of #$Role <code>ROLE</code>.  For example, the primary use associated with any instance of #$ElectricalToaster is making toast: we can represent this by (#$typePrimaryFunction #$ElectricalToaster #$MakingToast #$deviceUsed). As another example, the primary use associated with a government intelligence organization is performing instances of #$IntelligenceAnalysisProcess.           We can represent this as (#$typePrimaryFunction #$GovernmentIntelligenceOrganization #$IntelligenceAnalysisProcess #$performedBy).\n<p>\nNote that this predicate should be used to represent only the very specific <code>SIT-TYPE</code> in which something is intended to function in some role.  For example, in most contexts, while it is correct to assert (#$typePrimaryFunction #$Telephone #$AudioCommunicating #$deviceUsed), it is not correct to assert (#$typePrimaryFunction #$Telephone #$Communicating #$deviceUsed) since #$Communicating is not specific enough. Instead of the latter sentence, (#$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable #$Telephone #$Communicating #$deviceUsed) is preferable.  When asserting a #$typePrimaryFunction GAF, use the most specific #$Situation <code>SIT-TYPE</code> available in the microtheory in which you are making the assertion, as long as you do not believe <code>SIT-TYPE</code> is too specific.  If no #$Situation specific enough is available, consider creating one.\n<p>\nThis predicate presupposes that instances of the first argument have the function that they do in virtue of the <i>design</i> of some intelligent agent. If the \"functionality\" in question does not obviously derive from some agent's design or artifice, #$typePrimaryFunction-AgnosticIntentional (q.v.) should be used instead. See also (#$TypeCapableFn #$behaviorCapable), #$typeIntendedBehaviorCapable and #$primaryFunction.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab904).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab904,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab904,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
