x_comment('x_Company-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization. #$Company-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a company consists of between 60 and 190 soldiers, and is typically made up of three to five platoons (see #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon).  Two to five companies typically make up an army #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon.\n<p>\nIn the US Marine Corps, a company consists of roughly 240 marines, and is typically made up of four platoons.  Five companies typically make up a marine #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab93e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab93e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab93e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab93e,15368).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab93e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab93e,[]).
x_comment('x_Battalion-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization. #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a battalion consists of between 300 and 1,000 soldiers, and is typically made up of four to six companies (see #$Company-MilitaryEchelon).  Two to five battalions typically make up an army #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon.\n<p>\nIn the US Marine Corps, a battalion consists of roughly 1,200 marines, and is typically made up of five companies.  Five battalions typically make up a marine #$Regiment-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab93f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab93f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab93f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab93f,15373).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab93f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab93f,[]).
x_comment('x_Platoon-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a platoon consists of between 16 and 44 soldiers, and is typically made up of two to four squads (see #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon) or sections (see #$Section-MilitaryOrganization).  Three to five platoons typically make up an army #$Company-MilitaryEchelon.\n<p>\nIn the US Marine Corps, a platoon consists of roughly 60 marines, and is typically made up of four squads.  Four platoons typically make up a marine #$Company-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab940).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab940,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab940,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab940,15378).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab940,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab940,[]).
x_comment('x_Squad-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of both #$USArmyEchelon and #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a squad consists of between 9 and 10 soldiers, and is the smallest type of unit in the Army force structure.  Two to four squads typically make up an army #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon.\n<p>\nIn the US Marine Corps, a squad consists of roughly 15 marines, and is typically made up of three fire teams (see #$FireTeam-MilitaryEchelon).  Four squads typically make up a marine #$Platoon-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab941).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab941,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab941,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab941,15383).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab941,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab941,[]).
x_comment('x_FireTeam-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USMarineCorpsEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Marine Corps, a fire team is a small, task-oriented unit consisting of roughly 5 marines.  Three fire teams typically make up a marine #$Squad-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab942).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab942,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab942,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab942,15388).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab942,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab942,[]).
x_comment('x_Regiment-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Regiment-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USMarineCorpsEchelon and #$USArmyEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Marine Corps, a regiment consists of roughly 6,000 marines, and is typically made up of five battalions (see #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon)\n<p>\nIn the US Army, certain brigade-sized units (see #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon) are sometimes called \"regiments\".") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab943).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab943,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab943,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab943,15391).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab943,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab943,[]).
x_comment('x_Brigade-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon is an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a brigade is a special-purpose unit consisting of between 3,000 and 5,000 soldiers, and is typically made up of two to five battalions (see #$Battalion-MilitaryEchelon).  Three brigades (or brigade-sized units) typically make up an army #$Division-MilitaryEchelon.  An army brigade is usually formed to fulfill a specific objective, and is commanded by a brigadier general.  \n<p>\nNote that certain brigade-sized US army units are called \"regiments\" (see #$Regiment-MilitaryEchelon).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab944).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab944,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab944,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab944,15396).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab944,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab944,[]).
x_comment('x_Division-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Division-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a division consists of between 10,000 and 15,000 soldiers, and is typically made up of three brigade-sized units (see #$Brigade-MilitaryEchelon).  Two to five divisions typically make up an army #$Corps-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab945).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab945,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab945,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab945,15401).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab945,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab945,[]).
x_comment('x_Corps-MilitaryEchelon',"A #$MilitaryUnitTypeByEchelon and a subcollection of #$ModernMilitaryOrganization.  #$Corps-MilitaryEchelon is and an instance of #$USArmyEchelon.  \n<p>\nIn the US Army, a corps consists of between 20,000 and 45,000 soldiers, is typically made up of two to five divisions (see #$Division-MilitaryEchelon), and is commanded by a lieutenant general.  Two or more corps typically make up an #$Army-MilitaryEchelon.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab946).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab946,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
