x_comment(x_centroidOfObject,"A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate.  <code>(#$centroidOfObject OBJ CENTROID)</code> means that <code>CENTROID</code> is the centroid of <code>OBJ</code>.  The <i>centroid</i> of an object is its unweighted center of mass.  The centroid of an object is not guaranteed to be part of, or on, the object.  For instance, the centroid of a perfect sphere is the center of the sphere, which is not on the sphere itself.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab97e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab97e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab97e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab97e,15502).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab97e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab97e,[]).
x_comment(x_AxisymmetricObject,"A specialization of #$SpatialThing.  Each instance of #$AxisymmetricObject is a (tangible or intangible) spatial thing that is symmetric with respect to an axis.  Such an axis, which is a #$Line-Straight, is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> (see #$symmetricAxes) of the spatial thing.  An axially symmetric object might be a #$BilaterallySymmetricObject or a #$RadiallySymmetricObject (qq.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab97f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab97f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab97f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab97f,15503).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab97f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab97f,[]).
x_comment(x_bilateralAxes,"A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a tangible object with at least one well-defined pair of opposing sides (see #$BilateralObject) to an axis it has with respect to some such pair of sides.  <code>(#$bilateralAxes OBJECT AXIS)</code> means that <code>AXIS</code> is a #$Line-Straight that passes through the respective centers (see #$centroidOfObject) of each of two opposing sides (i.e. the front-back, left-right, or top-bottom sides) of <code>OBJECT</code>.\n<p>\nCf. #$symmetricAxes.  Note that a bilateral axis of an object might or might not also be a symmetric axis of it.  For example, the left-right axis of a bicycle wheel is also an axis of symmetry of the wheel, but the top-bottom axis of a frying pan is not an axis of symmetry.\n<p>\nSee also #$objectSides and #$BilateralAxisFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab980).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab980,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab980,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab980,15504).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab980,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab980,[]).
x_comment(x_objectAxes,"A binary #$RelativeLocationalPredicate that relates a (tangible or intangible) spatial thing to an intrinsic axis (if it has any).  By an \"intrinsic\" here we mean an axis an object has either purely in virtue of its geometric shape or in virtue of some natural or conventional feature it has regarding its directional orientation.  More precisely, <code>(#$objectAxes OBJECT AXIS)</code> means that either (i) <code>AXIS</code> is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> of <code>OBJECT</code> (see the specialization #$symmetricAxes) or (ii) <code>OBJECT</code> is an #$BilateralObject (q.v.) and <code>AXIS</code> passes through the centers (see #$centroidOfObject) of two if its opposing sides (see the specialization #$bilateralAxes).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab981).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab981,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab981,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab981,15509).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab981,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab981,[]).
x_comment(x_objectTopSide,"(#$objectTopSide OBJECT TOPSIDE) means that TOPSIDE is the entire top surface part of OBJECT. See also #$TopAndBottomSidedObject and #$TopSide.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab982).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab982,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab982,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab982,15510).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab982,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab982,[]).
x_comment(x_BilateralAxisFn,"A ternary function that takes a #$BilateralObject and a pair of its opposing sides to the straight line that passes through the centers (see #$centroidOfObject) of those two sides.  See #$bilateralAxes.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$BilateralAxisFn MyCar (#$BackFn MyCar) (#$FrontFn MyCar)) is the back-front axis of my car.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab983).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab983,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab983,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab983,15511).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab983,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab983,[]).
x_comment(x_OwlExportableRelation,"This is the collection of relations that we currently are able to define translations for into OWL Full.  Usually these are higher arity \"macro\" predicates or functions that require a specific mapping from our ontology into OWL Full.  For instance, *#$affiliatedWith* is not an instance of this collection because that is a binary predicate that can be defined in the normal way in OWL.  On the other hand, *#$relationAllExists* is an instance because assertions made with that predicate require a special translation to be expressible in OWL.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab984).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab984,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab984,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab984,15514).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab984,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab984,[]).
x_comment(x_near,"This intentionally-vague predicate relates spatial things that are relatively near each other.  <code>(#$near THIS THAT)</code> means that the distance between <code>THIS</code> and <code>THAT</code> is such that -- given the situation at hand and the sorts of things that <code>THIS</code> and <code>THAT</code> are -- they would be considered near each other by most observers.  Note that an object is #$near itself, anything it touches, and any part of itself.  When appropriate, use a more precisely-defined predicate such as #$nearToDegree, #$nearRelativeTo, #$nearForRolesInEventType, #$nearnessForRolesInEventType, #$touches-Directly, or #$physicalParts.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab985).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab985,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab985,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab985,15515).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab985,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab985,[]).
x_comment(x_equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid,"A specialization of #$sizeParameterOfObject.  <code>(#$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half the length of <code>SPHEROID</code>'s equatorial axes.  \n<p>\nWhenever two #$symmetricAxes (q.v.) of a #$Spheroid that is <i>not</i> a perfect sphere are of equal length, they are both <i>equatorial axes</i> of the spheroid.  All of the equatorial axes of such a spheroid lie in a single plane; the (unique) symmetric-axis of the spheroid that is perpendicular with its equatorial axes is known as its <i>polar axis</i> (cf. #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).\n<p>\nIf a spheroid's equatorial radius is greater than its polar radius, it is an #$OblateSpheroid; if its polar radius is greater than its equatorial radius, it is a #$ProlateSpheroid.  The greater radius (whichever it might be) measures the spheroid's <i>semi-major axis</i> (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength).  The equatorial and polar radii of a #$Sphere are both equal to it ordinary #$radius.\n<p>\nNote that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>DISTANCE</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has length <code>DISTANCE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab986).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab986,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab986,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab986,15516).
