x_comment(x_Spheroid,"A #$TwoDimensionalShapeType and a specialization of #$Ellipsoid (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Spheroid is an ellipsoid that has two axes (see #$symmetricAxes) of equal length (if <i>all</i> of an ellipsoid's axes are of equal length, it is a #$Sphere).  With respect to a non-spherical spheroid, the axes that are of equal length are <i><b>equatorial axes</b></i>, and an axis that is perpendicular to an equatorial axis is a <i><b>polar axis</b></i>.  (Since a non-spherical spheroid's equatorial axes are coplanar, it therefore has a unique polar axis.) \n<p>\nA spheroid whose equatorial axes are longer than its polar axis is an #$OblateSpheroid, and one whose polar axis is longer than its equatorial axes is a #$ProlateSpheroid.  See also #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid and #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid. \n<p>\nNote that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>LENGTH</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has <code>LENGTH</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab987).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab987,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab987,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab987,15523).
