x_comment(x_spheroidSemiminorAxisLength,"A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot (q.v.) that is used to specify (partially) the size a #$Spheroid. <code>(#$spheroidSemiminorAxisLength SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half of the length of <code>SPHERIOID</code>'s minor axis.  The <i>minor axis</i> of a spheroid is by definition the shortest of its three principle axes.  For an #$OblateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-minor axis length is equal to its polar radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).  For a #$ProlateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-minor axis length is equal to its equatorial radius (see #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).\n<p>\nTogether, the semi-minor axis length and the semi-<i>major</i> axis length (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength) of a spheroid determine its <i>flattening</i> and  \n<i>inverse flattening</i> (see #$spheroidInverseFlattening). For example, if the  \nsemi-major and semi-minor axis lengths are <code>MAJ</code> and <code>MIN</code>, respectively, then the inverse flattening is <code>INVFLAT = MAJ/(MAJ-MIN)</code>.\n<p>\nNote that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>LENGTH</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has <code>LENGTH</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab996).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab996,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab996,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab996,15570).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab996,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab996,[]).
x_comment(x_disjointWith,"A #$TaxonomicSlot predicate that relates #$Collections  (q.v.) that have no instances in common. <code>(#$disjointWith COL1 COL2)</code> means that <code>COL1</code> is disjoint with <code>COL2</code>: nothing is an instance of both. That is, there is no <code>THING</code> such that both <code>(#$isa THING COL1)</code> and <code>(#$isa THING COL2)</code> hold. For example, <code>(#$disjointWith #$Herbivore #$Carnivore)</code> holds because no animal is both a herbivore and a carnivore. Note that #$disjointWith is <b>not</b> irreflexive (see #$IrreflexiveBinaryPredicate): it is possible for a collection to be disjoint with itself, though only if it is empty (i.e. has no instances). Thus, for example, <code>(#$disjointWith #$Nothing #$Nothing)</code> holds.  Cf. #$intersectsWith.\n<p>\nAlso note that it is quite possible for two second (or higher) order collections to be disjoint even if an instance of one collection and an instance of the other collection themselves share instances.  Consider, for example, the disjoint collections #$SpatialThingTypeByShape and #$SpatialThingTypeByDimensionality.  #$RoundObject is an instance of the first and #$ThreeDimensionalThing is an instance of the second; and any solid spherical object is an instance of both of these latter collections.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab997).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab997,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab997,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab997,15577).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab997,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab997,[]).
x_comment(x_spheroidInverseFlattening,"A #$ScalarIntervalSlot (q.v.) that is used to specify the shape of a #$Spheroid in terms of a real number (or numeric interval).  <code>(#$spheroidInverseFlattening SPHEROID INVFLAT)</code> means that <code>SPHEROID</code> has the inverse flattening <code>INVFLAT</code>.  \nWhere <code>SPHEROID</code>'s semi-major axis length (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength) is <code>MAJ</code> and its semi-minor axis length (see #$spheroidSemiminorAxisLength),\n<pre>\n  INVFLAT = MAJ/(MAJ-MIN) .\n</pre>\nAn important use of the predicate #$spheroidInverseFlattening is in representing certain geospatial reference systems.  If it is known whether a given spheroid is an #$OblateSpheroid or a #$ProlateSpheroid (qq.v.), its  inverse flattening and semi-major axis length determine its precise shape and size.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab998).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab998,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab998,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab998,15580).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab998,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab998,[]).
x_comment(x_spheroidSemimajorAxisLength,"A #$MeasurableQuantitySlot (q.v.) that is used to specify (partially) the size a #$Spheroid. <code>(#$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half of the length of <code>SPHERIOID</code>'s major axis.  The <i>major axis</i> of a spheroid is by definition the longest of its three principle axes (see #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid and #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).  For an #$OblateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-major axis length is equal to its equatorial radius (see #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).  For a #$ProlateSpheroid (q.v.), the semi-major axis length is equal to its polar radius (see #$polarRadiusOfSpheroid).\n<p>\nAn important use of the predicate #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength is in representing certain geospatial reference systems.  See also #$spheroidInverseFlattening.  If it is known whether a given spheroid is oblate or prolate, then its semi-major axis length and inverse flattening determine its precise shape and size.\n<p>\nSee also #$spheroidSemiminorAxisLength.\n<p>\nNote that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>LENGTH</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has <code>LENGTH</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab999).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab999,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab999,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab999,15586).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab999,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab999,[]).
x_comment(x_relationExistsInstance,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to state that a given binary predicate holds between some instance of a given collection and a given thing.  <code>(#$relationExistsInstance BINPRED COL THING)</code> means that there exists an instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code> such that <code>(BINPRED INST THING)</code> holds.  This is equivalent to (and can thus be used in effect as shorthand for) the more complex form:\n<p>\n<pre>\n(#$thereExists ?INST\n  (#$and\n     (#$isa ?INST COL)\n     (PRED ?INST THING))) .\n</pre>\n<p>\nFor example, (#$relationExistsInstance #$eventOccursAt #$IllegalDrugActivity #$Sweden) means that there exists illegal drug activity in Sweden.  See also the shared  #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab99a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab99a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab99a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab99a,15593).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab99a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab99a,[]).
x_comment('x_approximatelyLocatedAt-Spatial',"An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-Spatial (q.v.) that relates a spatial thing to a space region such that the former is (at least) approximately or roughly located at the latter.  <code>(#$approximatelyLocatedAt-Spatial OBJ LOC)</code> means that <code>LOC</code> is at least a close approximation to the exact location (see the specialization #$exactlyLocatedAt-Spatial) of <code>OBJ</code>.  This entails that (i) <code>OBJ</code> and <code>LOC</code> spatially intersect (often, but not necessarily, to a significant degree), (ii) the lengths/areas/volumes (as the case may be, dimensionality-wise) of <code>OBJ</code> and <code>LOC</code> are approximately the same, and (iii) each  part of <code>OBJ</code> is #$near <code>LOC</code>, and vice versa.\n<p>\nThe built-in fuzziness of #$approximatelyLocatedAt-Spatial makes it useful for representing situations in which a physical object is modelled by a geometric shape.  For example, spatial reference systems typically model the Earth's surface as a #$Spheroid, and different reference systems use spheroids of slightly different dimensions (see #$spheroidInverseFlattening, #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength, and #$CartographicSpheroidType).  Although the Earth's surface is not perfectly spheroidal, it can be said to be <i>approximately</i> located at a spheroid of any of these types.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab99b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab99b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab99b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab99b,15603).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab99b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab99b,[]).
x_comment('x_basicPrice-Event',"#$basicPrice-Event is a binary predicate that relates a #$Buying to a #$MonetaryValue (qq.v.). <code>(#$basicPrice-Event BUYING MONEY)</code> means that the sum of the basic price of all the merchandise and services bought in the #$Buying event <code>BUYING</code> was the #$MonetaryValue <code>MONEY</code>.  <code>MONEY</code> does not include tax or service charges -- for that, see #$totalCharge-Event. This predicate is not to be confused with #$basicPrice (q.v.), which relates <i>bought things</i> (i.e. purchased instances of #$TemporalThing) to their basic prices.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab99c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab99c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab99c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab99c,15606).
