x_comment(x_BilaterallySymmetricObject,"A specialization of #$AxisymmetricObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$BilaterallySymmetricObject is a (tangible or intangible) spatial object that is symmetric with respect to some bisecting straight line (if the object is planar) or plane (if it's non-planar).  In this context, <i>symmetric</i> means that the line or plane divides the object into two equal halves with the same shape (or mirror-image shapes).  For example, a #$Circle, an isosceles #$Triangle, a #$Cone, and a human body are all bilaterally symmetric objects.\n<p>\nIf a #$BilaterallySymmetricObject <code>OBJ</code> is a one- or two-dimensional planar region, then any bisecting line with respect to which <code>OBJ</code> is symmetric is an <i>axis of symmetry</i> (see #$symmetricAxes) of <code>OBJ</code>.  If <code>OBJ</code> is a two- or three-dimensional non-planar object that is symmetric with respect to a bisecting plane <code>PLANE</code>, then any straight line with respect to which the space-region intersection of <code>OBJ</code> and <code>PLANE</code> is symmetric is itself an axis of symmetry of <code>OBJ</code>.  Thus, a circle has an infinite number of axes of symmetry (viz. any straight line that intersects the circle and passes through its center); an isosceles triangle has exactly one axis of symmetry; a cone has at least one axis of symmetry; and a human body has no axis of symmetry (though it does have three #$objectAxes (q.v.)).\n<p>\nSee also #$RadiallySymmetricObject.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab9c6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab9c6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab9c6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab9c6,15742).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab9c6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab9c6,[]).
x_comment('x_testAnswers-Cardinality-Exact',"An expression of the form (#$testAnswers-Cardinality-Exact TEST N) states that when the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST is run against a #$CycKB there are exactly N distinct instances of #$CycInferenceAnswer that should bear the relation #$queryAnswers to the #$CycLQuerySpecification, let's call it SPEC, that is associated with TEST via #$testQuerySpecification.  By extension, since each instance of #$CycInferenceAnswer contains exactly one instance of #$ELInferenceBindingSet, N should be the cardinality of the #$ELSetOfInferenceBindingSets that bears the relation #$queryAnswersCycL-Exact to an instance of #$CycQueryRunning in which SPEC plays the role #$inferenceQuerySpecification (and which is run against the same #$CycKB).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab9c7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab9c7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
