x_comment(x_CartesianCoordinateSystem,"A specialization of #$CoordinateSystem.  Each instance of #$CartesianCoordinateSystem is based on two or three perpendicular axes (see #$Line-Straight) that intersect at a point, called the <i>origin</i>.  Two axes are used for coordinating a two-dimensional space; three axes are used for coordinating a three-dimensional space.  Any point in the space  coordinated by the system can be specified by a pair (or triple) of #$Distances, representing distances from the origin along each of the two (or three) axes.\n<p>\nMore precisely, the axes of a given Cartesian coordinate system are respectively designated its <i><b>x</b>-axis</i>, its <i><b>y</b>-axis</i>, and (if it has one) its <i><b>z</b>-axis</i>.  The <i><b>x</b>-coordinate</i> of a given #$Point measures its least distance from the <b>y</b>-axis (in a 2D system) or from the #$Plane defined by the <b>y</b> and <b>z</b> axes (in a 3D system).  And similarly for the <i><b>y</b>-coordinate</i> and (if it has one) the <i><b>z</b>-coordinate</i> of the point.  \n<p>\nGiven a particular Cartesian coordinate system, binary predicates for giving a point's <b>x</b>-, <b>y</b>-, and <b>z</b>-coordinates can be defined using the meta-predicates #$xAxisPredicateOfSystem, #$yAxisPredicateOfSystem, and #$zAxisPredicateOfSystem, respectively.\n<p>\nCf. #$PolarCoordinateSystem.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ab9ff).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ab9ff,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ab9ff,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ab9ff,15882).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ab9ff,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ab9ff,[]).
x_comment(x_PolarCoordinateSystem,"A specialization of #$CoordinateSystem (q.v.).  Each instance of #$PolarCoordinateSystem consists of a of a fixed #$Point called the <i>origin</i> or <i>pole</i>, and a fixed #$Ray-Geometrical that terminates at the pole, called the <i>polar axis</i>, which is usually taken to be the positive half of the <i><b>x</b>-axis</i>.  A fixed #$Line-Straight passing through the pole and perpendicular to the <b>x</b>-axis is taken to be the <i><b>y</b>-axis</i>.  For three-dimensional systems, the straight line passing through the pole and perpendicular to both the <b>x</b> and <b>y</b> axes is the <i><b>z</b>-axis</i>. \n<p>\nIn a two-dimensional polar coordinate system (also called a <i>circular coordinate system</i>), the location of a given point <b>P</b> in the plane is specified by giving (i) the #$Distance from the pole to <b>P</b> (called the <i>radius</i>) and (ii) the size of the angle (see #$AngularDistance) that the ray from the pole through <b>P</b> makes with the polar axis.\n<p>\nTwo common types of three-dimensional polar coordinate systems are <i>cylindrical coordinate systems</i> and <i>spherical coordinate systems</i>.  In a cylindrical system, the location of a point <b>P</b> is specified by giving (i') the distance (or <i>radius</i>) between the <b>z</b>-axis and <b>P</b>, (ii') the angular distance (called the <i>azimuth</i> or <i>longitude</i>) between the polar axis and the line from the origin to the projection of <b>P</b> onto the <b>xy</b>-plane, and (iii') the signed distance (or <i>height</i>) from the <b>xy</b>-plane to <b>P</b>.  In a spherical system, the location of a point <b>P</b> is specified by giving (i'') the radius [defined as in (i) above], (ii'') the azimuth or longitude [defined as in (ii') above], and (iii'') the angular distance (called the <i>colatitude</i> or <i>polar angle</i>) between the <b>z</b>-axis and the line from the origin to <b>P</b>.  The #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem (q.v.) is an example of a spherical coordinate system.\n<p>\nGiven a particular polar coordinate system, predicates for specifying the radius, the azimuth, and the colatitude of a point can be defined using the meta-predicates #$radialDistancePredicate, #$azimuthAnglePredicate, and #$altitudeAnglePredicate, respectively.\n<p>\nCf. #$CartesianCoordinateSystem.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba00).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba00,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aba00,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aba00,15889).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aba00,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aba00,[]).
x_comment(x_altitudeAnglePredicate,"A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem (q.v.).  <code>(#$altitudeAnglePredicate ALT-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>ALT-PRED</code> is the binary predicate used to express colatitude or polar angle coordinates of #$SpatialThings in the #$PolarCoordinateSystem (q.v.) <code>SYS</code>.  <code>ALT-PRED</code>'s two argument-places will be restricted (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$SpatialThing (or some specialization thereof) and #$AngularDistance, respectively.\n<p>\nThus (e.g.), <code>(ALT-PRED POINT (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 30))</code> would mean that, in <code>SYS</code>, <code>POINT</code> is on a ray that terminates at the pole and makes a 30-degree angle with the <b>z</b>-axis.\n<p>\nFor example, #$latitude is the #$altitudeAnglePredicate for the  #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.\n<p>\nSee also #$radialDistancePredicate and #$azimuthAnglePredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba01).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba01,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aba01,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aba01,15898).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aba01,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aba01,[]).
x_comment(x_azimuthAnglePredicate,"A specialization of #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem (q.v.).  <code>(#$azimuthAnglePredicate AZI-PRED SYS)</code> means that <code>AZIPRED</code> is the binary predicate used to express azimuth angle coordinates of #$SpatialThings in the #$PolarCoordinateSystem (q.v.) <code>SYS</code>.  <code>PRED</code>'s two argument-places will be restricted (via #$argIsa) to instances of #$SpatialThing (or some specialization thereof) and #$AngularDistance, respectively.\n<p>\nThus (e.g.), <code>(AZI-PRED POINT (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 30))</code> would mean that, in <code>SYS</code>, <code>POINT</code> (or, in a 3-D system, <code>POINT</code>'s image on the <b>xy</b> plane) is on a ray that terminates at the pole and makes a 30-degree angle (counter-clockwise) with the polar axis.\n<p>\nFor example, #$longitude is the #$azimuthAnglePredicate for the  #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem.\n<p>\nSee also #$radialDistancePredicate and #$altitudeAnglePredicate.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba02).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba02,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aba02,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aba02,15905).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aba02,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aba02,[]).
x_comment(x_CoordinateSystem,"A specialization of #$FrameOfReference (q.v.).  This is the collection of systems with respect to which the locations of #$SpatialThings can be specified.  Each #$CoordinateSystem has an associated suite of coordinate value predicates (see #$coordinateValuePredicateOfSystem and its specializations), which assign coordinates to spatial things.  \n<p>\nThe two most common types of coordinate systems are #$CartesianCoordinateSystems and #$PolarCoordinateSystems (qq.v.).  The #$GeodeticCoordinateSystem is an example of the latter; it has two coordinate value predicates, #$latitude and #$longitude, both of which assign #$AngularDistances to spatial things.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba03).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba03,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aba03,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aba03,15912).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aba03,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aba03,[]).
x_comment(x_ProvidingSubsidy,"A specialization of #$GrantingMoney. Each instance of #$ProvidingSubsidy is a monetary gift from an instance of #$RegionalGovernment to some other agent (usually, either a private company or individual, or some other instance of #$RegionalGovernment), with the understanding that the recipient will use the money in an enterprise that benefits the public.") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba04).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba04,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aba04,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
