x_comment('x_cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric',"<code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric Q-SPEC DEGREE)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> is an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification that is, to the degree of relevance <code>DEGREE</code>, pertinent to an instance of information gathering, due to the pertinence of the terms in <code>SET</code>).\n<p>\nThis predicate is similar in meaning to #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree (q.v.).  Whereas, however, #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree takes an instance of #$Relevance as an argument in the second argument position, #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric takes a number in the range of 0 to 1. \n<p>\n<code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric Q-SPEC 0)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> has no relevance (<i>i.e.</i> it is equivalent to <code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric Q-SPEC (#$NoAmountFn #$Relevance))</code>.\n<p>\nNOTE: This predicate should only be used in microtheories which specify the appropriate qualifications of assertions made with it: e.g., for whom the query is pertinent, on what task, at what time and place, <i>etc.</i>") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba0b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba0b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
