x_comment(x_molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample,"A ternary predicate that relates a sample of something -- typically a solution -- to the molar concentration of *chemical objects* of a given species which it contains. (#$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample SAMPLE CHEM-OBJ-TYPE VALUE) holds just in case SAMPLE has VALUE, expressed in moles per liter, of ions/molecules of type CHEM-OBJ-TYPE.  The concentration of ions in a sample is expressed in terms of ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) NUM). Note that for ionic compounds this expresses the molarity of the *dissociated* species in a solution: e.g., if SAMPLE is a 1M aqueous solution of HCl,  it would be incorrect to state (#$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample SAMPLE (#$MoleculeFn #$HydrogenChloride) ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) 1)) since HCl, a strong acid, dissociates completely in #$HydrogenPlusIons and #$ChlorideIons, so that SAMPLE contains virtually no amount of HCl *molecules*. On the other hand, it would be correct to assert (#$molarityOfChemicalObjectInSample (q.v.) SAMPLE #$HydrogenPlusIons ((#$PerFn #$Mole-ChemicalCountUnit #$Liter) SOME-NUM)).") :- x_cid(a65b16517aba5a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517aba5a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517aba5a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517aba5a,16142).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517aba5a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517aba5a,[]).
