x_comment(x_aisPriorToAISInAIS,"An instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate that identifies an ordering among two instances of #$AbstractInformationStructure in some larger structure.\n<pre>\n   (#$aisPriorToAISInAIS AIS-1 AIS-2 STRUCTURE)</pre>\nmeans that <code>AIS-1</code> occurs prior to <code>AIS-2</code> within the context <code>STRUCTURE</code>, itself an instance of #$AbstractInformationStructure.  \"occurs prior to\" in this context means that <code>AIS-1</code> comes before <code>AIS-2</code> as understood by the conventions used in interpreting <code>STRUCTURE</code>.  So if <code>AIS-1</code> and <code>AIS-2</code> are abstract character strings (c.f., #$CharacterString) of English and <code>STRUCTURE</code> is a document formatted in the typical way that English documents are formatted, then any physical instantiation <code>PRINTOUT</code> of <code>STRUCTURE</code> will be such that an instantiation of (#$instantiationOfAIS) <code>AIS-1</code> will appear to the left of or higher than or on an earlier page than an instantiation of <code>AIS-2</code> on <code>PRINTOUT</code>.<p>Note that because the same #$AbstractInformationStructure can appear multiple times in a larger structure, #$aisPriorToAISInAIS is neither asymmetric (see #$asymmetricInArgs) in its first and second argument-places, nor irreflexive (see #$interArgDifferent) in those argument-places.  Thus where <code>STRUCTURE</code> is an abstract information structure for the book <i>Green Eggs and Ham</i>, the following will hold true: <code>(#$aisPriorToAISInAIS \"Sam I am\" \"Sam I am\" STRUCTURE)</code>.  This feature makes #$aisPriorToAISInAIS somewhat unusual as an instance of #$TernaryOrderingPredicate; contrast with #$cisPriorToCISInStructure.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abadb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abadb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abadb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
