x_comment(x_completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs,"An instance of #$InferenceCompletenessPredicate that relates a predicate to a set, the members of which are lists that serve to specify values and argument places in assertions formed with the predicate.  #$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs is similar to the more specialized and focused predicate, #$completeExtentEnumerableForValueInArg, which states completeness information for a predicate given a single value and argument-place.  Where\n<pre>\n    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValueInArg PRED VALUE N)</pre>\nmeans that every fact expressible in the form\n<pre>\n    (PRED . ARGS)</pre>\nin which <code>VALUE</code> appears in the <code>N</code>th argument-place is enumerable,\n<pre>\n    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs PRED SET),</pre>\nwhere <code>SET</code> consists of a single list whose first member is <code>VALUE</code> and second is <code>N</code>, expressible as,\n<pre>\n    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs PRED \n      (#$TheSet \n        (#$TheList VALUE N)))</pre>\nwill have the same meaning.\n<p>In general, \n<pre>\n    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs PRED \n      (#$TheSet \n        (#$TheList VALUE-1 INTEGER-1) ... (#$TheSet VALUE-N INTEGER-N))),</pre>\nmeans that every fact expressed in the form of the sentence <code>S</code> =\n<pre>\n    (PRED . ARGS)</pre>\nis enumerable if for each pair <code>VALUE</code> and <code>INTEGER</code> in <<code>VALUE-1</code>, <code>INTEGER-1</code>> ... <<code>VALUE-N</code>, <code>INTEGER-N</code>> is such that <code>VALUE</code> appears in the <code>INTEGER</code>th argument place of <code>S</code>.\n<p>\nFor example,\n<pre>\n    (#$completeExtentEnumerableForValuesInArgs #$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure \n      (#$TheSet \n        (#$TheList #$NounPhrase 3)\n        (#$TheList EMailMessageText001 4)))</pre>\nmeans that every noun phrase in the text of this particular e-mail is known.  I.e., every fact expressible in the form,\n<pre>\n    (#$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure OCCURRENCE NUMBER #$NounPhrase EMailMessageText001)</pre>\nis enumerable by the system.  In practical terms, this means that when querying for \n<pre>\n    (#$nthOccurrenceOfStructureTypeInStructure ?OCCURRENCE ?NUMBER #$NounPhrase EMailMessageText001)</pre>\nall values of <code>?OCCURRENCE</code> and <code>?NUMBER</code> can be found (using removal only).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abae2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abae2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abae2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
