x_comment(x_spatiallyDisjoint,"A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$spatiallyRelated (q.v.) that holds between  #$SpatialThings that do not spatially overlap.  <code>(#$spatiallyDisjoint THING1 THING2)</code> means that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> are in the same spatial universe (see #$UniversalSpaceRegion), but there is no space region that they both subsume (see #$spatiallySubsumes).  It follows that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> have no spatial #$parts in common.\n<p>\nFor example, the United States and the Soviet Union were spatially disjoint, as were Plato and Aristotle, and as are the planets Saturn and Jupiter.\n<p>\nCf. #$spatiallyIntersects.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abb20).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abb20,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abb20,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abb20,16745).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abb20,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abb20,[]).
x_comment(x_cospatial,"A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$spatiallySubsumes (q.v.).  <code>(#$cospatial THING1 THING2)</code> means that the <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> have exactly the same spatial extent.  This implies that they have the same location, shape, and size.  But note that it does <i>not</i> imply that they are one and the same, or that they have any parts in common.  For we allow that distinct things (e.g. a tangible object and the pure space region it occupies) can spatially coincide without sharing any common parts.  #$SpaceRegions that are cospatial, however, are identical (see #$identicalSpaceRegions).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abb21).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abb21,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
