x_comment(x_ConcaveTangibleObject,"A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  This is the collection of tangible objects that are concave.  The surface of a concave object is \"indented\", i.e. contains points such that the straight line-segment between them is not subsumed by the object (see #$spatiallySubsumes).  \n<p>\nCf. #$ConvexTangibleObject.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abb43).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abb43,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abb43,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abb43,16814).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abb43,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abb43,[]).
x_comment(x_SheetOfSomeStuff,"A specialization of #$PartiallyTangible.  Each instance of #$SheetOfSomeStuff is a partially tangible thing which (in its \"intrinsic\" shape -- see #$CurrentShapeVsIntrinsicShape) is continuous and, when flattened, uncrumpled, unfurled, etc., has one dimension which is significantly smaller than the other two dimensions.  Although each instance of #$SheetOfSomeStuff is a _continuous_ sheet, it may be penetrable, so #$Screen-Mesh, #$Netting, and #$LaceCloth are specializations of #$SheetOfSomeStuff.  Instances of #$SheetOfSomeStuff  may be in solid, liquid, or gaseous form.  An instance of #$Puddle on a relatively flat surface (but not in a deep pothole) or an instance of #$AltoStratusCloud would be instances of this collection.  Note that being an instance of #$SheetOfSomeStuff implies nothing about the current configuration of the instance; in particular, instances of #$SheetOfSomeStuff need not be perpetually lying flat.  For example, aluminum foil on a roll or a bedspread crumpled up on a bed are instances of #$SheetOfSomeStuff. Cf. #$SheetShapedObject.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abb44).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abb44,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abb44,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abb44,16817).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abb44,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abb44,[]).
x_comment('x_Corner-TwoEdge',"A specialization of #$CornerOnObject and #$LinearObject-Tangible (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Corner-TwoEdge is a corner of a sheet-like tangible object (see #$SheetOfSomeStuff and #$SheetShapedObject): a part of the object wherein two more-or-less straight linear edges meet to form an angle of substantially less than 180 degrees.  A polygonal-shaped object has at least three two-edge corners.\n<p>\nSee also #$Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abb45).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abb45,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abb45,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abb45,16818).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abb45,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abb45,[]).
x_comment('x_Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge',"A specialization of #$CornerOnObject (q.v.).  Each instance of #$Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdge is a corner of a #$SolidTangibleThing: a part of the object wherein three (or more) more-or-less straight linear edges (see #$EdgeOnObject) meet in such a way that any two of them form an angle of substantially less than 180 degrees.\n<p>\nExamples of #$Corner-ThreeOrMoreEdges include corners of the boxes and the tops of pyramids.\n<p>\nSee also #$Corner-TwoEdge.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abb46).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abb46,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
