x_comment(x_suspendedIn,"A binary #$SpatialPredicate.  <code>(#$suspendedIn OBJ FLUID)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is fully immersed in <code>FLUID</code> (see #$in-ImmersedFully), and <code>FLUID</code> provides (via fluid dynamics) a significant supporting or upward force on <code>OBJ</code> so that OBJ does not undergo rapid falling.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abba6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abba6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abba6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abba6,17058).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abba6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abba6,[]).
x_comment(x_touches,"A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$near that holds between certain tangible objects.  <code>(#$touches OBJ1 OBJ2)</code> means that <code>OBJ1</code> and <code>OBJ2</code> are in contact, either directly or indirectly.  Two spatial things are directly in contact just in case there is nothing between them; to indicate that such a situation obtains between two spatial things, use the specialization #$touches-Directly.  Two spatial things touch indirectly if there is only a very thin object or substance between them.  Here 'very thin' means that the distance between the surfaces of the two spatial things is very much less than the distance between their center points.  \n<p>\nHere are some examples in which #$touches holds: an airplane touches the air around it; my feet touch the floor (even though I am wearing shoes); a blanket touches the person sleeping under it (even if there is a sheet); a hovercraft touches the water (even though a cushion of air is between it and the water).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abba7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abba7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abba7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abba7,17059).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abba7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abba7,[]).
x_comment('x_touches-Directly',"A specialization of #$touches (q.v.).  <code>(#$touches-Directly REGION1 REGION2)</code> means that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> are #$SpatialThings that are in direct spatial contact.  That is, there is a point or region in <code>REGION1</code> and a point or region in <code>REGION2</code> such that the distance between those points/regions is zero.  \n<p>\nNote that either one of <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> might or might not be #$PartiallyTangible.\n<p>\nNote also that <code>REGION1</code> and <code>REGION2</code> might or might not be #$spatiallyDisjoint (q.v.).  For the case where they are disjoint, see the specialization #$touches-Externally; for the case where they're not disjoint, see \nthe specialization #$spatiallyIntersects.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abba8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abba8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abba8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abba8,17062).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abba8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abba8,[]).
x_comment(x_trajectoryPassesThrough,"An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$nonDeliberateActors.  <code>(#$trajectoryPassesThrough MOVE LOC)</code> means that the trajectory (see #$trajectory-Complete) of the object moving (see #$objectMoving) <code>OBJ</code> in the #$Translation-SingleTrajectory <code>MOVE</code> passes through the location (see #$EnduringThing-Localized) <code>LOC</code>.  Here \"passes through <code>LOC</code>\" implies that, in the course of moving from its #$fromLocation to its #$toLocation, <code>OBJ</code> first arrives in and then leaves <code>LOC</code>.  \n<p>\nNote that <code>LOC</code> is <i>not</i> constrained to be #$spatiallyDisjoint with this from-location or to-location; <code>LOC</code> might even subsume (see #$spatiallySubsumes) either of them.  Thus, if (say) <code>LOC</code> subsumes the from-location, <code>OBJ</code> must first leave <code>LOC</code>, then arrive back in <code>LOC</code>, and then leave <code>LOC</code> again in order to pass through it.  \n<p>\nAlso note that <code>LOC</code> must be #$Stationary during <code>MOVE</code>, relative to the associated #$FrameOfReference.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abba9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abba9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abba9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abba9,17068).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abba9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abba9,[]).
x_comment(x_traversalPassesThrough,"A specialization of #$pointOnTraversal.  <code>(#$traversalPassesThrough TRAV POINT)</code> means that <code>POINT</code> is a point that the #$Traversal (q.v.) <code>TRAV</code> <i>passes through</i>, which requires that <code>TRAV</code> must first enter and then leave <code>POINT</code>.  Thus, <code>TRAV</code> does <i>not</i> pass through its starting-point (or ending-point) unless it happens to meet that point twice.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abbaa).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abbaa,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abbaa,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abbaa,17073).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abbaa,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abbaa,[]).
x_comment(x_subObligations,"A #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate that is a specialization of #$normativeSpecificationParts.  (#$subObligations OBLIG SUBOBLIG) means that the #$Obligation (q.v.) SUBOBLIG is a part of the wider #$Obligation OBLIG. SUBOBLIG might be (i) a part of what all of the #$obligatedAgents to OBLIG are obligated to, (ii) the whole of what some of the obligated agents to OBLIG are obligated to, or (iii) some combination of both of the foregoing cases.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$subObligations #$TheTenCommandments #$TheFirstCommandment) holds.  And a given state's body of laws pertaining to minors is a subobligation of the state's entire penal code.\n<p>\nCf. #$subAgreements.  Note that, although not all #$Obligations are #$Agreements, if (#$subObligations OBLIG SUBOBLIG) holds and both OBLIG and SUBOBLIG are agreements, then (#$subAgreements OBLIG SUBOBLIG) holds.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abbab).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abbab,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abbab,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abbab,17074).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abbab,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abbab,[]).
x_comment(x_subAgreements,"A #$NonPhysicalPartPredicate that is a specialization of #$normativeSpecificationParts.  (#$subAgreements AGREE SUBAGREE) means that the #$Agreement (q.v.) SUBAGREE is a part of the larger #$Agreement AGREE.  SUBAGREE might be (i) a part of what all of the #$agreeingAgents to AGREE agree to, (ii) the whole of what some of the agreeing agents to AGREE agree to, or (iii) some combination of both of the foregoing cases.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$subAgreements #$LawOfTheSea-UnitedNationsConvention #$MineralRightsClause-UNLawOfTheSea) holds.  And the agreement that I will not attack you first is one of the #$subAgreements of our mutual truce.\n<p>\nCf. #$subObligations.  Note that, although not all #$Agreements are #$Obligations (q.v.), if (#$subAgreements AGREE SUBAGREE) holds and both AGREE and SUBAGREE are obligations, then (#$subObligations AGREE SUBAGREE) holds as well.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abbac).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abbac,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abbac,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abbac,17079).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abbac,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abbac,[]).
x_comment(x_newDiscourseReferentInUDS,"(#$newDiscourseReferentInUDS UDS PEG) means that PEG is a discourse\nentity introduced in the discourse by the utterance to which UDS\ncorresponds.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abbad).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abbad,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
