x_comment('x_exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical',"An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical and #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical; it relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.).   <code>(#$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent is the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.\n<p>\nNote that #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  Use #$locationState to supply such additional information.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding function #$GeographicalSpaceRegionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abc49).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abc49,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abc49,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abc49,17392).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abc49,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abc49,[]).
x_comment('x_whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical',"An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical that relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.) in which all of the former is located.   <code>(#$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at some part of <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent subsumes the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.\n<p>\nNote that #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  Use #$locationState to supply such additional information.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.\n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical; and see #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abc4a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abc4a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abc4a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abc4a,17397).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abc4a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abc4a,[]).
x_comment('x_partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical',"An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical that relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.) in which part of the former is located.   <code>(#$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that part of <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent is subsumed by the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.\n<p>\nNote that #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  Use #$locationState to supply such additional information.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.\n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$exactlyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical; and see #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abc4b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abc4b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abc4b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abc4b,17402).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abc4b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abc4b,[]).
x_comment('x_genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical',"An #$AbsoluteLocationalPredicate and specialization of #$notFarFrom that relates a #$GeographicalThing to a #$GeographicalSpaceRegion (qq.v.) such that part of the former is located at a part of the latter.   <code>(#$genericallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical OBJ GEOSPACE)</code> means that part of <code>OBJ</code> is exactly located at a part of <code>GEOSPACE</code>.  <code>GEOSPACE</code> corresponds to a set of pairs of geographical coordinates (i.e. degrees latitude and longitude) whose extent intersects the exact space on a planet's surface that is occupied by <code>OBJ</code>.\n<p>\nNote that #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical is agnostic as to whether or not <code>OBJ</code> is (partially or wholly) located under, on, or above the ground at <code>GEOSPACE</code> (see #$locationState); that is why this predicate is <i>not</i> a specialization of #$spatiallyIntersects.  Also note that this predicate assumes a planetary #$FrameOfReference, typically #$PlanetEarth and the #$TerrestrialFrameOfReference.\n<p>\nSee also the specializations #$partiallyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical and #$whollyLocatedAt-SurfaceGeographical.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abc4c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abc4c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abc4c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abc4c,17407).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abc4c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abc4c,[]).
x_comment(x_polarRadiusOfSpheroid,"A specialization of #$sizeParameterOfObject.  <code>(#$polarRadiusOfSpheroid SPHEROID DISTANCE)</code> means that <code>DISTANCE</code> is one-half the length of <code>SPHEROID</code>'s polar axis.  \n<p>\nWhenever two #$symmetricAxes (q.v.) of a #$Spheroid that is <i>not</i> a perfect #$Sphere have the same length, they are both <i>equatorial axes</i> of the spheroid (cf. #$equatorialRadiusOfSpheroid).  All of the equatorial axes of such a spheroid lie in a single plane; and the (unique) symmetric-axis of the spheroid that is perpendicular with its equatorial axes is known as its <i>polar axis</i>. \n<p>\nIf a spheroid's equatorial radius is greater than its polar radius, it is an #$OblateSpheroid; if its polar radius is greater than its equatorial radius, it is a #$ProlateSpheroid.  The greater radius (whichever it might be) measures the spheroid's <i>semi-major axis</i> (see #$spheroidSemimajorAxisLength).  A #$Sphere's polar and equatorial radii are both equal to its ordinary #$radius.\n<p>\nNote that, since an axis is a #$Line-Straight, it strictly speaking has infinite length.  So when we say that a given axis <code>AXIS</code> of a spheroid <code>SPHEROID</code> has the length <code>DISTANCE</code>, it is really shorthand for saying that the line-segment part of <code>AXIS</code> whose endpoints are where <code>AXIS</code> intersects <code>SPHEROID</code> has length <code>DISTANCE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abc4d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abc4d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abc4d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abc4d,17412).
