x_comment(x_UsingAForFn,"This function can be used to denote the collection of instances of #$Event in which some instance of #$Agent-PartiallyTangible deliberately uses some instance of a subcollection of #$SomethingExisting in some subcollection of #$PurposefulAction. (#$UsingAForFn OBJ_TYPE ACT_TYPE ROLE) denotes the subcollection of  #$PurposefulAction in which an instance OBJ of the  subcollection OBJ_TYPE of #$SomethingExisting is used in an instance ACT of some subcollection ACT_TYPE of #$PurposefulAction in the instance ROLE of #$Role. For example,  (#$UsingAForFn #$Hammer #$ConstructingABuilding #$deviceUsed) denotes the subcollection of #$PurposefulAction in which an instance of #$Hammer is used in an instance of #$ConstructingABuilding as a #$deviceUsed. (#$UsingAForFn #$Recipe-CW #$CookingFood #$instructionsFollowed) denotes the subcollection of #$PurposefulAction in which an instance of #$Recipe-CW is used in an instance of #$CookingFood as the #$instructionsFollowed.  Note that ACT_TYPE need not be the  #$typePrimaryFunction for OBJ_TYPE, e.g. one could in principle reify (#$UsingAForFn #$HuntingKnife #$Whittling #$deviceUsed), though #$Whittling is not a #$typePrimaryFunction of #$HuntingKnife.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcb7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcb7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcb7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abcb7,17661).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abcb7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcb7,[]).
x_comment(x_TextString,"A subcollection of #$CharacterString.  Each instance of #$TextString is a #$CharacterString that constitutes a                                                                                                   meaningful unit (with respect to some actual language or other                                                                                                             #$CommunicationConvention), such as words, phrases, sentences,                                                                                                             multi-sentence constructions, or symbols that contribute to the meaning of                                                                                                 phrases or sentences (including syncategormatic objects such as                                                                                                            punctuation symbols).  Formal languages may define multi-symbol constructs                                                                                                  that have meaning but are not considered words -- these, too are                                                                                                          \ninstances of #$TextString. For example, this sequence of characters:\n<pre>\n  er#q2\n</pre>\nis a #$CharacterString but <i>not</i> (as far as I know) a #$TextString. Conversely, the sentence you are now reading is a #$TextString, as is the text of this entire comment. \n<p>\nNote that, as with all #$CharacterStrings, a #$TextString is a sequence of abstract symbols, not a tangible instantiation of it (cf. #$CharacterStringToken).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcb8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcb8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcb8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
