xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcc3,[]).
x_comment(x_Traversal,"The collection of all traversals of paths and networks.  A traversal is a trace of a pathway along pre-existing #$Path-Generics.  It is the \"path-representation\" of certain actual trajectories (instances of #$Trajectory) that are confined to paths.  Different actual trajectories may have the same traversal; to see this, consider two cars driving along a block of 10th St.  The actual car movements have different trajectories -- say, one drove straight while the other switched lanes many times.  The two different trajectories are both characterized by the same traversal of that block of 10th Street.  \n<p>\nA traversal of a network of paths can be represented by (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) where <b>CHAIN</b> is a #$PathChain.  We may choose to use a more detailed representation of the traversal, i.e. use a #$PathChain made of more, but shorter, paths.  For example, we may use several paths to replace a cycle in <b>CHAIN</b> if we want to be clear about the direction in that part of the traversal, or we may have to replace a path in <b>CHAIN</b> by several 'shorter' paths if we want to preserve the fact that the object moved backward at a small part of <b>PATH</b> and then resumed its original direction.  In summary, a traversal is a constraint on a trajectory and a characterization of it in terms of traversing pre-existing #$Path-Generics.   Technically, a #$Traversal from <b>A</b> to <b>B</b> can be expressed as (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) where <b>CHAIN</b> is a #$PathChain such that the first item in <b>CHAIN</b> is <b>A</b> and the last is <b>B</b> (we can assert: (#$traversalFrom (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) <b>A</b>) and (#$traversalTo (#$TraversalFn <b>CHAIN</b>) <b>B</b>)).  \n<p>\nNote that a #$Traversal, unlike a #$Path-Generic, can cross itself, i.e. can \"pass through\" a point on it many times.  Mathematically: given a #$PathSystem or #$CustomarySystemOfLinks, a #$Traversal of it is a directed path that has a homomorphism into that system.\n<p>\nRelated constants include #$TraversalFn, #$traversalFrom, #$traversalTo, #$pointOnTraversal, #$subTraversals, and #$traversalPassesThrough, #$traversalInSystem.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcc4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcc4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcc4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abcc4,17706).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abcc4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcc4,[]).
x_comment('x_recipientOfInfo-Intended',"A specialization of both #$recipientOfInfo and #$communicationTarget that is used to indicate who among those who receive information in a communication event were also <i>intended</i> to receive the information. (#$recipientOfInfo-Intended COMM RECIPIENT) holds whenever (i) RECIPIENT successfully receives the information communicated in the communication event COMM (see #$recipientOfInfo) and (ii) RECIPIENT is one of the #$Agent-Generics that the #$senderOfInfo in                                        the #$CommunicationAct-Single COMM intended to receive the information (see #$communicationTarget).  \n<p>\nFor example, suppose I am speaking at a closed meeting to one or more persons on classified information and, unbeknownst to me, a spy outside the room is listening in and hears everything I am saying.  If each of the persons in the meeting hears everything I am saying, then each bears the relation #$recipientOfInfo-Intended to each instance of #$CommunicationAct-Single COMM during the meeting of which I am the #$senderOfInfo.  The spy, however, bears only the relation #$recipientOfInfo to each of these instances.  Suppose, however, that one person at the meeting is hard of hearing and does not hear what I am saying.  Each event COMM is still a #$CommunicationAct-Single since it had at least one #$subEvents that is an instance of #$AccessingAnIBT (at least the spy heard what was said) but the person who is hard of hearing is a #$communicationTarget of COMM but not a #$recipientOfInfo-Intended since he or she did not receive the information, i.e. was not a #$recipientOfInfo.  \n<p>\nSee also #$intendedAudience.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcc5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcc5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcc5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abcc5,17713).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abcc5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcc5,[]).
x_comment(x_inputs,"An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$objectActedOn that relates a given event to an object or material that is used in that event and thereby either \"used up\" (i.e. destroyed) or incorporated into another object. <code>(#$inputs EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> is a pre-existing participant in (see #$preActors) the #$CreationOrDestructionEvent <code>EVENT</code> which, during and due to <code>EVENT</code>, is either destroyed (see #$inputsDestroyed) or incorporated into a new entity (see #$inputsCommitted). \n<p>\nFor example, the pigments used to paint the Mona Lisa were #$inputs to Leonardo's painting process; however, his brushes were not #$inputs, even though they were changed a little by it.  In general, in instances of #$Manufacturing, materials or objects are inputs if they find their way into the product manufactured or if they are destroyed in the manufacturing process (e.g. the coke used in manufacturing steel).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcc6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcc6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcc6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abcc6,17718).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abcc6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcc6,[]).
x_comment(x_outputsRemaining,"A specialization of both #$outputs and #$preActors that relates physical destruction events to surviving parts of the things destroyed in those events.  <code>(#$outputsRemaining EVENT OBJECT)</code> means that <code>OBJECT</code> persists after <code>EVENT</code>, and that it existed before <code>EVENT</code> as an identifiable part of one of the things destroyed during <code>EVENT</code> (see #$inputsDestroyed).  \n<p>\nExamples: (1) when a sheet of plywood is sawn through, the original board is destroyed and the remaining-outputs are the two smaller plywood pieces; (2) when a mixture undergoes a #$FluidFiltrationProcess, the mixture itself is destroyed, while both the material that was in suspension (see #$suspendedPart) and the fluid it was suspended in (see #$suspendingFluid) are remaining-outputs.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcc7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcc7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcc7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abcc7,17721).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abcc7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcc7,[]).
x_comment(x_eventOccursAt,"An #$ActorSlot and a specialization of #$inRegion that relates a particular event to the instance of #$EnduringThing-Localized at which it occurs.  <code>(#$eventOccursAt EVENT LOC)</code> means that the spatial extent of <code>EVENT</code> is within <code>LOC</code>. For example, (#$eventOccursAt LomaPrietaEarthquake SanFranciscoBayArea).  \n<p>\nSee also the more general predicate #$eventPartiallyOccursAt, which relates an event to any instance of #$SpatialThing-Localized in which some sub-event of it occurs.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcc8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcc8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abcc8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abcc8,17724).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abcc8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abcc8,[]).
x_comment(x_communicationParticipants,"A predicate for relating a #$Communicating event to an agent  participating in the event. (#$communicationParticipants COMM AGENT) means that AGENT is a participant in COMM.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abcc9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abcc9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
