x_comment(x_AbstractVisualStructure,"A specialization of #$AbstractStructure (q.v.).  Each instance of #$AbstractVisualStructure (AVS) is a structure that can be discerned visually.   Any concrete <i>instantiation</i> of a given AVS consists of a particular spatial (or spatio-temporal) arrangement of shapes and/or colors.  A given AVS might have multiple instantiations.   By the same token, a given concrete visual arrangment (appearing, say, on a sheet of paper or a computer monitor screen) might simultaneously instantiate multiple AVSs, corresponding to different degrees of abstractness.\n<p>\nFor example, when displaying a particular image of a sunset, my computer screen might instantiate (among others) the following AVSs: (i) an AVS such that each of its instantiations consists of a round region and a contrasting surrounding region, (ii) an AVS such that each of its instantiations consists of a round <i>orange</i> region and a surrounding <i>blue</i> region, and (iii) an AVS such that each of its instantiations consists of an image that is pixel-for-pixel indiscernable from my screen in its current state.\n<p>\n#$AbstractVisualStructures can be thought of as the abstract commonality in virtue of which objects, or arrangements of objects, look the same (to some degree of granularity).  Abstract visual structures can be instantiated by objects or groups of objects.  Thus, a group of objects in a room, together with the room itself, instantiate shapes and colors in a certain spatial arrangement; and any group of objects instantiating all the same #$AbstractVisualStructures as the first group would be visually indiscernible from that group.\n<p>\nA notable specialization of this collection is #$GraphicalStructure.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd2d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd2d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd2d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd2d,17883).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd2d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd2d,[]).
x_comment(x_TemporalThing,"A specialization of #$Individual.  This is the collection of all things that have temporal extent or location -- things about which one might sensibly ask \"<i>When?</i>\".  #$TemporalThing thus contains many kinds of things, including events, physical objects, agreements, and pure intervals of time.  Major specializations of this collection are #$SomethingExisting, #$Situation, and #$TimeInterval (qq.v.).\n<p>\nThings that are timeless -- such as mathematical sets, #$Collections, and numbers -- are of course  <i>not</i> instances of #$TemporalThing (cf. #$AtemporalThing).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd2e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd2e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd2e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd2e,17890).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd2e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd2e,[]).
x_comment(x_temporallySubsumes,"A #$ComplexTemporalPredicate (q.v.). <code>(#$temporallySubsumes LONG SHORT)</code> means that all time points in the #$temporalExtent (q.v.) of <code>SHORT</code> are contained in that of <code>LONG</code>.   This implies that <code>SHORT</code> does not start before, or end after, <code>LONG</code>.  Moreover, at any time point or interval when <code>LONG</code> does not exist or occur or obtain, neither does <code>SHORT</code>.\n<p>\nNote that #$temporallySubsumes is a strictly temporal relation; it is thus weaker than its specialization #$subEvents, which further entails that its first (temporally longer) argument has the second as a \"part\".\n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$cotemporal.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd2f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd2f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd2f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd2f,17893).
