x_comment(x_IsaGenlsHierarchy,"The collection of all instances of #$ClassSubclassHierarchy that describes a domain along class (#$elementOf) and sub-class (#$subsetOf) lines, where the domain is restricted to individuals (#$Individual) and collections (#$Collection).  Given that <code>(#$typedGenlPreds #$isa #$elementOf)</code> and <code>(#$typedGenlPreds #$genls #$subsetOf)</code>, this collection can also be described as a collection of taxonomic systems that describe a domain along #$isa and #$genls lines.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd40).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd40,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd40,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd40,17932).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd40,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd40,[]).
x_comment(x_algorithmComputes,"A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to the functions that they constitute effective procedures for computing <code>(#$algorithmComputes ALGORITHM FUNCTION)</code> means that <code>ALGORITHM</code> is a sequence of operations that constitute a strictly mechanical procedure for determining the value of <code>FUNCTION</code> for any argument in its domain (see #$valueOfFunctionForArgs and #$relationDomain).  By their very nature, all algorithms compute some function or other.  In some cases, distinct algorithms will compute the same function (see #$equivAlgorithms).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd41).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd41,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd41,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd41,17933).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd41,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd41,[]).
x_comment(x_algorithmInputs,"A predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to their allowable inputs.  <code>(#$algorithmInputs ALGORITHM THING)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is a possible input to <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  That is, if <code>ALGORITHM</code> is applied to <code>THING</code>, a finite sequence of intermediate results, and possibly a final output (see #$algorithmOutputFor), will be generated, after which <code>ALGORITHM</code> will halt.  Depending on the nature of <code>ALGORITHM</code>, <code>THING</code> might be a character-string, a number, a physical object, a set, a function, a list, or something else.  \n<p>\nAn algorithm need not generate a final output for all allowable inputs (e.g. a division algorithm when the divisor is zero).  While most algorithms of interest have a range of allowable inputs (e.g. all integers) for which they generate a  corresponding range of result-sequences, an algorithm might be so specific as to have only a single allowable input (e.g. a dedicated algorithm for squaring the number 2).  Unless stated otherwise, a thing is an allowable input for a given algorithm if and only if it is an element of the domain of the first operation within the algorithm.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd42).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd42,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd42,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd42,17934).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd42,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd42,[]).
x_comment(x_algorithmOutputFor,"A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to their outputs for given inputs.  <code>(#$algorithmOutputFor ALGORITHM INPUT OUTPUT)</code> means that <code>ALGORITHM</code>, when applied to <code>INPUT</code>, produces <code>OUTPUT</code> as its final result.  Note that <code>ALGORITHM</code> might not produce a final output for every allowable input (see #$algorithmInputs).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd43).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd43,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd43,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd43,17937).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd43,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd43,[]).
x_comment(x_algorithmProps,"A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to propositions describing the individual steps or operations in which they consist.  <code>(#$algorithmProps ALGORITHM PROP)</code> means that <code>PROP</code> describes a particular step in <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  \n<p>\n<code>PROP</code> can be thought of as having the force of a command within the context of <code>ALGORITHM</code>: it tells an agent or machine that is following <code>ALGORITHM</code> what to do at some particular juncture.  <code>PROP</code> will normally make reference to a (generic) input (which might itself consist of the outputs of one or more previous steps in <code>ALGORITHM</code>) and describe an operation to be performed on the input, the result of which will be the output (if any) of the current step for that input.  If <code>PROP</code> is not the final step in <code>ALGORITHM</code>, this output might serve as an input to a subsequent step.  <code>PROP</code> can be thought of as describing a \"minimal\" subalgorithm (i.e. operation sequence of length 1) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd44).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd44,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd44,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd44,17938).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd44,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd44,[]).
x_comment(x_computerProcessMaxAlgorithm,"A specialization of the predicate #$computerProcessAlgorithms (q.v.) that relates a computer process to the unique\n\"maximal\" #$Algorithm (q.v.) followed via that process.  <code>(#$computerProcessMaxAlgorithm PROCESS ALGORITHM)</code> means that  <code>PROCESS</code> involves carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on one or more inputs and does <i>not</i> involve carrying out any proper superalgorithm (see #$subAlgorithms) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd45).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd45,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd45,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd45,17941).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd45,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd45,[]).
x_comment(x_equivAlgorithms,"A binary predicate that relates #$Algorithms that are procedures for completing the same task(s) with respect to the same inputs --  i.e. algorithms that compute the same function (see #$algorithmComputes).  <code>(#$equivAlgorithms ALG1 ALG2)</code> means that <code>ALG1</code> and <code>ALG2</code> have the same set of allowable inputs and, for any such input, they both produce the same output (or both produce no output).  No further relationship is implied regarding the constituent operations or #$subAlgorithms of <code>ALG1</code> and <code>ALG2</code>; only the initial inputs and final outputs are relevant.  \n<p>\nFor example, two different procedures for alphabetizing a list of names, or two explicit sets of directions for getting to the same destination from a given starting point, constitute equivalent algorithms, regardless of the details of either algorithm's internal structure.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd46).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd46,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd46,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd46,17943).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd46,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd46,[]).
x_comment(x_expressesAlgorithms,"A specialization of the predicate #$containsInformation that relates an #$InformationBearingThing (q.v.) to an #$Algorithm (q.v.) that it expresses or fully describes.  <code>(#$expressesAlgorithms IBT ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>IBT</code> unambiguously describes each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  <code>IBT</code> might be a written or spoken set of directions, an instance of #$ComputerProgramCopy, or something else.  Any agent or machine capable of interpreting <code>IBT</code> should be able, upon doing so, actually to carry out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on any allowable input (assuming that the agent or machine possesses the knowledge or abilities required to perform each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code>).  \n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$expressesMaxAlgorithm.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd47).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd47,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd47,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd47,17946).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd47,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd47,[]).
x_comment(x_expressesMaxAlgorithm,"A specialization of the predicate #$expressesAlgorithms that relates an #$InformationBearingThing (q.v.) to\na \"maximal\" algorithm that it expresses or fully describes. <code>(#$expressesMaxAlgorithm IBT ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>IBT</code> unambiguously describes each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code> and does not express (see #$expressesAlgorithms) any proper superalgorithm (see #$subAlgorithms) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  <code>IBT</code> might be a written or spoken set of directions, an instance of #$ComputerProgramCopy, or something else.  \n<p>\nAny agent or machine capable of interpreting <code>IBT</code> should be able, upon doing so, actually to carry out <code>ALGORITHM</code> on any allowable input (assuming that the agent or machine possesses the knowledge or abilities required to perform each operation in <code>ALGORITHM</code>).  \n<p>\nNote that, in general, an IBT might express more than one maximal algorithm (e.g. a page might have instructions for two separate algorithms printed on it).  A computer program copy, however, expresses <i>exactly one</i> maximal algorithm: the maximal algorithm used by the code for the copy.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd48).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd48,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd48,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd48,17949).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd48,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd48,[]).
x_comment(x_genlAlgorithms,"A specialization of the predicate #$equivAlgorithms that relates #$Algorithms (q.v.) to equivalent ones that are structurally similar to them except for possibly being less detailed.  <code>(#$genlAlgorithms SPEC-ALG GENL-ALG)</code> means that <code>SPEC-ALG</code> and <code>GENL-ALG</code> are functionally equivalent algorithms (see #$equivAlgorithms), and that <code>SPEC-ALG</code> is at least as detailed a sequence of operations as <code>GENL-ALG</code>.  \n<p>\nMore precisely, for each successive operation <code>OP</code> in <code>GENL-ALG</code>, <code>SPEC-ALG</code> contains (in the same relative position) either <code>OP</code> itself or a series of operations <code>OP<sub>1</sub></code>, ..., <code>OP<sub>N</sub></code> such that, whenever <code>OP</code> produces output <code>OUT</code> from input <code>IN</code>, then the relative product of <code>OP<sub>1</sub></code>, ..., <code>OP<sub>N</sub></code> for <code>IN</code> is <code>OUT</code>.  \n<p>\nFor example, <code>GENL</code> might contain a step that simply says \"Sort list <code>L</code> alphabetically\", whereas the analogous segment of <code>SPEC-ALG</code> delineates the series of steps involved in doing an alphabetical bubble-sort on <code>L</code>.  A third algorithm, which was just like <code>SPEC-ALG</code> except for specifying another type of alphabetizing procedure, would constitute another specialization of <code>GENL</code>.  Arguably, corresponding executable code, source code, and pseudocode versions of a given instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW often use maximal algorithms that are strictly speaking distinct, but ordered by #$genlAlgorithms.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd49).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd49,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd49,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd49,17955).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd49,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd49,[]).
x_comment(x_instructionsAlgorithms,"A specialization of the predicate  #$containsProposition-CW that relates a instance of #$Instructions to an #$Algorithm (q.v.) that is part of its information content. <code>(#$instructionsAlgorithms INSTR ALGORITHM)</code> means that following <code>INSTR</code> (see #$instructionsFollowed) requires carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> (see #$algorithmUsed).  \n<p>\nSee also the specialization #$instructionsMaxAlgorithm.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd4a).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd4a,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd4a,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd4a,17960).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd4a,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd4a,[]).
x_comment(x_instructionsMaxAlgorithm,"A specialization of the predicate #$instructionsAlgorithms (q.v.) that relates an instance of #$Instructions to the \"maximal\" #$Algorithm that it involves.  <code>(#$instructionsMaxAlgorithm INSTR ALGORITHM)</code> means that following <code>INSTR</code> requires carrying out <code>ALGORITHM</code> and does <i>not</i> require carrying out any proper superalgorithm (see #$subAlgorithms) of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd4b).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd4b,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd4b,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd4b,17963).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd4b,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd4b,[]).
x_comment(x_programAlgorithms,"A binary predicate that relates code for a computer program to an #$Algorithm (q.v.) used by that code.  <code>(#$programAlgorithms CODE ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>CODE</code> uses <code>ALGORITHM</code>: the event of running a copy of <code>CODE</code> on a computer system involves the carrying out of <code>ALGORITHM</code> on one or more inputs (see #$computerProcessAlgorithms).  <code>ALGORITHM</code> might correspond to part or all of <code>CODE</code>; in the former case <code>ALGORITHM</code> will be a proper #$subAlgorithms of the \"maximal\" algorithm (see #$programMaxAlgorithm) used by <code>CODE</code>.  \n<p>\nDifferent computer code, whether written in the same or in different languages, can use the same algorithm; and if the codes differ only in ways that are irrelevent to the (entire) sequence of operations that they represent, they will use all of the same algorithms.  Note, however, that two <i>versions</i> of code for the same instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW will not necessarily use all (or even many) of the same algorithms; and the algorithms used by one of these versions might not all even be functionally equivalent (see #$equivAlgorithms) to their counterparts used by the other version, if (say) some of the program's functionality changes from version to version (e.g. <i>WordPerfect</i> 5.1 versus <i>WordPerfect</i> 6.0).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd4c).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd4c,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd4c,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd4c,17964).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd4c,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd4c,[]).
x_comment(x_programMaxAlgorithm,"A specialization of the predicate #$programAlgorithms that relates code for a computer program to the \"maximal\" algorithm used by that code.  <code>(#$programMaxAlgorithm CODE ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>CODE</code> uses <code>ALGORITHM</code> (see #$programAlgorithms) and uses no proper superalgorithm of <code>ALGORITHM</code>.  <code>ALGORITHM</code> corresponds to all of <code>CODE</code>, and any #$subAlgorithms of <code>ALGORITHM</code> will be  #$programAlgorithms of <code>CODE</code>.  \n<p>\nDifferent computer code, whether written in the same or in different languages, can use the same maximal algorithm; this will be the case if the codes differ only in ways that are irrelevent to the (entire) sequence of operations that they represent.  Note, however, that two <i>versions</i> of code for the same instance of #$ComputerProgram-CW will not necessarily use the same maximal algorithm; such codes' respective maximal algorithms might not even be functionally equivalent (see #$equivAlgorithms), if (say) some of the program's functionality changes from version to version (e.g. <i>WordPerfect</i> 5.1 versus <i>WordPerfect</i> 6.0).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd4d).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd4d,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd4d,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd4d,17967).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd4d,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd4d,[]).
x_comment(x_subAlgorithms,"A specialization of the predicate #$subInformation that holds between #$Algorithms (q.v.).  <code>(#$subAlgorithms SUPER-ALG SUB-ALG)</code> means that <code>SUPER-ALG</code> contains <code>SUB-ALG</code> as a part.  That is, the sequence of operations that constitutes <code>SUB-ALG</code> is a subsequence of that which constitutes <code>SUPER-ALG</code>.  <code>SUB-ALG</code> is essential to <code>SUPER-ALG</code> in that, if the former were replaced in the latter with a distinct algorithm, the result would be distinct from <code>SUPER-ALG</code>.  \n<p>\nNote that a given algorithm might be a subalgorithm of various different algorithms, even when the latter are not related to one another by the subalgorithm relation.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd4e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd4e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd4e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd4e,17970).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd4e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd4e,[]).
x_comment(x_UsingAnAlgorithm,"A specialization of #$Action whose instances are events in which one or more intelligent agents (possibly software agents) accurately, completely, and \"purposely\" follow the steps of an #$Algorithm (q.v.) with respect to some input(s) (see #$algorithmInputs).  \n<p>\nIn using an algorithm, one is of course thereby also doing whatever it is that the algorithm is an algorithm <i>for</i>.  For example, in following an algorithm for long division one is in fact dividing one number by another.  Instances of #$UsingAnAlgorithm are by definition \"successful\" in that the algorithm in question must not be deviated from or incompletely followed.  \n<p>\nAn event in which a computer follows an algorithm (see #$computerProcessAlgorithms) might be an instance of #$UsingAnAlgorithm, but only if that event is an agentive computer process associated with some pre-existing instance of #$SoftwareAgent (see #$softwareAgentProcesses).  \n<p>\nFor actions in which an agent, rather than carrying out an algorithm directly, uses a device that embodies an algorithm, see #$Computing.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd4f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd4f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd4f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd4f,17973).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd4f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd4f,[]).
x_comment(x_FollowingInstructions,"A specialization of #$Action whose instances are events in which one or more intelligent agents (possibly software agents)   accurately, completely, and \"purposely\" follow some instance of    #$Instructions (q.v.).  In following instructions, one is of course thereby also doing whatever it is that the instructions are instructions <i>for</i>.  For example, in following a certain set of instructions for baking an apple pie one is in fact baking an apple pie.  \n<p>\nInstances of #$FollowingInstructions are by    definition \"successful\" in that the instructions in question must not be deviated from or incompletely followed.  If the instructions being followed involve one or more instances of #$Algorithm (see #$instructionsAlgorithms), then the instance of #$FollowingInstructions will contain that many instances of    #$UsingAnAlgorithm (q.v.) as subevents.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd50).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd50,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd50,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd50,17980).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd50,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd50,[]).
x_comment(x_algorithmUsed,"A specialization of the predicate #$informationUsed that relates an act in which an agent (possibly a software agent) uses an #$Algorithm (q.v.) to the algorithm s/he/it is following.  <code>(#$algorithmUsed USING ALGORITHM)</code> means that <code>USING</code> involves some agent \"purposefully\" and accurately using <code>ALGORITHM</code> with respect to some input.\n<p>\nSee also #$instructionsFollowed.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd51).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd51,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd51,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd51,17983).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd51,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd51,[]).
x_comment(x_instructionsFollowed,"A specialization of the predicate #$informationUsed that relates acts in which an agent follows a set of instructions to the instance of #$Instructions that agent follows. <code>(#$instructionsFollowed FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS)</code> means that <code>FOLLOWING</code> involves some agent purposefully, accurately, and completely following <code>INSTRUCTIONS</code>.\n<p>\nSee also #$algorithmUsed.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd52).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd52,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd52,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd52,17986).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd52,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd52,[]).
x_comment(x_compiledVersionOfCode,"A ternary predicate that relates source code for a computer program, an executable machine code translation of it, and the compiler that translates the first into the second.  <code>(#$compiledVersionOfCode SOURCE EXEC COMPILER)</code> means that <code>EXEC</code> is the output of <code>COMPILER</code> for the input <code>SOURCE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd53).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd53,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd53,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd53,17989).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd53,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd53,[]).
x_comment(x_Algorithm,"A specialization of #$MathematicalOrComputationalThing and #$AspatialInformationStore (qq.v.).  An instance of #$Algorithm is a particular finite sequence of operations that constitutes an effective, or strictly mechanical, procedure for completing some task, computing some function, or simply performing a number of unrelated operations.  \n<p>\nInstances of #$ComputerProgram-CWIS use algorithms (see #$programAlgorithms), and some instances of #$Instructions (e.g. some instances of its specializations #$Recipe-CW and #$DirectionsToGetSomewhere), if sufficiently explicit, involve algorithms.  \n<p>\nAn algorithm can be viewed as a specific way of computing a particular instance of #$Function-Denotational, i.e. a way determining the value of the function for any argument in the function's domain (see #$algorithmComputes).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd54).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd54,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd54,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd54,17990).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abd54,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abd54,[]).
x_comment(x_expertRegardingDeviceType,"An assertion of the form (#$expertRegardingDeviceType AGENT DEVICE-TYPE) means that the #$Person AGENT has extensive knowledge about instances of the sub-collection of #$SolidTangibleArtifact DEVICE-TYPE.  This predicate does not specify the nature of that knowledge, but it could include both the creation, use, and maintenance and repair of instances of this collection.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abd55).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abd55,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abd55,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abd55,17995).
