x_comment(x_subjectivelyEvaluates,"This predicate can be used to assert that an agent has made a certain essentially subjective assessment or value judgment about a particular individual.  (#$subjectivelyEvaluates AGENT INDIV EVAL) means that the AGENT projects upon INDIV the #$EvaluativeQuantity (q.v.) EVAL.  As the evaluation in question is essentially subjective, the above formula of course does _not_ entail either (i) that INDIV actually has the feature it is subjectively thought to have (or some \"objective\" counterpart thereof) or (ii) that any other given agent would also attribute EVAL to INDIV.  Indeed, outside of contexts explicitly defined so as to represent some particular agent's point of view (e.g. some sentient agent's inner mental world or the world-view espoused by a particular religion), it arguably makes no sense to attribute an #$EvaluativeQuantity to an individual directly. \n<p>\nFor example, (#$subjectivelyEvaluates Sally SallyLearningToRideABike #$Easy) says that Sally herself finds/found learning to ride a bike easy; and this might be true regardless of any \"objective\" ranking of the relative level of difficulty involved in learning to ride a bike, and regardless even of any third-person observer's judgment as to how hard or easy a time Sally had learning to ride.  (Of course, assuming Sally is at least minimally self-aware and the observer similarly perceptive, we would expect their respective judgments on this point likely to match fairly well.)  \n<p>\nNote that AGENT's basis for the assessment might be largely emotional, logical-inferential (e.g. having a plausible argument), empirical, or some combination of these.  Note also that, while there is an almost trivially sense in which _any_ judgment or attribution made by a sentient agent has a subjective (i.e. conscious, inner) aspect or manifestation, #$subjectivelyEvaluates pertains specifically to #$EvaluativeQuantity, and thus to what are normally considered value judgments (broadly construed).  Hence the use of the expressions \"essentially subjective\" and \"projects upon\" in the foregoing to describe the sort of attributions at issue.  \n<p>\nFor a similar predicate that relates an agent to a state of affairs onto which s/he projects an evaluative attribute, see #$evaluates-Propositional.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abda5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abda5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abda5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
