x_comment(x_Observing,"A collection of information-gathering events in which an agent or group of agents acquire information by observing some thing or event.  The observation may be mediated by equipment, such a binoculars, video cameras, body wires, an intercom, and other aids to real-time observation, and direct sensory perception on the part of a #$PerceptualAgent-Embodied is not required, although in cases of group observation, we require that there be at least one member of the group playing the role of #$recipientOfInfo in the #$Observing. Positive examples: a military unit's following air traffic by means of radar or a submarine crew's tracking the progress of another submarine by means of sonar.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abde5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abde5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abde5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abde5,18241).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abde5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abde5,[]).
x_comment(x_minQuantValue,"A specialization of #$scalarBounds (q.v.) that relates a given #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) to the minimum #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.) it subsumes (see #$quantitySubsumes). <code>(#$minQuantValue SCALAR POINT)</code> means that the lower limit of <code>SCALAR</code> is <code>POINT</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, if all mailroom employees earned from five to eight dollars per hour, <code>(#$minQuantValue \"MailroomPayRange\" (#$DollarsPerHour 5))</code> would hold.  Other examples:\n<pre>\n  (#$minQuantValue (#$Mile 15 20) (#$Mile 15))\n\n  (#$minQuantValue (#$Unity 6 12) 6).\n</pre>\n<p>\nSee also #$maxQuantValue and #$pointQuantValue.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abde6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abde6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abde6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abde6,18242).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abde6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abde6,[]).
x_comment(x_maxQuantValue,"A specialization of #$scalarBounds (q.v.) that relates a given #$ScalarInterval (q.v.) to the maximum #$ScalarPointValue (q.v.) it subsumes (see #$quantitySubsumes). <code>(#$maxQuantValue SCALAR POINT)</code> means that the upper limit of <code>SCALAR</code> is <code>POINT</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, if all mailroom employees earned from five to eight dollars per hour, <code>(#$maxQuantValue \"MailroomPayRange\" (#$DollarsPerHour 8))</code> would hold.  Other examples:\n<pre>\n  (#$maxQuantValue (#$Mile 15 20) (#$Mile 20))\n\n  (#$maxQuantValue (#$Unity 6 12) 12).\n</pre>\n<p>\nSee also #$minQuantValue and #$pointQuantValue.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abde7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abde7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abde7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abde7,18252).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abde7,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abde7,[]).
x_comment(x_CharacterStringType,"A #$SecondOrderCollection.  Each instance of #$CharacterStringType is a collection of concrete character-string tokens (regardless of their physical form: written, spoken, or whatever) that all represent the same abstract character-string.  That is, each instance of #$CharacterStringType is the collection of all character-string tokens that #$tokensString some particular character-string.  (Thus #$CharacterStringType might have been called \"<i>TokenTypeByCharacterString</i>\".)  An example is the collection of all tokens of the string 'hello', whether inscribed, sounded, or electromagnetically encoded.  \n<p>\nFor more specialized collections that correspond to particular physical forms, see \n#$CharacterStringMode and its specializations.  Note that although instances of #$CharacterStringType group character-string tokens according what abstract character-strings they represent, character-string types are not to be confused with the abstract character-strings themselves; see #$typeForString.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abde8).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abde8,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abde8,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abde8,18262).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abde8,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abde8,[]).
x_comment(x_GeographicalAgent,"A specialization of both #$MultiIndividualAgent and #$GeographicalThing.  Each instance of #$GeographicalAgent is a group of people and/or organizations cohesive enough to be treated as an agent (see the collection #$Agent-PartiallyTangible, of which #$GeographicalAgent is also a specialization), and which occupies a particular instance of #$GeographicalRegion.  Important specializations of #$GeographicalAgent include #$GeopoliticalEntity, #$University, and #$Neighborhood.  \n<p>\nNote that instances of #$GeographicalAgent are viewed in two significantly different ways with respect to two different types of geography-related microtheories. In a \"physical\" geography microtheory (i.e. #$PhysicalGeographyMt and its submicrotheories), geographical agents are clearly distinguished from the regions they occupy. (#$TerritoryFn GEO-AGENT) is used in these contexts to denote the land mass (an instance of  #$GeographicalRegion) occupied by a given geographical agent GEO-AGENT. In a \"dualist\" geography microtheory (i.e. #$DualistGeopoliticalMt and its submicrotheories), on the other hand, geographical agents are viewed as being <i>both</i> agents <i>and</i> land masses (instances of #$GeographicalRegion). Thus, there is little need for #$TerritoryFn in the latter sort of context. (Despite their somewhat paradoxical flavor, dualist microtheories arguably allow Cyc to mimic commonsense reasoning about geographical agents and regions more closely than do the stricter physical microtheories.) There are also some \"generic\" geography microtheories (e.g. #$WorldGeographyMt and #$UnitedStatesGeographyMt) which are neutral with respect to the physical and dualist views. \n<p>\nAlso see the shared-note #$Dualist-Physical-AndGenericGeographyMtNote.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abde9).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abde9,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abde9,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abde9,18266).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abde9,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abde9,[]).
x_comment(x_subSituationTypes,"A specialization of #$temporallySubsumes-TypeType and #$partTypes (qq.v.).   This predicate is a type-level correlate of #$subSituations (q.v.), and is used to state that situations (see #$Situation) of one given type always have temporal situations of another given type as sub-situations.  <code>(#$subSituationTypes SUPERTYPE SUBTYPE)</code> means that for every instance <code>SUPERINST</code> of <code>SUPERTYPE</code>, there is an instance <code>SUBINST</code> of <code>SUBTYPE</code> such that <code>(#$subSituations SUPERINST SUBINST)</code>. \n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$subSituationTypes UnderstandingBachelorhood UnderstandingManhood)</code> might mean that anyone's being in a situation (i.e. #$MentalState) of understanding the concept of bachelorhood involves being in a situation of understanding the concept of manhood.  \n<p>\nAn important specialization of this predicate is #$subEventTypes.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abdea).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abdea,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abdea,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abdea,18271).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abdea,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abdea,[]).
x_comment(x_orgTypeFacilityType,"This predicate is a type-level version of #$orgFacilities. (#$orgTypeFacilityType ORG-TYPE FAC-TYPE) means that every instance of ORG-TYPE is related to some instance of FAC-TYPE via the #$orgFacilities relation, and that every instance of FAC-TYPE is related to some instance of ORG-TYPE via the #$orgFacilities relation.  This is also the predicate corresponding to #$FacilityTypeOfOrganizationTypeFn (q.v.).") :- x_cid(a65b16517abdeb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abdeb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
