x_comment(x_angleBetween,"This is a very general predicate used to state the angle between two spatial things that intersect. <code>(#$angleBetween THING1 THING2 ANGLE)</code> means that the #$AngularDistance between <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> is <code>ANGLE</code>. There are several points to note about the meaning of this predicate:\n<p>\n1. While it is necessary that <code>THING1</code> and <code>THING2</code> #$spatiallyIntersects in order for there to exist some <code>ANGLE</code> between them, it is not sufficient. Two things may intersect in such a complex fashion that it is impossible to determine a measurable angle between them.\n<p>\n2. The arg3 of this predicate is <i>not</i> functional. In many cases, the measured angle between two intersecting things may form more than one angle. For example, two intersecting straight lines, <code>LINE1</code> and <code>LINE2</code>, form two angles, <code>ANGLE</code> and <code>SUPP-ANGLE</code>, which are supplementary to one another. (see #$supplementaryAngles) In such a case we posit that both <code>(#$angleBetween LINE1 LINE2 ANGLE)</code> and <code>(#$angleBetween LINE1 LINE2 SUPP-ANGLE)</code> are true. In addition, it may be that the measured angle depends on the orientation of the observer, or the context of the measurement. For example, measurement of the vertex angles of polygons often distinguishes between the <i>interior angles</i> and <i>exterior angles</i>. \n<p>\n3. In almost all cases, the best way to use this predicate is to write rules which state sufficient conditions for concluding #$angleBetween for special cases, such as for the angles of intersection between two lines. See the consequent rules on this predicate for examples of such.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abe32).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abe32,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abe32,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abe32,18430).
