x_comment(x_signalsEndOf,"(#$signalsEndOf <b>SIT</b> <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b>) means that\n<b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> serves as a signal to participants in and/or\nobservers of <b>SIT</b> that <b>SIT</b> is ending or has ended.   Such a 'signaling' relation is  determined by convention -- i.e., it holds in virtue of there being some operative convention \nwhich relates instances of one event-type to instances of another.\n (Note that the relevant convention may be unwritten, limited to a small group of agents, temporary, etc.)  Many\nspecializations of #$Event-Organized have such conventional signals\nregarding their completion.  For example, the end of a primary or\nsecondary school #$ClassMeeting is often announced by the ringing of a\nbell, and the end of a #$DramaticPerformance is often marked by the\nlowering of a curtain (a signal to the audience).  Organized sports\nprovides many examples -- e.g., an official blowing his whistle\nsignals the end of live action in an organized\n(#$GameFn #$Football-American).  Note an important distinction:\n<b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> may simply communicate to observers that SIT has\nalready ended, as is (often) the case with a curtain lowering at the\nend of a performance.  Or <b>SIGNAL-EVENT</b> may itself <i>bring\nabout</i> the end of SIT, by convention, as does the whistle-blowing\nin football.  To make the second, stronger, claim, use\n#$signalsEndOf-Performative.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abe5e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abe5e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abe5e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abe5e,18529).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abe5e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abe5e,[]).
