x_comment(x_projectionScale,"A binary predicate that relates a #$SurfaceProjection function to its <i>scale</i>: the number by which the distance between any two things in the source surface (see #$projectionSource) can be multiplied to yield the distance between their respective images in the target surface (see #$projectionTarget).  Clearly, not every projection has a scale; a projection might distort its source in such a way that the relative distances between things are not maintained.  \n<p>\nA #$MapProjection (q.v.) typically has a scale that is significantly less than 1.  Often such a scale will be 1 divided by some positive integer.  For example, a scale of \"1:2500\" entails that a distance of 2500 meters in the source (e.g the Earth's surface) corresponds to 1 meter in the target (a map of the world).  Note that a map might be designated as having a particular scale despite exhibiting some minor relative-distance distortion, if the latter is deemed to be negligable for practical purposes.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abea4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abea4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abea4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abea4,18629).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abea4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abea4,[]).
x_comment(x_EqualAreaProjection,"A specialization of #$SurfaceProjection (q.v.).  Each instance of #$EqualAreaProjection is a surface projection function that preserves the \nthe areas of two-dimensional regions.  That is, if a two-dimensional #$SpaceRegion in the function's domain has a certain #$Area (see #$areaOfObject), then the region's image in the function's range will have the same area.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abea5).
