x_comment(x_hasAsBoundary,"(#$hasAsBoundary REG BOUND) means that BOUND either borders on REG (see #$bordersOn) or part of the boundary of REG is spatially subsumed by BOUND.  It is particularly useful when one wishes to be agnostic about whether BOUND is or is not a sub-region of REG or whether BOUND contains a sub-region which is a sub-region of REG, or whether REG simply borders on (see #$bordersOn) BOUND.  Some examples: (#$hasAsBoundary #$Virginia-State #$PotomacRiver) and (#$hasAsBoundary #$Maryland-State #$PotomacRiver).  Whereas the Potomac merely borders on Virginia, the non D.C. portion of the length of the Potomac is a sub-region of Maryland.  Alternatively, BOUND can be shared by two regions which border on each other.  Thus, since the northern and southern halves of the Red River belong to Oklahoma and Texas respectively, #$hasAsBoundary is an appropriate relationship \nbetween Oklahoma and the Red River as well as between Texas and the Red River.  Note that unlike #$formsBorderBetween, the bounding region need not be thin or small relative to the bounded object(s).  Thus, both England and the United States have the Atlantic Ocean as the second argument in #$hasAsBoundary assertions.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abf39).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abf39,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abf39,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
