x_comment(x_cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree,"<code>(#$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree Q-SPEC DEGREE SET)</code> means that <code>Q-SPEC</code> is an instance of #$CycLQuerySpecification that is, to the degree of relevance <code>DEGREE</code>, pertinent to the instance of information gathering #$InformationGathering), as a consequence of the pertinence of the terms in the #$Set-Mathematical <code>SET</code>.\n<p>\n<code>Q-SPEC</code> may have as an #$InferenceSentenceParameter a fully-bound query sentence, or it may be a query specification which has open variables.  If <code>Q-SPEC</code> has open variables, it may be that some appropriate restriction of those variables will yield a more obviously pertinent query.\n\nFor example, if someone is studying a crime denoted by \"<code>Crime008</code>\", a CycL query specification with this inference sentence parameter will be pertinent:\n<p>\n<pre>\n(#$likelySuspects ?ACT ?AGENT)\n</pre>\n<p>\nFurther, a restriction of the open variables in that CycL query specification will yield a more obviously pertinent sentence:\n<p>\n<pre>\n(#$likelySuspects Crime008 ?AGENT)\n</pre>\n<p>\nIn such a case, it will be true that a CycL query specification with the latter inference sentence parameter will be pertinent to a higher degree of relevance than a CycL query specification with the former inference sentence parameter.\n<p>\nSee also #$cycLQuerySpecificationPertinentToDegree-Numeric.\n<p>\n<b>NOTE</b>: This predicate should only be used in microtheories which specify the appropriate qualifications of assertions made with it: e.g., for whom the query is pertinent, on what task, at what time and place, etc.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abf5e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abf5e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abf5e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abf5e,18913).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abf5e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abf5e,[]).
x_comment(x_cultureOperativeForEvent,"(#$cultureOperativeForEvent <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b>\n<b>CULTURE</b>) means that most or all #$socialParticipants in\n<b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b> expect or accept that the social customs of\n<b>CULTURE</b> will be largely followed during <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b>.\nSome allowances might be made for the ignorance or discomfort of\nparticipants who are not members of <b>CULTURE</b>. For example,\n(#$cultureOperativeForEvent <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b> <b>CULTURE</b>)\ntypically holds when all the participants in <b>SOCIAL-EVENT</b> are\nmembers of <b>CULTURE</b> (though in this case, the\nexpectation of conformance is often not a conscious one).  In the\ncase of events which involve participants from multiple cultures, cross-cultural\nguidelines sometimes dictate that one particular culture is\n'operative.'  E.g., in many instances of #$HostedSocialGathering\ninvolving participants from multiple cultures, it is the culture of\nthe #$hostOfEvent which is largely followed, since he or she \ncontrols much of the structure and form of the events.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abf5f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abf5f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abf5f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
