x_comment(x_ConicProjection,"A specialization of #$CompositeMapProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$ConicProjection is a map projection whose intermediate surface (see #$projectionIntermediate) is a cone or series of cones (see #$ConicalSurface-WithoutBase).  If the cone's axis coincides (cones' axes coincide) with the source #$Spheroid's axis (see #$axisOfSurfaceOfRevolution),  parallels are represented as arcs of circles, and meridians as lines converging toward the nearer pole (see #$polesOfSpheroid).\n<p>\nA conic projection that uses a single cone is a #$SimpleConicProjection, and one that uses a series of cones is a #$PolyconicProjection.  Among the simple conic projections, some are such that the cone is tangent with the spheroid only along a single line, and others have the cone tangent with the spheroid along two lines (see #$SecantConicProjection).  Conic projections distort least near the lines of tangency, and distortion is zero at the lines themselves.  If the cone's and the spheroid's axes coincide, the line(s) of tangency is\ncalled a <i>standard parallel</i>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abff6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abff6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abff6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abff6,19259).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abff6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abff6,[]).
