x_comment(x_ModifiedLambertConformalProjection,"A specialization of #$SecantConicProjection whose instances are similar to ordinary #$LambertConformalProjections, but have been modified so as to be better suited for representing polar regions.  These are also known as <i>Ney's projections</i>.  Typically, when a conic projection is centered at a pole, the parallels are shown as arcs instead of circles, and the pole is shown as as arc instead of a point.  In a #$ModifiedLambertConformalProjection, a parallel near the pole is chosen as its higher standard parallel; then a little \"stretching\" is done to complete the circles of the parallels and to eliminate the arc of the pole\n<p>\nA modified Lambert conformal projection is virtually conformal over its entire extent; but since not strictly a #$ConformalProjection it's not suited for highly accurate work.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abffc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abffc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abffc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517abffc,19270).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517abffc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517abffc,[]).
x_comment(x_PolyconicProjection,"A specialization of #$ConicProjection (q.v.).   Each instance of #$PolyconicProjection is a conic projection that uses a <i>series</i> of tangent cones, consisting of a cone tangent with each parallel (or, more precisely, each of those that is some whole number of degrees above or below the equator).\nSuch projections are free of distortion along any parallel and along the central meridian.  They are used (e.g.) in atlases to show large areas; they are not used in navigation.") :- x_cid(a65b16517abffd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517abffd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517abffd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
