x_comment(x_TranscendentalNumber,"A specialization of #$IrrationalNumber.  A #$RealNumber, <code>x<sub>T</sub></code>, is a #$TranscendentalNumber if and only if for all <code>n>0</code> <code>x<sub>T</sub></code> is not the solution to any polynomial equation of the form\n<pre>\na<sub>n</sub>x<sup>n</sup> + a<sub>n-1</sub>x<sup>n-1</sup> + ... + a<sub>1</sub>x + a<sub>0</sub> = 0\n</pre>\nwhere the <code>a<sub>i</sub></code> are #$Integers. Cf. #$AlgebraicNumber.\n<p>\n#$TranscendentalNumbers are important in the history of mathematics because their investigation provided the first proof that it is impossible using only a straightedge and compass to construct a #$Square of equal area to a given #$Circle. The impossibility of such a construction follows from the proof that <pre>(#$isa #$Pi-Number #$TranscendentalNumber).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac0a5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac0a5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac0a5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
