x_comment('x_testFollowupTestFormula-All',"An expression of the form (#$testFollowupTestFormula-All TEST FORMULA) is used to partially describe a followup test that should be run on the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST to test the properties of the answers returned by TEST.  Specifically, FORMULA is both an #$CycLOpenSentence and a #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This formula will support the creation of a second test that would have the same query formula as the #$CycLQuerySpecification of TEST but with FORMULA conjoined to the relevant query, within the scope of any explicit quantifiers. (In the case of a hypothetical query, it would be conjoined to the consequent of the implication.)  As an example, assume that TEST has the hypothetical query <pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y))\n</pre>\nThe assertion (#$testFollowupTestFormula-All TEST (and (#$isa ?Y #$FemaleHuman)(#$isa ?X #$FemaleHuman))) would generate the followup query formula:<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)\n    (#$and\n      (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y)\n      (#$isa ?X #$FemaleHuman)\n      (#$isa ?Y #$FemaleHuman)))\n</pre>The #$CycLQuerySpecification of the followup test will assume the same parameter settings as the #$CycLQuerySpecification for TEST, most significantly the #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter. The success of TEST requires that <b>all bindings</b> returned by TEST are also returned by the followup query. It is assumed that the introduction of FORMULA does not cause the query formula for the followup query to have a different set of free variables.  See also #$testFollowupTestFormula-Some and #$testFollowupTestFormula-None.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac144).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac144,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac144,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac144,19839).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac144,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac144,[]).
x_comment('x_testFollowupTestFormula-None',"An expression of the form (#$testFollowupTestFormula-None TEST FORMULA) is used to partially describe a followup test that should be run on the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST to test the properties of the answers returned by TEST.  Specifically, FORMULA is both an #$CycLOpenSentence and a #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This formula will support the creation of a second test that would have the same query formula as the #$CycLQuerySpecification of TEST but with FORMULA conjoined to the relevant query, within the scope of any explicit quantifiers. (In the case of a hypothetical query, it would be conjoined to the consequent of the implication.)  As an example, assume that TEST has the hypothetical query <pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y))\n</pre>\nThe assertion (#$testFollowupTestFormula-None TEST (#$isa ?Y #$NonPersonAnimal)) would generate the followup query formula:<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)\n    (#$and\n      (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y)\n      (#$isa ?Y #$NonPersonAnimal)))\n</pre>The #$CycLQuerySpecification of the followup test will assume the same parameter settings as the #$CycLQuerySpecification for TEST, most significantly the #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter. The success of TEST requires that <b>no bindings</b> are returned by the followup test. That is, this test specification can be used to screen the answers to the first query so as to ensure that certain classes of answers are not returned.  It is assumed that the introduction of FORMULA does not cause the query formula for the followup query to have a different set of free variables.  See also #$testFollowupTestFormula-All and #$testFollowupTestFormula-Some.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac145).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac145,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac145,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac145,19852).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac145,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac145,[]).
x_comment('x_testFollowupTestFormula-Some',"An expression of the form (#$testFollowupTestFormula-Some TEST FORMULA) is used to partially describe a followup test that should be run on the #$KBContentTest-FullySpecified TEST to test the properties of the answers returned by TEST.  Specifically, FORMULA is both an #$CycLOpenSentence and a #$CycLSentence-Askable.  This formula will support the creation of a second test that would have the same query formula as the #$CycLQuerySpecification of TEST but with FORMULA conjoined to the relevant query, within the scope of any explicit quantifiers. (In the case of a hypothetical query, it would be conjoined to the consequent of the implication.)  As an example, assume that TEST has the hypothetical query <pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y))\n</pre>\nThe assertion (#$testFollowupTestFormula-Some TEST (#$equals ?Y ?X)) would generate the follow up query formula:<pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?X)\n    (#$and\n      (#$likesAsFriend ?X ?Y)\n      (#$equals ?X ?Y)))\n</pre>The #$CycLQuerySpecification of the followup test will assume the same parameter settings as the #$CycLQuerySpecification for TEST, most significantly the #$InferenceMicrotheoryParameter. The success of TEST requires that the set of bindings returned by the followup query is <b>non-empty.</b>  Furthermore, this predicate assumes that the introduction of FORMULA does not cause the query formula for the followup query to have a different set of free variables.  See also #$testFollowupTestFormula-All and #$testFollowupTestFormula-None.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac146).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac146,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac146,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
