x_comment(x_PredicateUnionFn,"A binary #$PredicateDenotingFunction that takes two predicates to their \"union\" or \"disjunction\": the predicate that is by definition their <i>nearest possible common generalization</i>.  <code>(#$PredicateUnionFn PRED1 PRED2)</code> is the predicate <code>UNIONPRED</code> such that, for any sequence <code>ARGS</code> and context <code>MT</code>, <code>(UNIONPRED . ARGS)</code> holds in <code>MT</code> if and only if either <code>(PRED1 . ARGS)</code> or <code>(PRED2 . ARGS)</code> holds in <code>MT</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$PredicateUnionFn #$biologicalDaughters #$biologicalSons) = #$biologicalOffspring.\n<p>\nIf either <code>PRED1</code> or <code>PRED2</code> is of fixed arity <code>N</code>, then the other must either be of arity <code>N</code> or be variable-arity, and <code>UNIONPRED</code> is itself of arity <code>N</code>; if both <code>PRED1</code> and <code>PRED2</code> are variable-arity, then so is <code>UNIONPRED</code>.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$predicateUnion.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac246).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac246,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac246,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
