x_comment(x_hasNonAspectualQuantity,"The most general of the #$NonAspectualQuantitySlots (q.v.): binary predicates that relate a thing to a #$NonAspectualQuantity (q.v.) that characterizes it.   An object's <i>non-aspectual</i> quantities are those that it has simpliciter, without being qualified to any particular aspect or dimension of the object.  Thus <code>(#$hasNonAspectualQuantity OBJ NONASPECT-QUANT)</code> means that <code>NONASPECT-QUANT</code> is a unqualified property of <code>OBJ</code>.\n<p>\nFor example, <code>(#$hasNonAspectualQuantity HotPotato004 (#$DegreeCelsius 70))</code>  means that HotPotato004 has the temperature of 70 degrees celsius.  (In contrast, a #$Distance such as (#$Inch 5) can only characterize a 3-dimensional object with respect to a particular dimension; e.g. it might be HotPotato004's <i>length</i>.)") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac28e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac28e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
