x_comment(x_logicallyTrueSentence,"A specialization of #$trueSentence and #$necessary that is used to state that a given CycL sentence is logically true (in the intended semantics of CycL) -- i.e. it is equivalent to #$True.  Note that this is not a #$KBDependentRelation: we do not assume that all logical truths are provable in Cyc.  Also, note that #$logicallyTrueSentence is #$notAssertible.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac2a4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac2a4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac2a4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac2a4,20467).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac2a4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac2a4,[]).
x_comment(x_DeReAttitudePredicate,"A specialization of #$CognitiveAttitudePredicate (q.v.).  A de re attitude predicate is a relation that (loosely stated) holds among an agent, a specified thing, and a property, such that the agent bears a certain <i>de re</i> propositional attitude (e.g. de re belief, de re doubt, de re knowledge) regarding that object's having that property.  More precisely, each instance <code>DE-RE-PRED</code> of #$DeReAttitudePredicate is a ternary predicate that takes as arguments an #$IntelligentAgent, a #$CycLPropositionalSentence (that is also an #$CycLSentence-Assertible), and an integer; and <code>(DE-RE-PRED AGENT SENT N)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> has a certain de re attitude to the thing <code>OBJ</code> denoted by the <code>N</code>th-place constituent of <code>SENT</code>, with respect to <code>OBJ</code>'s (the thing itself, regardless of what expression is used to denote it) having the property that the proposition expresssed by <code>SENT</code> attributes to it.  For example,\n<pre>\n (#$believesOfArg #$Meyer (#$isa #$Patches-Cat #$TabbyCat) 2)\n</pre>\nmeans that Meyer believes of Patches that he is a tabby cat.  A defining feature of de re attitude predicates is that they allow substitutions of coreferring terms in the specified (i.e. <code>N</code>th) position within the predicate's own second argument (i.e. <code>SENT</code>).  Thus, if the expression 'GarysPet'  happens also to denote Patches, then the sentence displayed above entails\n<pre>\n  (#$believesOfArg #$Meyer (#$isa GarysPet #$TabbyCat) 2) .\n</pre>\nCf. #$PropositionalAttitudeSlot.  And see the function #$DeReVersionFn, which takes as argument a \"de dicto\" propositional attitude slot (e.g. #$beliefs) and returns the corresponding #$DeReAttitudePredicate (#$believesOfArg).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac2a5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac2a5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac2a5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac2a5,20468).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac2a5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac2a5,[]).
x_comment('x_bordersOn-AgentAgnostic',"A common generalization (see #$genlPreds) of #$bordersOn and #$politiesBorderEachOther, used to state that two objects share a border, without requiring inter-argument consistency with regard to agency or region-hood. <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic GEO-THING1 GEO-THING2)</code> means that the instances <code>GEO-THING1</code> and <code>GEO-THING2</code> of #$GeographicalThing border each other, allowing for a CycL representation of, e.g., \"Canada borders the Atlantic Ocean,\" namely <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic #$Canada #$AtlanticOcean)</code> that is neither unduly complex (e.g., <code>(#$thereExists ?TERRITORY (#$and (#$territoryOf ?TERRITORY #$Canada) (#$bordersOn ?TERRITORY #$AtlanticOcean)))</code>, nor requires reasoning from a special \"dualist\" microtheory that wouldn't otherwise be used for a given application.  #$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic is conservative (see #$conservativeViaArg) via #$territoryOf in both its arguments, allowing one to conclude, e.g., <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Mexico)</code> from <code>(#$bordersOn (#$TerritoryFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica) (#$TerritoryFn #$Mexico))</code>.  It is conservative via (#$InverseBinaryPredicateFn #$territoryOf) in both arguments, as well, allowing the derivation of <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic (#$TerritoryFn #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica) (#$TerritoryFn #$Mexico))</code> from <code>(#$politiesBorderEachOther #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica #$Mexico)</code>.  As a corollary, \"mixed\" derivations, e.g., <code>(#$bordersOn-AgentAgnostic #$UnitedStatesOfAmerica (#$TerritoryFn #$Mexico))</code> are also possible.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac2a6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac2a6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac2a6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac2a6,20477).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac2a6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac2a6,[]).
x_comment(x_borderingNeighbors,"(#$borderingNeighbors SITUATION GEO-THING) means that in the instance SITUATION of #$BorderingSomething, GEO-THING is one of the instances of #$GeographicalThing that borders another instance of that collection.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac2a7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac2a7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
