x_comment(x_contributorToTheory,"<code>(#$contributorToTheory AGENT THEORY)</code> means that <code>AGENT</code> contributed intellectually to the elaboration of <code>THEORY</code>, a scientific theory. <code>AGENT</code> needs to have played an active and significant role in the elaboration of <code>THEORY</code>. In particular supplying means or logistic infrastructure isn't considered a contribution, as for instance funding research or typewriting copies of manuscripts. Similarly, animals or human being used as test subjects for experimental purposes by scientists aren't considered to be contributors in this sense. Positive example: Zermelo, Skolem and Fraenkel contibuted to the elaboration of #$ZermeloFraenkelSetTheory. Note that this theory is named after whom are traditionally considered to be its two most significant contributors. We do not formalize the contingente and subjective notions of 'most' or 'more'\nsignificant contributor. We'll use the same predicate to give to Skolem\ncredit for its contribution to the theory.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac392).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac392,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac392,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac392,20921).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac392,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac392,[]).
x_comment(x_mtSpindleOfTheory,"<code>(#$mtSpindleOfTheory THEORY SPINDLE)</code> means that <code>SPINDLE</code> is the microtheory spindle that contains the laws or axioms (see #$LawOfNature and #$Axiom) of the #$PhilosophicalTheory <code>THEORY</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac393).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac393,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
