x_comment(x_typeLevelVersionInArg,"A quintary #$MetaRelation predicate. <code>(#$typeLevelVersionInArg RELN TYPERELN N M QUANT)</code> means that <code>TYPERELN</code> is a type-level version of <code>RELN</code> with regard to their respective <code>N</code>th and <code>M</code>th argument-places.  This implies that if <code>RELN</code>'s <code>N</code>th argument-place is constrained to take <i>instances</i> of a collection <code>COL</code> (see #$argIsa), then <code>TYPERELN</code>'s <code>M</code>th argument-place is constrained to take <i>specializations</i> of <code>COL</code> (see #$argGenl).  (Usually, <code>N</code> and <code>M</code> will be the same.)  Moreover, where <code>INST</code> and <code>SPEC</code> are respectively an instance and a specialization of <code>COL</code>, and <code>RELN</code> and <code>TYPERELN</code> are both predicates, what the sentence <code>(TYPERELN ... SPEC<sub>M</sub> ...)</code> states is a generalization of what the sentence <code>(RELN ... INST<sub>N</sub> ...)</code> states.  If <code>QUANT</code> is a universal quantifier (i.e. #$forAll), the former sentence says something about <i>all</i> of the instances of some collection -- which, depending on the case, could either be <code>SPEC</code> or the collection of things <code>RELN</code> relates via its <code>N</code>th argument-place to the rest of the given arguments.  If <code>QUANT</code> is an existential quantifier (e.g. #$thereExists), it says something about <i>some</i> (unspecified) instance of one of these collections.  (Typically, <code>TYPERELN</code> will be a #$MacroRelation whose #$expansion involves <code>RELN</code> and <code>QUANT</code>.)\n<p>  \nWhen <code>RELN</code> and <code>TYPERELN</code> are <i>functions</i> rather than predicates, the situation is analogous to the above.\n<p>\nFor example, (#$typeLevelVersionInArg #$groupMembers #$groupMemberType 2 2 #$forAll) holds.  While <code>(#$groupMembers GROUP THING)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is a member of <code>GROUP</code>, <code>(#$groupMemberTypes GROUP TYPE)</code> means that <i>all</i> the members of <code>GROUP</code> are instances of <code>TYPE</code>.  For another example, (#$typeLevelVersionInArg #$instrument-Generic #$instrumentType-Generic 2 2 #$thereExists) holds. <code>(#$instrumentType-Generic EVENT TYPE)</code> means that <i>some</i> instance of <code>TYPE</code> plays the role of #$instrument-Generic in <code>EVENT</code>.\n<p>\nSee also the binary generalization #$typeLevelCognates.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac450).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac450,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac450,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac450,21278).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac450,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac450,[]).
x_comment(x_FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverseFn,"A binary function that takes a collection and a binary predicate to the #$FacetingCollectionType (if any) that facets the collection on the basis of the <i>inverse</i> of the predicate.  (#$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn <b>COL BINPRED</b>) is the faceting-collection-type <code>FACET</code> such that both <code>(#$facets-Generic COL FACET)</code> and <code>(#$facetBasedOnBinaryPredInverse FACET BINPRED)</code> hold.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding predicate #$facetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredInverse.    And see #$FacetOfCollectionBasedOnBinaryPredFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac451).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac451,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
