x_comment(x_objectEdges,"A #$RelativeLocationalPredicate and a specialization of #$properlySpatiallySubsumes-Tangential (q.v.) that relates a (two- or higher-dimensional) spatial object to its edges.  <code>(#$objectEdges OBJ EDGE)</code> means that <code>EDGE</code> is one of the edges of <code>OBJ</code>.  More precisely, <code>EDGE</code> is a (not necessarily straight) \"line\" segment (see #$LinearObject) that defines a boundary of <code>OBJ</code>.  Where two or more edges of <code>OBJ</code> meet (see #$spatiallyIntersects), their point of intersection is a <i>corner</i> of <code>OBJ</code> (see #$CornerOnObject).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac46f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac46f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac46f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac46f,21399).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac46f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac46f,[]).
x_comment(x_collRelation,"A ternary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a predicate to a pair of collections such that each instance of the first (second) collection is related by the predicate to (from) some instance of the second (first) collection.  Thus, <code>(#$collRelation PRED COL1 COL2)</code> is true if and only if both of these are true:\n<pre>\n  (#$relationAllExists PRED COL1 COL2)\n\n  (#$relationExistsAll PRED COL1 COL2)\n</pre>\nFor example,\n<pre>\n  (#$collRelation #$groupMembers #$PlanetarySystem #$Sun)\n</pre>\nmeans that (i) every planetary system includes a sun and (ii) every sun is a member of a planetary system.\n<p>\n#$collRelation is used to represent plural sentences.  For instance, \"The cats slept\" means that for some (non-empty non-singleton) class of cats and some (non-empty) class of sleeping events, each cat in the cat-class was in the #$bodilyDoer relation to some event in the sleeping-class, and each sleeping was in the inverse relation to some cat in the cat-set.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac470).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac470,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac470,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac470,21400).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac470,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac470,[]).
x_comment(x_assertedArgCardinality,"An #$ArgCardinalityPredicate (q.v.) that can be used to put a \"state-of-the-KB\" cardinality constraint on a predicate with respect to a given argument-place.  Unlike with most argument-constraint predicates, a constraint imposed by #$assertedArgCardinality is not intended necessarily to reflect the intrinsic nature of the constrained predicate per se, but to limit the number of sentences built from (the CycL \nterm for) the predicate that can coexist as assertions in the Cyc Knowledge Base.  Thus a cardinality constraint imposed by #$assertedArgCardinality can be (and normally is) stricter than the appropriate analogous constraint imposed by #$argCardinality (q.v.).  \n<p>\n<code>(#$assertedArgCardinality PRED N CARD)</code> means that, at any given time, the KB should contain no more than <code>CARD</code> different assertions of the form <code>(PRED ... ARGN<sub>I</sub> ...)</code> that are all exactly alike except that they each have a different term <code>ARGN<sub>I</sub></code> in their respective <code>N</code>th argument-positions.  \n<p>\nSee also #$argCardinality and #$interArgCardinality.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac471).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac471,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac471,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac471,21413).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac471,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac471,[]).
x_comment(x_admittedAllAndOnlyArgument,"A ternary #$MetaRelation predicate that relates a #$Collection to an integer (representing an argument-place) to a #$Relation such that collection contains all of and only the #$admittedArguments (q.v.) to the specified argument-place of the relation.  <code>(#$admittedAllAndOnlyArgument COL ARGNUM RELN)</code> means that, for any thing <code>THING</code>, <code>THING</code> is an instance of <code>COL</code> if and only if <code>THING</code> satisfies all of the (single-argument) argument-type constraints (see #$ArgIsaPredicate and #$ArgGenlPredicate) for the <code>ARGNUM</code>th argument position of <code>RELN</code>.  \n<p>\nIf a given relation has an argument-place on which there is only one arg-isa constraint, and no arg-genl constraints, the collection used in the arg-isa constraint is related to the given relation by #$admittedAllAndOnlyArgument.  Thus <code>(#$admittedAllAndOnlyArgument #$HomoSapiens 1 #$spouse)</code> holds.  Otherwise, things get a bit more complicated.  For example, the collection of all and only the admitted-arguments for the first argument-position of #$JuvenileFn is (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$ExistingObjectType #$genls #$Animal).\n<p>\nSee also #$admittedAllArgument and #$admittedSomeArgument.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac472).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac472,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac472,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac472,21419).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac472,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac472,[]).
x_comment(x_relationOnly,"A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a given unary predicate to a collection of things that includes everything the predicate holds of.  <code>(#$relationOnly PRED COL)</code> means that, for any thing <code>THING</code>, if <code>(PRED THING)</code> holds, then do does <code>(#$isa THING COL)</code>.  Thus, <code>(#$relationOnly PRED COL)</code> is equivalent to (the universal closure of) the more complex form:\n<pre>\n  (#$implies \n    (PRED <b>?X</b>) \n    (#$isa <b>?X</b> COL)) .\n</pre>\nFor example, <code>(#$relationOnly #$completelyAssertedCollection #$FiniteSetOrCollection)</code> holds because a collection must be of finite size in order for its extent to be completely asserted in the Knowledge Base.  \n<p>\nSee also #$relationAll the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac473).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac473,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac473,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac473,21424).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac473,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac473,[]).
x_comment(x_relationAll,"A binary #$RuleMacroPredicate that relates a given unary predicate to a collection of things that the predicate holds of.  <code>(#$relationAll PRED COL)</code> means that, for every instance <code>INST</code> of <code>COL</code>, <code>(PRED INST)</code> holds.  Thus <code>(#$relationAll PRED COL)</code> is equivalent to -- and so can be used in effect as shorthand for -- (the unversal closure of) the more complex form:\n<pre>\n  (#$implies \n    (#$isa <b>?INST</b> COL) \n    (PRED <b>?INST</b>)) .\n</pre>\nFor example, <code>(#$relationAll #$assertedSentence #$CycLAssertion)</code> means that all #$CycLAssertions are asserted sentences.  \n<p>\nSee also #$relationOnly and the shared #$NoteAboutUseOfRuleMacroPredicates.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac474).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac474,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac474,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac474,21433).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac474,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac474,[]).
x_comment(x_PresentTenseVersionFn,"A function that allows one to define temporally qualifiable versions of role predicates used in Davidsonian-style representations of situations.  <code>(#$PresentTenseVersionFn ROLE)</code> denotes the instance PRED of #$CoexistingObjectsPredicate that is a present tense version of <code>ROLE</code>, an instance of #$BinaryRolePredicate.  This means that both <code>(#$arg2SometimesIsa ROLE TYPE)</code> and <code>(ROLE SITUATION ARG2)</code> are timelessly true just in case there is some time interval <code>T</code> such that at every moment throughout T, <code>(PRED SITUATION ARG2)</code> and <code>(#$isa ARG2 TYPE)</code> holds.  \n<p>\n#$PresentTenseVersionFn thus allows one to compose terse representations of situation-types than Davidsonian-only vocabulary will allow.  For example, to say that only transportation devices act as transporters using Davidsonian vocabulary, one must write a complex rule: <pre>\n  (#$implies\n    (#$and\n      (#$isa ?EVENT (#$TransportViaFn #$TransportationDevice))\n      (#$temporalExtent ?EVENT ?EVENT-EXTENT)\n      (#$indexicalReferent #$QueryMt ?MT)\n      (#$mtMonad ?MT ?MONAD)\n      (#$transporter ?EVENT ?TRANSPORTER)) \n   (#$thereExists ?TIME\n     (#$ist\n       (#$MtSpace ?MONAD\n         (#$MtTimeWithGranularityDimFn ?TIME #$TimePoint))\n       (#$isa ?TRANSPORTER #$TransportationDevice))))</pre>  However, with #$PresentTenseVersionFn, one may simply express this as <pre>\n   (#$relationAllOnly \n     (#$PresentTenseVersionFn #$transporter)\n     (#$TransportViaFn #$TransportationDevice) #$TransportationDevice).</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac475).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac475,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac475,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac475,21442).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac475,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac475,[]).
x_comment(x_Headland,"A specialization of #$Promontory.  Each instance of #$Headland is a high bluff that jutts out into a large body of water.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac476).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac476,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
