x_comment(x_reasonsForAction,"(#$reasonsForAction ACTION PRED AGENT THING) means that ACTION was performed by AGENT, and that a crucial reason for AGENT's\nperforming ACTION is the fact that (PRED AGENT THING), where PRED is an instance of #$CognitivePredicate. For example, if the Saudi Arabian air force were to conduct a  bombing raid (RAID01) against targets in Iran shortly after the occurrence of a terrorist attack (ATTACK01) directed against Saudi Arabia, and believed by the Saudis to have been sponsored by Iran, then it is reasonable to conclude that (#$reasonsForAction RAID01 #$beliefs #$SaudiArabia\n(#$directingAgent ATTACK01 #$Iran)). Similarly, if Joe-Bob jumps (JUMP01) up\nfrom his desk because he sees a spider (SPIDER01) on it, then it is true that\n(#$reasonsForAction JUMP01 #$sees Joe-Bob SPIDER01). When arg2 is an instance of\nPropositionalAttitudeSlot, arg4 will generally be an #$CycLSentence-Assertible. However, as the  example of Joe-Bob and the spider shows, it need not always be an #$CycLSentence-Assertible.\n<p>\nNote: #$reasonsForAction is not a spec-pred of #$inReactionTo, but in\nsome cases a given claim can be expressed using either of these predicates. For\nexample, the sentence above explaining Saudi Arabia's motivation for a\nhypothetical attack on Iran might also be expressed as: (#$inReactionTo RAID01\nATTACK01). However, #$reasonsForAction is a more discriminating predicate, in\nthat it allows one to identify the precise attitude that motivated a given\naction. For this reason, #$reasonsForAction is somewhat less versatile, since one must have more detailed information about the agent's mental state to use this predicate than to use #$inReactionTo; on the other hand, when this information is available, it can be quite useful to make it explicit. Finally, note that in some cases #$reasonsForAction is more versatile than #$inReactionTo for instance, #$inReactionTo does not apply to the example above involving Joe-Bob and the spider. See the comment on #$inReactionTo.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac5e3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac5e3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac5e3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac5e3,22156).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac5e3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac5e3,[]).
x_comment(x_followingValue,"An instance of both #$OrderingPredicate and #$ComparisonPredicate (qq.v.), and a specialization of both #$colinearQuantities and #$lessThan (qq.v.).  #$followingValue is simply the restriction of the very general #$lessThan relation to colinear #$Quantity[ies] (q.v.).  <code>(#$followingValue LO-QUANT HI-QUANT)</code> means that <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> are colinear quantities such that <code>HI-QUANT</code> is a higher or greater value than <code>LO-QUANT</code>.  It follows that there is some (unspecified) linear ordering (see #$TotallyOrderedQuantityType) to which both <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> belong.\n<p>\nNote that the above sentence would typically be asserted only if one or both of <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> are instances of #$GenericQuantity (or the more general #$NonNumericScalarQuantity), and -- as is usually the case -- they are not grounded in numerically-quantifiable units of measure (see #$NoteAboutGivingGenericValueFunctionsNumericValues).  If <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code> <i>do</i> have numerical grounding, a #$followingValue assertion is unnecessary since #$numericallyEquals, #$greaterThanOrEqualTo, and #$greaterThan are automatically computable using arithmetic relations holding between the respective minimums and maximums (see #$minQuantValue and #$maxQuantValue) of <code>LO-QUANT</code> and <code>HI-QUANT</code>.  For example, one might well use #$followingValue to state that (#$HighAmountFn #$Glamour) is more glamorous than (#$LowAmountFn #$Glamour); but it is unnecessary to make a #$followingValue assertion about (#$MilesPerHour 5) and (#$MilesPerHour 25 35), since #$greaterThan is automatically computable in that case.\n<p>\nSee also #$GenericValueFunction and #$followingValueOnScale.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac5e4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac5e4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac5e4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac5e4,22170).
