x_comment(x_TypicalityPredicate,"A #$PredicateType.  Each instance of #$TypicalityPredicate is a #$SetOrCollectionPredicate that expresses a typical (frequent, common, expected, standard, normal, significantly likely) relation between things, i.e. one that usually holds but does not hold universally or by definition.  Such predicates are typically used to state general (i.e. quantified), loose, defeasible or probabilistic principles in a terse way; for example, we may represent the fact that grass is typically green with the #$CycLSentence (#$typicalColorOfType #$Grass-Plant #$GreenColor).  #$TypicalityPredicates do not always have the word \"typical\" in their name, and may in certain \"degenerate\" cases express principles which do in fact hold universally, as in, for example, (#$typicalColorOfType GreenGrass #$GreenColor).") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac61e).
