x_comment(x_kbOrthogonal,"<code>(#$kbOrthogonal COL1 COL2)</code> means that in the current query context, the following are provable:<p>\n\n1. <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$genls COL1 COL2))</code> <br>\n2. <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$genls COL2 COL1))</code> <br> \n3. <code>(#$unknownSentence (#$disjointWith COL1 COL2))</code> <p>\n\nIn other words, in the current query context, it is not provable that either <code>COL1</code> subsumes <code>COL2</code> or that <code>COL2</code> subsumes <code>COL1</code>, and neither is it provable that <code>COL1</code> and <code>COL2</code> are disjoint.<p>\nUnlike other predicates related to orthogonality (such as #$orthogonalSetsOrCollections and #$orthogonalInMt (qq.v.)), #$kbOrthogonal is a #$KBDependentRelation and as such is not assertible.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac698).
