x_comment(x_verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString,"<code>(#$verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString SENTENCE RULE COMPOUND)</code> means that the CycL sentence <code>SENTENCE</code> is a logical sentence whose truth verifies that <code>RULE</code>, a rule for parsing noun compounds (see #$NounCompoundRule) is a good rule for parsing the particular noun compound <code>STRING</code>.  For example, <pre>\n (#$verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString\n   (#$Quote\n     (#$typicallyRelnTo #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation\n      (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$Field-Land #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation\n        (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant))  \n      (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant))) #$PumpkinPatch-NCR \"corn fields\")</pre> means that the truth of the CycL sentence \n<pre>\n (#$typicallyRelnTo #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation\n   (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToFn #$Field-Land #$primaryActivityTypeOfLocation\n     (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant)) \n   (#$SubcollectionOfWithRelationToTypeFn #$MaintainingSomething #$objectTakenCareOf #$CornPlant))</pre> validates the semantics that #$PumpkinPatch-NCR produces for \"corn fields.\"  Thus a necessary condition for the truth of <code>(#$verificationExampleForNCRuleAppliedToString SENTENCE RULE COMPOUND)</code> is the truth of <code>SENTENCE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac786).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac786,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac786,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac786,22883).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac786,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac786,[]).
x_comment(x_resultQuotedIsa,"A binary #$MetaRelation predicate that holds between functions and expression-types. #$resultQuotedIsa indicates that any value returned by a given #$Function-Denotational is a \"quoted instance\" of a given expression-type.   More correctly, <code>(#$resultQuotedIsa FUNC TYPE)</code> means that, for any term-sequence <code>ARGS</code>, if the expression <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code> is an #$admittedNAT (q.v.), then it is an instance of the #$SubLExpressionType <code>TYPE</code>.  That is, the following holds:\n<pre>\n  (#$quotedIsa (FUNC . ARGS) TYPE)\n</pre>\nAnd, equivalently, so does:\n<pre>\n  (#$isa (#$Quote (FUNC . ARGS)) TYPE)\n</pre>\n\nTo contrast this predicate with #$resultIsa: <code>(#$resultIsa FUNC COL)</code> holds just in case each #$defined term of the form <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code> <i>denotes</i> an instance of <code>COL</code>, whereas <code>(#$resultQuotedIsa FUNC COL)</code> holds just in case each admitted (i.e. syntactically and semantically well-formed) term of the form <code>(FUNC . ARGS)</code> <i>is itself</i> an instance of <code>COL</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac787).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ac787,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ac787,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ac787,22895).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517ac787,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517ac787,[]).
x_comment(x_ncRuleExceptionStrings,"<code>(#$ncRuleExceptionStrings RULE COMPOUND)</code> means that parsing the noun compound string <code>COMPOUND</code> using the noun compound rule <code>RULE</code> will result in semantics that are an unlikely or even incorrrect interpretation of <code>COMPOUND</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ac788).
