x_comment(x_defaultEquivSubSitTypes,"This #$SituationTypeRelation expresses a relation that holds between the prescribed substructures of two situations, conditional on one situation being a #$properSubSituations of the other. (#$defaultEquivSubSitTypes SIT-TYPE1 SIT-TYPE2 SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2) means that where SIT1 is an instance of SIT-TYPE1 and SIT2 is an instance of SIT-TYPE2 such that (#$properSubSituations SIT1 SIT2), any instance of SUBSIT-TYPE2 that is a #$properSubSituations of SIT2 will be identical with any instance of SUBSIT-TYPE1 that is an #$properSubSituations of SIT1, barring evidence to the contrary.  This is a very strong condition, even for a default, but there are cases for which it is warranted.  For example, consider #$MurderForHire and #$Murder.  It is true that every #$MurderForHire subsumes a #$Murder as a #$properSubSituations.  It is also reasonable to assume that any #$Murder includes a #$Dying as a #$subEvent.  Other things being equal, the default assumption should be that any #$Dying which is known to occur as a proper subsituation of a #$Murder is identical with any #$Dying that is known to occur as a proper subsituation of a #$MurderForHire of which the #$Murder is a part:  thus, (#$defaultEquivSubSitTypes #$MurderForHire #$Murder #$Dying #$Dying).") :- x_cid(a65b16517acaea).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acaea,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acaea,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acaea,23972).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acaea,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acaea,[]).
x_comment(x_defaultSubsumptiveSubSitTypes,"This #$SituationTypeRelation expresses a relation that holds between the prescribed substructures of two situations, conditional on one situation being a #$properSubSituations of the other.  More specifically, (#$defaultSubsumptiveSubSitTypes SIT-TYPE1 SIT-TYPE2 SUBSIT-TYPE1 SUBSIT-TYPE2) means that where SIT1 is an instance of SIT-TYPE1 and SIT2 is an instance of SIT-TYPE2 such that (#$properSubSituations SIT1 SIT2), any proper subsituation of SIT2 that is an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE2 will be a #$subSituations (not necessarily a #$properSubSituations) of any instance of SIT1 that is an instance of SUBSIT-TYPE1.  For example, suppose that a #$HostileSeizure occurs as part of a #$MilitaryOperation, and that it is generally the case that (#$candidateProperSubSituationTypes #$HostileSeizure (#$AcquiringFn #$Weapon)) and also that (#$candidateProperSubSituationTypes #$MilitaryOperation (#$AcquiringFn #$MilitaryHardware)).  It stands to reason that in a #$MilitaryOperation which includes a #$HostileSeizure as a #$properSubSituations, any (#$AcquiringFn #$Weapon) that occurs as part of the seizure should be regarded as a subsituation of any (#$AcquiringFn #$MilitaryHardware) that occurs as part of the #$MilitaryOperation, provided there is no evidence to the contrary;  e.g. evidence that the hardware-aquisition qua military op doesn't temporally subsume the weapon acquisition qua seizure.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acaeb).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acaeb,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acaeb,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
