x_comment(x_elementOf,"A very general binary predicate that relates a thing to any set or collection (see #$SetOrCollection) that it is a member or element of. <code>(#$elementOf THING SETORCOL)</code> means that <code>THING</code> is an element of <code>SETORCOL</code>.  \n<p>\n#$elementOf is a more general relation than #$isa.  Whereas #$isa is used exclusively to talk about membership in #$Collections,  #$elementOf can also be used to talk about membership in mathematical sets (see #$Set-Mathematical).") :- x_cid(a65b16517acb5e).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acb5e,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acb5e,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acb5e,24144).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acb5e,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acb5e,[]).
x_comment(x_convexHullOf,"A binary predicate that relates a given two- or three-dimensional spatial thing to its intangible \"convex hull\".  If <b>OBJECT</b> is a #$TwoDimensionalThing, then <code>(#$convexHullOf HULL OBJECT)</code> means that <b>HULL</b> is the entire one-dimensional boundary (see #$ClosedLinearObject) of the minimal convex 2-D space region that #$spatiallySubsumes <b>OBJECT</b> (see #$ConvexSpaceRegion and #$Surface).  If <b>OBJECT</b> is a #$ThreeDimensionalThing, then <code>(#$convexHullOf HULL OBJECT)</code> means that <b>HULL</b> is the entire closed two-dimensional surface (see #$ClosedSurfaceRegion) of the minimal convex 3-D space region that subsumes <b>OBJECT</b>.  \n<p>\nNote that the dimensionality of <b>HULL</b> is always one less than that of <b>OBJECT</b>.  Also note that, while <b>OBJECT</b> might or might not be a #$SpatiallyContinuousThing, <b>HULL</b> is spatially-continuous in either case.\n<p>\nSee also the corresponding function #$ConvexHullFn.  To describe the whole convex space region bounded by a given object's convex hull, use #$convexSpaceRegionSubsuming-Minimal.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acb5f).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acb5f,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acb5f,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acb5f,24147).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acb5f,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acb5f,[]).
x_comment('x_SpaceRegion-Empirical',"A specialization of #$SpaceRegion, #$SpatialThing-Localized, and #$IntangibleExistingThing (qq.v.).  Instances of #$SpaceRegion-Empirical are intangible regions of space located in the empirically observable universe.  A space region might or might not be connected (see #$SpatiallyContinuousThing).  It might be partially or completely filled with (occupied by) #$PartiallyTangibles, or it might be completely empty (but cf. #$FreeSpaceRegion).  In any case, the space region itself is not to be confused with a physical object or other spatially localized (non-space-region) thing that might happen to be #$cospatial with it.  A given space region can be characterized fully merely by specifying its location and dimensions.  Thus (although this is not the case with spatial things in general), space regions are identical (#$equals) if and only if they are #$cospatial.  #$SpaceRegion-Empirical is in a way the spatial analogue of #$TimeInterval, whose own instances can be fully characterized by specifying their temporal properties; these two collections can be used, respectively, to talk about space and time as \"dimensions\".  Specializations of #$SpaceRegion-Empirical include #$Point-Empirical, #$LineSegment-Empirical, #$Surface-Empirical, and #$SpaceChunk-Empirical.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acb60).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acb60,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
