x_comment(x_TruthFunction,"A major subcollection of #$Relation that subsumes the collections #$Predicate, #$LogicalConnective, and #$Quantifier (qq.v.). Truth-functions, or rather the expressions that represent or denote them, are used to form sentences.  More precisely, any CycL expression that denotes an instance of #$TruthFunction (and only such an expression) can appear in the \"0th\" argument-position (i.e. as the term immediately following the opening parenthesis) of a #$CycLSentence.  \n<p>\nThe name 'TruthFunction' derives in part from the fact that the sentences thus formed (if semantically well-formed and quantificationally closed; see #$CycLSentence-Assertible and #$CycLClosedSentence) are the kind of expression that can be true or false (with respect to a given context and interpretation). Though not really functions (cf. #$Function-Denotational), many #$TruthFunctions are \"truth-functional\" in the sense that the truth-value of a (closed, semantically well-formed) sentence <code>(RELN ARG1..ARGN)</code> built using a truth-function <code>RELN</code> is uniquely determined (with respect to a given context) by <code>RELN</code> together with the argument(s) <code>ARG1..ARGN</code> to which it is applied.  For example, the sentence <code>(#$mother #$ChelseaClinton #$HillaryClinton)</code> is made true (in the actual world) by the fact that Chelsea Clinton's mother is Hillary Clinton.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba0).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba0,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba0,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba0,24296).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba0,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba0,[]).
x_comment('x_denotes-TypeType',"A binary predicate that relates a type of denoting expression (i.e. a specialization of #$CycLClosedDenotationalTerm) to a collection.  <code>(#$denotes-TypeType EXPR-TYPE COL)</code> means that each instance of <code>EXPR-TYPE</code> denotes (see #$denotes) some instance of <code>COL</code>, and no instance of <code>COL</code> is denoted by a CycL term that is not an instance of <code>EXPR-TYPE</code>.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba1).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba1,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba1,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba1,24299).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba1,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba1,[]).
x_comment(x_totalFunctionOn,"A binary predicate that relates a given function (see #$Function-Denotational) to any #$SetOrCollection that it is a \"total function\" on (cf. #$TotalFunction).  <code>(#$totalFunctionOn FUNC SETORCOL)</code> means that <code>FUNC</code> returns a value for any (sequence of) element(s) of <code>SETORCOL</code> taken as argument(s).  \n<p>\nMore precisely: where the #$arity of <code>FUNC</code> is <code>N</code>, then <code>(#$totalFunctionOn FUNC SETORCOL)</code> is true if and only if for any <code>N</code>-length sequence of elements of <code>SETORCOL</code> (<code>THING<sub>1</sub>, ..., THING<sub>N</sub></code>), there is some <code>VALUE</code> such that this holds:\n<pre>\n  (#$equals VALUE (FUNC THING<sub>1</sub> ... THING<sub>N</sub>)) .\n</pre>\nWhere <code>FUNC</code> is a #$VariableArityFunction it is the same, except that  \nthe above must hold of every sequence of elements of <code>SETORCOL</code> whose length is between the #$arityMin and the #$arityMax of <code>FUNC</code> (inclusive).\n<p>\nTypically, <code>FUNC</code> will be a #$PartialFunction (or at least not known to be a #$TotalFunction) and <code>SETORCOL</code> a proper subset of the set/collection of all #$admittedArguments to <code>FUNC</code>.\n<p> \nFor example, #$QuotientFn is a total function on both #$PositiveInteger and #$NegativeInteger, but not on #$Integer (since dividing by 0 is undefined).") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba2,24300).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba2,[]).
x_comment(x_relationDomain,"A #$MetaRelation predicate that associates each instance of #$Relation with its domain: the set of all sequences of things that the relation relates to something.  For example, the domain of #$likesAsFriend is the set of (length one sequences of) sentient animals who like some sentient \nanimal or other, and the domain of #$DifferenceFn is the set of all pairs of #$ScalarIntervals (since #$DifferenceFn relates each such pair to the result of subtracting its second member from its first member).  <code>(#$relationDomain RELATION DOMAIN)</code> means that the domain of <code>RELATION</code> is <code>DOMAIN</code>.  \n<p>\nMore precisely (and following standard mathematical practice): if the #$relationalArity (q.v.) of <code>RELATION</code> is <code>N</code> (for <code>N</code> > 1), <code>DOMAIN</code> is the set of all (<code>N</code>-1)-length #$Lists consisting of the first N-1 items of some list in the #$relationExtension (q.v.) of <code>RELATION</code>; if <code>RELATION</code> is unary it has no domain, and #$relationDomain is undefined for such a <code>RELATION</code>.  \n<p>\nThough defined for polyadic relations generally, <code>DOMAIN</code> will normally be of particular interest only when <code>RELATION</code> is a function (i.e. an instance of  #$Function-Denotational), in which case <code>RELATION</code> relates each  element of <code>DOMAIN</code> to exactly one thing (which, in turn, is an element  of the #$relationRange of <code>RELATION</code>).  See also #$DomainOfFunctionFn.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba3,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba3,24312).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba3,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba3,[]).
x_comment(x_AbstractThing,"The collection that is by definition the intersection of #$AtemporalThing and #$AspatialThing (qq.v.).  A given thing is an instance of #$AbstractThing just in case it is \"abstract\", in the sense of having neither temporal nor spatial extent or location.  Specializations of #$AbstractThing include #$SetOrCollection, #$Number-General, #$Relation, and #$Proposition (qq.v.).  Note that a set or collection is considered an abstract thing even if its elements are not abstract.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba4).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba4,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba4,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba4,24318).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba4,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba4,[]).
x_comment(x_Microtheory,"A specialization of #$AspatialInformationStore and #$AbstractIndividual (qq.v.).  Each instance of #$Microtheory is an atemporal, aspatial, informational thing that represents a context in Cyc.  Each microtheory (or 'mt') serves to group a set of assertions together that share some common assumptions; the assertions in an mt constitute the content of that mt.  Note that each assertion in the Cyc knowledge base must be explicitly stated to be true in at least one microtheory.  Assertions stated to be true in one mt will also be true  (by inference) in more specialized mts that depend on the content of that mt.  For example, if something is true in the #$HumanSocialLifeMt (q.v.), then it should by default be true in the more specialized #$UnitedStatesSocialLifeMt (q.v.).  Specialized microtheories are related to the more general microtheories on which they depend by the predicate #$genlMt (q.v.).  Note that every query is made in some mt, and the answer one gets to a query depends on the mt in which it is asked, since the only assertions which can be used to answer a query in an mt are those  explicitly stated to be true in that mt, or in some more general mt.  See also the predicate #$ist, which is used to relate an assertion to the microtheories in which it is true.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba5,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba5,24319).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba5,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba5,[]).
x_comment(x_MapFunctionOverSetList,"A macro expanded evaluatable function, #$MapFunctionOverSetList is the composition of #$MapFunctionOverList and #$MapFunctionOverSet. If <code>FUNCTION</code> is a #$UnaryFunction and <code>SET-LIST</code> is a list of sets, i.e. an instance of <code>(#$ListOfTypeFn #$Set-Mathematical)</code>, then <code>(#$MapFunctionOverSetList FUNCTION SET-LIST</code>) applies <code>FUNCTION</code> to each element of each set in <code>LIST-SET</code> and returns a new list of sets. ") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba6).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba6,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba6,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acba6,24320).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acba6,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acba6,[]).
x_comment(x_MapFunctionOverSetOfSets,"A macro expanded evaluatable function, #$MapFunctionOverSetOfSets is the composition of #$MapFunctionOverSet with itself. If <code>FUNCTION</code> is a #$UnaryFunction and <code>SET-OF-SETS</code> is a #$SetOfSets, then <code>(#$MapFunctionOverSetOfSets FUNCTION SET-OF-SETS</code>) applies <code>FUNCTION</code> to each element of each set in <code>SET-OF-SETS</code> and returns a new set of sets.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acba7).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acba7,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acba7,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
