x_comment(x_negationPredsWRTArgs,"<code>(#$negationPredsWRTArgs PRED-1 PRED-2 LIST)</code> means that the two predicates <code>PRED-1</code> and <code>PRED-2</code> stand in a #$negationPreds -like relation to each other with respect to a specified correlation among their argument-places.  The two predicates, though both of fixed arity (see #$FixedArityRelation), might have identical or different #$arity; in the latter case, <code>PRED-1</code>'s #$arity is required to be higher than that of <code>PRED-2</code>.  The argument-place correlation between <code>PRED-1</code> and <code>PRED-2</code> is encoded in <code>LIST</code>, a list of non-negative integers whose length is equal to the arity of <code>PRED-2</code>.  The position of an integer within the list represents that same argument-position within <code>PRED-2</code>, while the size of the integer represents the corresponding argument-position within <code>PRED-1</code>.\n<p>Thus <code>(#$negationPredsWRTArgs PRED-1 PRED-2 LIST)</code> means that whenever <code>PRED-1</code> holds of a given argument sequence <code>SEQUENCE-1</code>, then #$negationPreds holds of the sequence <code>SEQUENCE-2</code>, defined as follows: Where the <code>I</code>th item in <code>LIST</code> is the integer <code>N</code>, the <code>I</code>th item if <code>SEQUENCE-2</code> is the same as the <code>N</code>th item in <code>SEQUENCE-1</code>.  For example,\n<pre>\n  (#$negationPredsWRTArgs #$touchesWith #$spatiallySeparated (#$TheList 3 2))</pre>\nentails\n<pre>\n  (#$not\n    (#$and\n      (#$touchesWith #$MingXu Steak8 Fork25)\n      (#$spatiallySeparated Fork25 Steak8))).</pre>") :- x_cid(a65b16517acc05).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acc05,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acc05,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acc05,24447).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acc05,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acc05,[]).
x_comment(x_companyEarnings,"An instance of #$TemporallyQualifiableRelation, <code>(#$companyEarnings COMPANY AMOUNT)</code><T> means that at the time period T (but not necessarily at any proper sub-interval of T), the earnings of the company <code>COMPANY</code> are <code>AMOUNT</code>, an instance of #$MonetaryValue.  The earnings of a company are arrived at by subtracting certain expenses and taxes from revenues.  As such, #$companyEarnings is #$netIncome as applied to instances of #$Business.  This sense of \"earnings\" can be contrasted to the related notion of \"earnings\" as applied to individual people, which refers to the money (typically, the gross) earned through employment or investments.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acc06).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acc06,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
