x_comment(x_vulnerableIn,"<code>(#$vulnerableIn SITUATION OBJECT RESULT-TYPE)</code> means that when in situation <code>SITUATION</code>, existing object <code>OBJECT</code> is vulnerable to hardships of type <code>RESULT-TYPE</code>.  The purpose of #$vulnerableIn is to represent vulnerabilities that stem from being in a particular situation, and presumably from playing some sort of role in that situation.  It is the situation taken as a whole, including the important background conditions, that pose the vulnerability, not some object exclusively.  Given this sense of vulnerability, and so as to distinguish this predicate from #$vulnerableTo and the like, we characterize <code>RESULT-TYPE</code> from <code>OBJECT</code>'s perspective.  <code>RESULT-TYPE</code> should therefore be a 'passive' term.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acddd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acddd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acddd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acddd,25109).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acddd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acddd,[]).
x_comment(x_energySourceForEvent,"This predicate is used to identify a particular source of energy used in a particular event.  <code>(#$energySourceForEvent EVENT ENERGYSOURCE)</code> means that <code>ENERGYSOURCE</code> provides (some of) the energy used in <code>EVENT</code>. <code>ENERGYSOURCE</code> might be a battery, the sun, a person, etc.  See also #$providesPowerFor.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acdde).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acdde,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acdde,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acdde,25110).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acdde,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acdde,[]).
x_comment(x_intendedPrimaryFunction,"A specialization of  both #$primaryFunction and #$intendedBehaviorCapable (qq.v.) that is used to indicate the primary or typical use a given  artifact (see #$Artifact-Generic) was designed to serve.  <code>(#$intendedPrimaryFunction ARTIFACT SITTYPE ROLE)</code> means that:\n<p>\n(i) the primary function of <code>ARTIFACT</code> is to play <code>ROLE</code> in situations of  the type <code>SITTYPE</code> and <br>\n(ii) <code>ARTIFACT</code> was intended by its designer  primarily to play <code>ROLE</code> in <code>SITTYPE</code>s.\n<p>\nFor example, the  intended primary function of a #$MammographyProvider is to  provide service (see #$providerOfService) in  #$MammographicBreastCancerScreenings; and the intended primary function of a #$Pump-Mechanical is to cause things to move  (see #$providerOfMotiveForce) in #$ConveyingMaterials events. Most artifacts have just one intended primary function, but  some have more than one.  For example, a clock-radio has two intended primary functions: displaying the current time of day  and converting transmitted radio signals into audible sounds.   Note that in #$SimpleDeviceFunctioningMt, however, it is  assumed that every #$PhysicalDevice has only one intended  primary function.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acddf).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acddf,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acddf,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acddf,25111).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acddf,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acddf,[]).
