x_comment(x_numberOfInstancesOfCollRelatedToInstancesOfType,"(#$numberOfInstancesOfCollRelatedToInstancesOfType TYPE INSTANCE TYPE2 NUM BIN-PRED) holds of every NUM such that NUM is the number of instances of TYPE2 that have BIN-PRED to INSTANCE where INSTANCE is an instance of TYPE.  For example, the relevant property for #$Country is #$eventOccursAt while the relevant property for #$TerroristGroup-Islamist is #$performedBy.  If #$Country replaced TYPE and the query were run with some given specialization of #$Event for TYPE2 with INSTANCE and NUM open, it would return pairs of Countries and numbers such that the number is the number of instances of EVENT-TYPE that occurred in that country.  Similarly, a query with #$TerroristGroup-Islamist replacing THING will net you pairs of terrorist groups that are instances of #$TerroristGroup-Islamist and the number of attacks they have performed.  Note that this predicate will only be well-defined for those types (and their specializations) that we have specifically supported.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acdfd).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acdfd,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acdfd,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acdfd,25160).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acdfd,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acdfd,[]).
x_comment(x_numberOfInstancesOfCollRelatedToSpecsOfType,"(#$numberOfInstancesOfCollRelatedToSpecsOfType TYPE INSTANCE TYPE2 NUM BIN-PRED) holds of every NUM such that NUM is the number of instances of TYPE2 that have BIN-PRED to INSTANCE where INSTANCE is a specialization of TYPE.  For example, if BIN-PRED is #$intendedAttackTargets and TYPE is #$GovernmentalBuilding, the running the query with a specified subcollection of #$Event fro TYPE2 will return pairs consisting of a specialization of #$GovernmentalBuilding and the number of instances of the event type that targeted that type of building.  Note that although arg3 takes any sort of collection, It is mostly designed for use with collections of events") :- x_cid(a65b16517acdfe).
