x_comment('x_Translation-RandomTrajectory',"The collection of all instances of single-trajectory translations (#$Translation-SingleTrajectory) in which the associated #$Trajectory is what physicists and engineers sometimes refer to as a 'random walk':  i.e., where the vector of the trajectory (the direction and speed of motion, with direction defined as the directed slope tangential to the graph at a given point) is essentially unpredictable, notwithstanding the availablity of information about the vector at previous points along the trajectory.  Usually, a #$Translation-RandomTrajectory comes about because the motion in question is the statistical summation of forces deriving from the microscopic state of the medium in which the #$primaryObjectMoving is fully or partly immersed.Examples include the Brownian motion of a particle suspended in water or some other fluid, or the path of a photon making its way from the core of the sun to the photosphere (constantly being absorbed and re-emitted on a random vector by the surrounding plasma:  takes about 100 years after its creation by nuclear fusion in the core to escape).") :- x_cid(a65b16517acefc).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acefc,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acefc,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acefc,25483).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acefc,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acefc,[]).
x_comment(x_Drifting,"The collection of all random path translations in which the #$primaryObjectMoving is suspended in a fluid, i.e. a liquid or a gas.  Examples include the movement of a toy baloon on the breeze (but not necessarily the movement of a manned hot-air balloon or a dirigible!) or the movement of the Mary Celeste at the time she was discovered.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acefd).
