x_comment(x_termPhrases,"(#$termPhrases TERM CONSTRAINT STRING) means that STRING is a word or\nphrase that denotes TERM and that meets CONSTRAINT. CONSTRAINT is an\ninstance of #$TermPhrasesConstraint (q.v.).  For example, CONSTRAINT\nmay be an instance of #$SpeechPartPredicate (such as #$singular or\n#$gerund); an instance of #$SpeechPart (such as #$Noun or\n#$Verb); or an instance of #$StringIndexingSlot (such as\n#$nameString).  If CONSTRAINT is a predicate, assertions on its\nspec-preds will also be searched: thus, the query (#$termPhrases TERM\n#$nameString ?STRING) will also return strings asserted of TERM using\n#$preferredNameString.  Similarly, if CONSTRAINT is a collection,\nassertions related to its specializations will be searched: thus, the\nquery (#$termPhrases TERM #$ProperNoun ?STRING) will return\n#$ProperCountNouns and #$ProperMassNouns that refer to TERM.  If\nCONSTRAINT is #$ProperNameString, assertions on predicates whose\narg2Isa #$ProperNameString will be searched.  If CONSTRAINT is\n#$CharacterString, no constraints will be imposed, and all lexifications\nof TERM are returned.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acf77).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acf77,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acf77,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
