x_comment(x_normativeSpecificationDescribesRelnToIns,"The predicate #$normativeSpecificationDescribesRelnToIns is used for partially specifying the satisfaction conditions for an instance of #$NormativeSpecification  Specifically, it associates an instance of #$NormativeSpecification <code>SPEC</code> with a #$BinaryPredicate <code>RELN</code> and an instance of #$Thing <code>THING</code>, to state the part of satisfying SPEC involves a situation <code>SIT</code> such that <code>(RELN SIT THING)</code>.  This predicate works in conjunction with #$normativeSpecificationDescribesSituationType, which specifies the #$isa constraints for <code>SIT</code>; indeed <code>(#$normativeSpecificationDescribesSituationType SPEC TYPE)</code> is equivalent to and shorthand for <code>(#$normativeSpecificationDescribesRelnToIns SPEC #$isa TYPE)</code>.  See #$completeSpecification for more information on how to characterize the satisfaction conditions for a normative specification.  See also #$normativeSpecificationDescribesRoleForInd, which is a more specific version of #$normativeSpecificationDescribesRelnToIns, dealing with instances of #$BinaryRolePredicate and #$SomethingExisting.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acff2).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acff2,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517acff2,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517acff2,25745).
xc_kb_names(a65b16517acff2,[]).
xc_mapping_rows(a65b16517acff2,[]).
x_comment(x_affectsNegatively,"The most general predicate used to relate a temporal thing to other temporal things that affect it in a way that is contrary to that thing's interests, well-being, or continued existence. <code>(#$affectsNegatively THING-1 THING-2)</code> means that the instance <code>THING-1</code> of #$TemporalThing harms, degrades, thwarts, disturbs, decreases the functionality of, or is otherwise bad for <code>THING-2</code>.   When possible, a more specific predicate (e.g., #$maleficiary, #$objectHarmed, #$negativeVestedInterest) should be used.") :- x_cid(a65b16517acff3).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517acff3,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
