x_comment(x_interArgDependentCardinality,"An instance of both #$ArgCardinalityPredicate and #$InterArgConstraintPredicate (qq.v.) that can be used to put a conditional argument-cardinality constraint on a specified argument-place of a predicate, dependent upon the type of thing occupying that or another specified argument-place.\n<p>\n(#$interArgDependentCardinality PRED M COL1 N COL2 CARD) means that when PRED's Mth argument is an instance of COL2, INS1, then, however the rest of the arguments are fixed, there is at most CARD things, INS2, that are instances of COL3 that stand in PRED to INS1.  For example, a relation like currentlyFieldedPlayerForTeam that relates a sports team in arg1 to a player in arg2 that is playing in a game the team is involved in the current context might have the following cardinality restriction\n<p>\n<code>(#$interArgDependentCardinality currentlyFieldedPlayerForTeam 1 BaseballTeam 2 Outfielder 3) </code><p>\nThis would entail that in a given context there are at most 3 outfielders playing in a game for a given team.  The most useful assertions of this type are when CARD=1.  In cases like those, simplification modules can utilize this information to unify distinct variables that may have arisen from independent transformations.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ad2f5).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ad2f5,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
