x_comment(x_latitude,"<code>(#$latitude THING ANGULAR-DISTANCE)</code> means that the #$SpatialThing <code>THING</code> is located at the north latitude <code>ANGULAR-DISTANCE</code>, in the sense that either the whole of <code>THING</code> lies at this latitude or <code>THING</code> has a proper part which lies at this latitude.  Thus if <code>THING</code> is, for instance, a large geographical region, there can be more than one true assertion of the form <code>(#$latitude THING X)</code>.  Some examples of assertions about latitude are:  \n<p>\n(#$latitude #$EarthsEquator (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 0)),<br> \n(#$latitude #$AxialNorthPoleOfEarth (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure 90)), and <br>\n(#$latitude #$AntarcticCircle (#$Degree-UnitOfAngularMeasure -66.5)).  \n<p>\nAs the last example indicates, latitudes of things in the southern hemisphere are expressed using negative real numbers.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ad372).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ad372,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ad372,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
xc_source_line(a65b16517ad372,26776).
