x_comment('x_MarburyVMadison-CourtCase',"A landmark case heard by the U.S. Supreme Court which established the basis for the exercise of judicial review in the United States.  #$WilliamMarbury had received a judicial appointment under President #$JohnAdams which #$JohnMarshall, who was then acting as Secretary of State, failed to deliver before Adams' term expired in 1801 (Marbury's appointment, along with several others, having being made literally at the eleventh hour before Adams' term expired).  #$ThomasJefferson, when sworn in as Adams' successor, continued to withold the appointments, and Marbury petitioned the #$UnitedStatesSupremeCourt to compel #$JamesMadison, Jefferson's Secretary of State, to deliver them.  The court, under the leadership of the same #$JohnMarshall, eventually found that Marbury's petition was misdirected in being directly addressed to the #$UnitedStatesSupremeCourt, inasmuch as the #$USConstitution only gave the court appelate jurisdiction, not original jurisdication, in such cases: hence, the judicial statue under which Marbury's petition was submitted was unconstitutional. This marked the first time that the #$UnitedStatesSupremeCourt asserted an authority, defended by Marshall in his opinion, to evaluate existing criminal and civil statutes with regard to constitutionality, i.e., the #$USJudicialRightToDetermineConstitutionalityOfLaw.") :- x_cid(a65b16517ad4ca).
xc_microtheory(a65b16517ad4ca,x_UniversalVocabularyMt).
xc_source_file(a65b16517ad4ca,'c:/snet/petta/repos/openworld_dr/KBs/fire/flat-files/nextkb/UniversalVocabularyMt.part06.krf').
